	[==[{"module":"camera","system_module":"camera","version":"1.0","rubric_version":"1.0","overview":"A great Roblox camera is ONE prioritized per-frame authority that OWNS the frame and composes every effect as summed offset layers over a single base pose. The build order is load-bearing: first take render-step ownership (CameraType.Scriptable + a single BindToRenderStep at RenderPriority.Camera, writing CFrame AND Focus once per frame); then build the base follow from a parameterized rig and smooth the INTERMEDIATE values (focus/zoom/shoulder/FOV) — never the final CFrame — deriving the pose with CFrame.lookAt each frame; then layer the differentiators. The five killer systems are (1) a context-driven mode state machine that retargets ONE smoother, (2) a framerate-independent critically-damped spring follow with velocity look-ahead, (3) reactive FOV plus an additive trauma/impulse shake stacked with other gameplay-driven channels over the same base, (4) collision/occlusion-aware framing that pulls in instantly and pops out smoothly, and (5) lock-on two-target composition plus a distinct cutscene system. Because this whole system is runtime/render code, the agent builds it almost entirely with run_luau (write the camera LocalScript into the place), create_instance/set_property (the script container + a Configuration of named tunables), and verifies every tier visually with capture / capture_angles / capture_animation; make_npc, make_tool, bind_hitbox and bind_behavior supply the gameplay state/events the reactive channels react to.","killer_systems":[{"name":"Context-driven camera mode state machine","what":"A set of distinct, individually-tuned MODES (explore/default-follow, sprint, aim-ADS, lock-on, mounted/vehicle, menu, cutscene) that all share ONE bind loop and ONE smoother. Each mode only supplies its own parameter record — FieldOfView, follow distance, shoulder/lateral offset, pitch limits, sensitivity, spring stiffness — and changing mode just retargets the spring goals so the engine blends the transition for free.","how":"With run_luau write a modes table { explore={fov,dist,shoulder,omega,sens}, sprint={...}, ads={...}, lockon={...}, mounted={...}, menu/cutscene={...} } and a currentMode field. The single render-step reads the ACTIVE record (never hardcoded numbers) and feeds mode.fov/mode.dist/mode.shoulder into the existing scalar+Vector3 springs; apply shoulder offset in camera-LOCAL space (followCF * CFrame.new(shoulder)). Flip currentMode on gameplay events — sprint key, RMB-held for ADS, lock-on toggle, entering a vehicle/seat, opening a menu. Source the events from real systems: bind_behavior state attributes, a make_tool's equip/aim, ContextActionService/UserInputService binds. Menu and cutscene modes UnbindFromRenderStep and hand off to a separate controller via a priority so only one owner writes the camera. No separate transition code — every param is already spring-driven, so the mode switch IS the blend."},{"name":"Framerate-independent critically-damped spring follow with look-ahead","what":"The focus/position is driven by a critically-damped spring (or a dt-corrected exponential damping), not a rigid CFrame lock and not a fixed-alpha lerp, so it settles with natural weight and feels identical at 30 and 240 FPS — plus velocity-based look-ahead so fast movement leads the camera to reveal what is ahead, easing back to zero on stop.","how":"With run_luau implement the analytic critically-damped spring (Game Programming Gems 4 / Thomas Lowe form: f=1+2*dt*omega; oo=omega*omega; hoo=dt*oo; hhoo=dt*hoo; detInv=1/(f+hhoo); p,v updated each frame) which is exact for any dt. Run TWO springs: a snappier one for the look-target/focus and a softer one for distance/position so the body trails the aim and settles on stop. Add look-ahead by offsetting the position spring's GOAL by the subject's planar velocity (Vector3(vel.X,0,vel.Z)*leadFactor from AssemblyLinearVelocity or Humanoid.MoveDirection*speed); the offset springs back to 0 when the player stops. Spring POSITIONS and re-derive orientation each frame with CFrame.lookAt — never spring on Euler angles (they wrap). If the subject is physics-driven, read its velocity on RunService.Stepped/PostSimulation so the camera reads final-physics state and does not drift-and-snap. Verify the same feel across framerates by capturing at the move peak with capture / capture_animation."},{"name":"Reactive FOV + additive trauma/impulse shake (a STACK, not one gimmick)","what":"Multiple independent reactive channels — a dynamic FieldOfView that kicks on sprint/dash/speed/ADS, an additive decaying-trauma screen-shake from smoothed Perlin noise, and a directional impulse kick — each driven by gameplay state/events and each composited OVER the same shared follow CFrame, rather than the camera shipping one lone effect.","how":"With run_luau add a dedicated scalar FOV spring whose goal rises with speed (base + clamp(speed-walk,0,max)*fovPerStud) and drops for ADS (base*zoomFactor), clamped 1..120, kept ~60..90 to avoid distortion. Keep a single trauma scalar in [0,1]; gameplay events ADD trauma (clamp to 1), each frame decay trauma toward exactly 0 and compute shake=trauma^2; sample THREE decorrelated math.noise channels (distinct seeds, t*freq) for pitch/yaw/roll plus an optional positional noise, build shakeCF = CFrame.new(posOffset)*CFrame.Angles(rx,ry,rz). For the DIRECTIONAL kick keep a separate spring-decayed offset seeded along the force direction (recoil up, hit-from-left, landing down). Apply ALL cosmetic offsets LAST as post-multiplies onto the already-smoothed follow CFrame (base * kickCF * shakeCF) so they ride on the follow and never feed back into the spring. Trigger trauma/kick from real events: bind_hitbox confirmed hits, make_npc attacks, landing detection, make_tool fire. FOV punches and shake fire on the SAME base — that is the stack."},{"name":"Collision- and occlusion-aware framing (Popper-style)","what":"The camera never clips through geometry and never hides the subject: it raycasts from the focus toward the desired camera position and pulls IN on obstruction, pops smoothly back out when clear, and also handles TARGET occlusion (dolly/peek/fade when something blocks the subject).","how":"With run_luau, after computing the desired eye position, build RaycastParams{FilterType=Exclude, FilterDescendantsInstances={character, ignoreFolder}} and workspace:Raycast from the FOCUS outward along the eye direction at the desired distance; pierce non-collidable / >0.95-transparent / water parts by adding them to the ignore list. Take allowed = hit and (hit.Distance - nearPad) or desiredDist. Make the pull-IN INSTANT (clamp distance to allowed immediately so you never clip) but smooth ONLY the pop-OUT (spring/lerp the radius back toward desiredDist). Sample several viewport-corner rays (Camera:WorldToViewportPoint), not just center, so thin/edge geometry that the lens corners clip is caught. For TARGET occlusion use Camera:GetPartsObscuringTarget(castPoints, ignoreList) — pass Vector3 cast POINTS and add the subject to the ignoreList — and fade/peek when #obscuring>0. Verify by capturing from inside cover with capture_angles."},{"name":"Lock-on two-target framing + distinct cutscene system","what":"A lock-on camera keeps subject AND target both readable: positioned behind the player along the player->target axis, distance/FOV scaled by their separation, eased soft-lock in and out with a yaw/pitch hand-off so unlock never snaps. Separately, a cutscene system tweens authored keyframe CFrames with eased FOV, unbinds the gameplay loop while it runs, and blends back in.","how":"With run_luau add a lockon mode: select the nearest valid target (alive + in range, magnitude + a facing dot-product cone), each frame compute look = subjectPos:Lerp(targetPos, bias~0.35) (not dead-center) and place the eye behind the subject along the subject->target vector at a distance/FOV that grows with their separation (verify both on-screen with Camera:WorldToViewportPoint); drive look-target and eye through the SAME springs (snappier omega while locked) so acquire/break ease; reuse the occlusion raycast; suppress manual yaw while locked; on unlock WRITE the current locked orientation back into the free-look yaw/pitch accumulators so it does not revert to a stale pre-lock CFrame. For cutscenes: a SEPARATE controller — UnbindFromRenderStep the gameplay loop, set CameraType.Scriptable, author waypoints as Parts/Attachments, sequence eased TweenService shots chained on Tween.Completed (not task.wait) or CFrame:Lerp along a spline, set CFrame AND Focus; on end, tween the last cutscene CFrame into the live gameplay CFrame, then re-Bind and restore CameraType. Trigger from the server via a RemoteEvent; build/animate the rig with spawn_rig / pose_joint / add_keyframe / capture_animation if it is rig-driven."}],"common_pitfalls":["Two writers fighting over the frame: any second script (or a leftover TweenService tween) that also writes camera.CFrame will flicker-fight the render loop. Route EVERYTHING through one BindToRenderStep authority and one write per frame. Pick ONE owner per state (a per-frame lerp and a TweenService tween writing the same FieldOfView/CFrame cancel each other).","Updating on Heartbeat or a plain RenderStepped:Connect instead of BindToRenderStep at RenderPriority.Camera.Value — the character moves on one pipeline stage and the camera on another, producing the classic 1-frame jitter. (The one exception: a camera that follows a PHYSICS object should read on Heartbeat/PostSimulation so it sees final-physics position, else it drift-and-snaps.)","Forgetting Camera.Focus. In Scriptable mode Focus does NOT auto-update; leaving it stale anchors dynamic lighting, streaming detail, occlusion culling and 3D audio at the old spot. Set CFrame AND Focus every frame.","Lerping the FINAL camera CFrame toward a moving target — it never settles, fights the render pipeline (makes the CHARACTER look jittery), and is the wrong axis to smooth. Smooth the INTERMEDIATE values (focus/pivot, zoom distance, shoulder offset, FOV) and DERIVE the CFrame with CFrame.lookAt each frame.","Framerate-dependent smoothing: a constant lerp alpha (0.2) or speed*dt as the alpha makes the camera faster on high-FPS machines and sluggish on low — the #1 'feels inconsistent / feels off' defect that looks fine only on the dev's machine. Use 1-math.exp(-stiffness*dt) or an analytic spring. Also: apply deltaTime exactly ONCE in sway/bob math (double-dt is jitter that scales with speed).","Magic numbers instead of named tunables, and one uniform damping for every axis/direction. Build the pose from a Configuration of named values (distance, height, lateral shoulder, look-target) and tune smoothing per axis AND per direction (e.g. snap-in vs spring-out, snappier aim vs softer body).","Shake/effects that erase or get eaten by the follow: overwriting camera.CFrame with the shake (instead of post-multiplying onto the base) wipes the follow/lock-on; applying shake BEFORE the spring lets the spring eat it; feeding the cosmetic offset back into the smoother makes the shake stick and drift. Compose cosmetics LAST, post-multiplied, never fed back. Use math.noise (Perlin) not math.random, with a DISTINCT seed per axis, and decay trauma to exactly 0.","Occlusion done wrong: forgetting to exclude the player's own character (camera slams to the face), smoothing the pull-IN (clips through walls for several frames), a single center ray (misses thin/edge geometry the lens corners clip), and not filtering transparent/non-collidable parts (camera 'collides' with glass, leaves, force-fields).","Aim/body coupling: aiming the weapon along the camera's forward axis when the camera has a shoulder offset makes shots miss the crosshair — raycast from the camera through screen-center (Camera:ViewportPointToRay) for the true aim point, and bake the shoulder offset into BOTH the eye and the look target. More broadly, compute the AIM direction on a channel independent of the smoothed/sprung body position so look stays responsive while the body lags.","No comfort controls. A single hard-coded FOV feels claustrophobic to some and fish-eyed to others (an expectation mismatch, not a bug), and always-on intense bob/sway/shake induces motion sickness with no recourse. Expose a player FOV slider and per-effect intensity sliders + a master motion toggle, with conservative defaults, persisted.","Cutscene mistakes: leaving the gameplay render loop bound so it overwrites the tween every frame (camera looks frozen/fighting); sequencing shots with task.wait(duration) instead of Tween.Completed (drifts if interrupted); tweening linearly through 3+ waypoints (robotic dog-legs — use a spline/eased per-segment lerp); and forgetting to restore CameraType=Custom + CameraSubject so the player is stuck with no control. Blend the last cutscene CFrame into the live camera instead of a hard cut.","Lock-on snap-back: on unlock reverting to the pre-lock CFrame snaps jarringly — write the current orientation back into the free-look angle accumulators. Also: locking onto dead/out-of-range targets (validate every frame, auto-release), and framing only the target so the player slides off-screen (bias the look toward the target ~0.35 and scale distance/FOV with separation, verify with WorldToViewportPoint)."],"tiers":[{"tier":"baseline","goal":"The camera OWNS the frame through a single prioritized per-frame bind, builds a parameterized base follow, smooths the INTERMEDIATE pivot/zoom/FOV (never the final CFrame), and is structured as a base pose plus summed additive offset layers — so every later effect has one clean place to compose.","steps":[{"id":"cam-b1-decide-control-mode","order_index":1,"instruction":"Before scripting, decide the cheapest control tier that meets the design and record it in a Configuration. For plain first-person, set StarterPlayer.CameraMode = LockFirstPerson and stop. For tweakable third-person that needs no custom behavior, leave CameraType.Custom and just set CameraMaxZoomDistance/CameraMinZoomDistance. Only commit to a fully custom Scriptable camera when the design needs behavior the stock camera cannot do (over-the-shoulder, isometric, lock-on, cutscenes, stacked reactive game-feel). Use create_instance to make a 'CameraConfig' Configuration under StarterPlayer and set_property for the chosen mode + zoom bounds.","addresses_criteria":["camera-renderstep-ownership-bind-contract"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Jumping straight to Scriptable means re-implementing mouse-look yaw/pitch, zoom, and gamepad input the default PlayerModule gave you for free — many 'my camera feels worse than default' results are a badly-rebuilt wheel. Choose Scriptable only when you truly need custom per-frame ownership."},{"id":"cam-b2-renderstep-ownership-contract","order_index":2,"instruction":"Take full per-frame ownership. With create_instance add a LocalScript under StarterPlayerScripts (the camera is per-client; only the owning user sees it). With run_luau (or by writing the script body) implement the contract: grab local cam = workspace.CurrentCamera (CurrentCamera, NOT workspace.Camera, and re-grab on a Changed signal), set cam.CameraType = Enum.CameraType.Scriptable so Roblox's default camera scripts stop writing CFrame, then register exactly ONE authority: RunService:BindToRenderStep('Camera', Enum.RenderPriority.Camera.Value, updateCamera). Inside updateCamera compute a single final CFrame and write cam.CFrame = finalCFrame AND cam.Focus = CFrame.new(lookAtPoint) every frame. Add a brief task.wait so the character loads, and put the script in StarterCharacterScripts (or nil-guard the character) so respawn does not error.","addresses_criteria":["camera-renderstep-ownership-bind-contract"],"tools":["create_instance","run_luau","set_property","capture"],"pitfalls":"Both load-bearing facts must hold or this is only a PARTIAL: (1) the per-frame write must be your bind at RenderPriority.Camera (BindToRenderStep, not Heartbeat/RenderStepped:Connect — those jitter), and (2) the stock follow camera must be OFF (CameraType.Scriptable — a raw cam.CFRame write does nothing until this is set). Also: forgetting .Value on the enum (it is an EnumItem, not the integer), referencing workspace.Camera (the edit camera), and forgetting Focus (it drives lighting/streaming/audio) are the three classic silent failures."},{"id":"cam-b3-parameterized-base-follow","order_index":3,"instruction":"Build the base follow pose from NAMED tunables, not magic numbers. With run_luau read the tunables from the Configuration (distance, heightOffset, lateral shoulderOffset, lookTarget, pitchLimits, sensitivity) and each frame: re-fetch character + HumanoidRootPart with FindFirstChild (bail if missing — characters respawn); compute focusPoint = root.Position + Vector3.new(0, heightOffset, 0); compute the eye via camera-LOCAL composition (anchorCF * CFrame.new(shoulderOffset.X, shoulderOffset.Y, distance), MULTIPLY not add) so you never accumulate; then finalCFrame = CFrame.lookAt(eye, focusPoint). For over-the-shoulder add the lateral offset to BOTH the eye and the look target. Capture mouse delta into clamped yaw/pitch accumulators (ContextActionService MouseMovement, MouseBehavior.LockCenter) for free-look. Store the tunables in the StarterPlayer Configuration via create_instance/set_property.","addresses_criteria":["camera-parameterized-rig-and-asymmetric-tuning","camera-renderstep-ownership-bind-contract"],"tools":["run_luau","create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Reading character/HumanoidRootPart once at script start breaks on respawn — re-fetch each frame. Writing cam.CFrame = cam.CFrame + Vector3 INSIDE the render step adds the delta EVERY frame (camera shoots off forever) — recompute an absolute anchor each frame. Baking the shoulder offset only into the eye (not the look target) makes the crosshair and camera disagree so raycasts miss the reticle. Always clamp pitch (e.g. ±75) or the camera flips over the character."},{"id":"cam-b4-layered-additive-composition","order_index":4,"instruction":"Structure the camera as a stable BASE pose plus SUMMED, independently-tunable offset layers composited ON TOP, so every later effect plugs into one pipeline. With run_luau define the compose order inside the single bind: finalCFrame = baseCFrame * shakeCFrame * bobCFrame * kickCFrame (each layer an identity transform until its system populates it), and/or route body-relative micro-offsets through Humanoid.CameraOffset. Initialize each layer (shake, bob, recoil kick, lean/ADS micro-sway, FOV delta) as a separate, named, zeroed offset the later tiers will drive. The cosmetic layers post-multiply the base and must be applied LAST.","addresses_criteria":["camera-layered-additive-composition"],"tools":["run_luau"],"pitfalls":"Applying any layer by OVERWRITING camera.CFrame (instead of multiplying onto the base) erases the follow/lock-on. Mixing world-space and camera-local offsets is the classic 'camera ends up in the void' bug — apply offsets in camera-local space (baseCF * offsetCF). Keep each layer a separate summed term so you can tune/disable one without touching the others."},{"id":"cam-b5-smooth-intermediate-not-final","order_index":5,"instruction":"Smooth the INPUTS, derive the CFrame — never lerp the final pose, and give each smoothed quantity its OWN persistent 'current' value AND its OWN smoothing constant. With run_luau, keep separate persistent current values for the focus/pivot point, the zoom distance, the shoulder-offset Vector3, and the FOV scalar, and ease EACH toward its goal every frame with framerate-independent damping: alpha_q = 1 - math.exp(-stiffness_q * dt), where dt is the BindToRenderStep dt and stiffness_q is a NAMED per-channel constant (focusStiffness, distanceStiffness, offsetStiffness, fovStiffness) — do NOT collapse them into one shared scalar. Then DERIVE the final camera CFrame from the smoothed intermediates with CFrame.lookAt(smoothedEye, smoothedFocus) (smoothedEye built from the smoothed distance + smoothed shoulder-offset applied via PointToWorldSpace), and set Camera.FieldOfView from the smoothed FOV. Never write currentCFrame = currentCFrame:Lerp(goalCFrame, alpha) on the final pose, and never apply one uniform damping constant to all channels. Run inside the single RenderPriority.Camera BindToRenderStep. Then use capture_animation while walking and confirm the body eases smoothly with NO final-CFrame jitter signature (no per-frame stale/desynced shake), reporting what the capture shows. (Scope: this step addresses camera-smooth-intermediate-not-final-cframe only; per-axis/per-direction ASYMMETRIC damping and rig parameterization for OTS/isometric/top-down generalization belong to the separate parameterized-rig step and must not be claimed here unless that full asymmetric, named-tunable rig is actually delivered.)","addresses_criteria":["camera-smooth-intermediate-not-final-cframe","camera-parameterized-rig-and-asymmetric-tuning"],"tools":["run_luau","capture_animation"],"pitfalls":"Lerping the final CFrame toward a moving target never settles, smooths the wrong axis, and (inside a RenderPriority.Camera bind) fights the render pipeline so the CHARACTER looks jittery even though the math 'looks' right. The fix the pros converge on: smooth the OFFSET inputs (shoulder/zoom/CameraOffset/focus), not the camera itself. Use capture_animation while walking to confirm the body eases without the final-CFrame jitter signature."}]},{"tier":"good","goal":"The camera becomes a context-driven state machine of tuned modes sharing one smoother, decouples aim from the lagging follow body, gates framing on movement state, fires a directional impulse kick distinct from shake, and is fully parameterized with axis- and direction-asymmetric smoothing.","steps":[{"id":"cam-g1-context-mode-state-machine","order_index":1,"instruction":"Build the context-driven mode state machine. With run_luau define a modes table where each mode is a record of tuned params { fov, distance, shoulderOffset (camera-local Vector3), pitchLimits, sensitivity, springOmega } and keep a currentMode. The single render loop reads the ACTIVE record (never hardcoded numbers) and feeds mode params into the smoother's goals (distance goal = mode.distance, shoulder applied as baseCF * CFrame.new(mode.shoulderOffset), FOV via a scalar smoother toward mode.fov, mouse delta scaled by mode.sensitivity). Switch currentMode on gameplay events — sprint key -> sprint, RMB held -> ads, lock-on toggle -> lockon, enter seat/vehicle -> mounted, open menu -> menu. Source the events from real systems: bind_behavior state attributes (StateChanged), a make_tool's equip/aim, ContextActionService binds. Menu/cutscene modes Unbind the render step and hand to a separate controller via a priority so only one owner writes the camera. Let the existing smoother do the blend — no separate transition code.","addresses_criteria":["camera-context-driven-mode-state-machine"],"tools":["run_luau","bind_behavior","make_tool","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Hard-cutting params between modes (assigning fov/distance directly) snaps jarringly — route every mode param through the smoother so the transition IS the blend. Don't let two modes both own the camera (ADS + cutscene) — use a priority stack. A single fixed default mode (no state machine) is the generic-AI tell the rubric grades down."},{"id":"cam-g2-look-body-decoupling","order_index":2,"instruction":"Decouple the aim/look direction from the smoothed follow body. With run_luau compute the camera's AIM channel (yaw/pitch from input, or the direction toward a tracked target) on its OWN, responsive channel — independent of the smoothed/sprung follow POSITION. Each frame: the aim updates immediately so the camera tracks where the player (or tracked target) looks, while the body/position lags through its smoother. Concretely, derive the look direction from the live aim accumulators and the eye position from the smoothed pivot, then lookAt(smoothedEye, smoothedEye + liveAimDir * far). Keep this rig-agnostic — it must work whether the subject is an R15 Humanoid, a vehicle, or a tracked object.","addresses_criteria":["camera-look-direction-body-decoupling"],"tools":["run_luau"],"pitfalls":"Routing the AIM through the same smoother as the position makes aiming feel mushy and disconnected (and nausea-inducing). Conversely, snapping the whole pose to input with no body smoothing loses the weight. The point is two channels: responsive aim, lagging body. Don't tie the decoupling to a Humanoid-specific field — read a generic subject position/velocity so it stays rig-agnostic."},{"id":"cam-g3-movement-state-gated-framing","order_index":3,"instruction":"Add movement-state-gated framing that eases to neutral. With run_luau read the subject's actual movement STATE/SPEED (Humanoid.MoveDirection.Magnitude / AssemblyLinearVelocity, or a bind_behavior state attribute) and change the camera's FRAMING in response — e.g. a velocity-scaled walk-bob (math.sin over time, amplitude/frequency tied to speed), a slight pull-back or height change while sprinting, an idle settle — then EASE that change back to neutral when the state clears. This must be DISTINCT from the smoother's own settle: it is a deliberate framing response to movement state, not just the spring catching up. Gate bob on actual movement (Magnitude > 0) so it does not run while standing still. Expose intensity tunables in the Configuration.","addresses_criteria":["camera-first-person-liveliness-speed-gated"],"tools":["run_luau","bind_behavior","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Apply deltaTime exactly ONCE in the bob/sway math — smoothing the value frame-rate-independently AND then multiplying by dt again double-applies dt and gives speed-dependent jitter. A fixed, unchanging frame that never reacts to movement reads as a tripod, not embodied motion. Tie bob speed to walkspeed or a slowed game looks sluggish. Keep amplitude conservative and toggleable (motion sickness)."},{"id":"cam-g4-directional-impulse-kick","order_index":4,"instruction":"Add a directional impulse kick, distinct from omnidirectional shake. With run_luau keep a separate spring-decayed offset (a Vector3/angle pair) that gameplay seeds along the force DIRECTION: recoil up, hit-from-the-left pushes the look right, landing pushes down. On the event, add an impulse along that direction; each frame let it decay back to zero via spring/damping and compose it as its own kickCFrame layer (base * kickCFrame, post-multiplied). Trigger from real events: bind_hitbox confirmed hits (use the hit vector for direction), make_tool fire (recoil up), a landing detector (down, scaled by fall speed), make_npc attacks. This conveys WHERE force came from — separate from the trauma rumble.","addresses_criteria":["camera-directional-impulse-kick"],"tools":["run_luau","bind_hitbox","make_tool","make_npc"],"pitfalls":"A kick that is just more omnidirectional shake fails the 'directional' bar — the offset must be seeded along the actual force direction so the player reads where it came from. Feeding the kick into the follow smoother makes it stick/drift — keep it a separate decaying layer post-multiplied onto the base. Don't reuse the trauma scalar for it; the kick is directional and discrete, the trauma is omnidirectional."},{"id":"cam-g5-asymmetric-per-axis-tuning","order_index":5,"instruction":"Make all smoothing constants tunable PER AXIS and PER DIRECTION rather than one uniform damping. With run_luau split the smoothing constants in the Configuration: a snappier stiffness for the aim/look-target than for the body/position; a different constant for vertical vs horizontal; and asymmetric in/out (e.g. snap-in vs ease-out where relevant). Confirm the whole pose is built from named tunables (distance, height-offset, lateral shoulder-offset, look-target, per-axis/per-direction smoothing) with no magic numbers left in the loop. Persist them in the Configuration via set_property so they are designer-tunable.","addresses_criteria":["camera-parameterized-rig-and-asymmetric-tuning"],"tools":["run_luau","set_property","create_instance"],"pitfalls":"One uniform damping for everything is the thing the rubric grades against — the body should lag softer than the aim, and some transitions (occlusion pull-in vs pop-out) are deliberately asymmetric. Leaving magic numbers inline (instead of named Configuration tunables) fails the parameterized-rig half of the criterion even if the feel is fine."}]},{"tier":"exceptional","goal":"The follow is a framerate-independent critically-damped spring with velocity look-ahead; reactive FOV and an additive trauma shake stack with the directional kick over ONE base; the camera is collision/occlusion-aware (snap-in, smooth-out, corner-sampled, target-occlusion handled); and a lock-on two-target composition plus a distinct cutscene system top it off — all verified visually.","steps":[{"id":"cam-e1-spring-follow-lookahead","order_index":1,"instruction":"Upgrade the intermediate smoothing to a framerate-independent critically-damped SPRING with look-ahead. With run_luau replace the exponential lerp on the pivot/distance with the analytic critically-damped spring (Thomas Lowe / GPG4 form: f=1+2*dt*omega; oo=omega*omega; hoo=dt*oo; hhoo=dt*hoo; detInv=1/(f+hhoo); update p and v) — exact for any dt so 30 and 240 FPS match. Run TWO springs: a snappier one for the look-target/focus and a softer one for distance/position so the body trails then settles. Add look-ahead by offsetting the position spring GOAL by the subject's planar velocity (Vector3(vel.X,0,vel.Z)*leadFactor from AssemblyLinearVelocity / Humanoid.MoveDirection*speed); the offset springs back to 0 on stop so fast movement reveals what's ahead. Spring POSITIONS and re-derive orientation with lookAt; if the subject is physics-driven read its velocity on RunService.Stepped/PostSimulation. Verify identical feel across framerates and the lead-then-settle with capture_animation.","addresses_criteria":["camera-framerate-independent-spring-follow-lookahead","camera-smooth-intermediate-not-final-cframe"],"tools":["run_luau","capture_animation","capture"],"pitfalls":"Naive Lerp(goal, 0.1) is dt-dependent (faster at high fps) and asymptotic (never reaches goal). Springing on Euler angles wraps at ±2π and snaps — spring positions and recompute orientation with lookAt. A physics-tracked spring run on RenderStepped desyncs with physics and jitters — use Stepped/PostSimulation for physics targets. An over-stiff spring kills the benefit; too loose feels disconnected and nauseating."},{"id":"cam-e2-reactive-fov-trauma-shake","order_index":2,"instruction":"Add reactive FOV plus an additive trauma shake as composed channels. With run_luau give FieldOfView its own scalar spring: goal = base + clamp(speed-walk,0,maxBonus)*fovPerStud on sprint/dash, base*zoomFactor on ADS, with a transient punch on dash that decays; clamp 1..120, keep gameplay ~60..90. For shake keep a single trauma scalar in [0,1]: gameplay events ADD trauma (clamp to 1); each frame trauma -= decay*dt toward exactly 0 and shake = trauma^2; sample THREE decorrelated math.noise channels (distinct seeds, t*freq) for pitch/yaw/roll plus optional positional noise; build shakeCF = CFrame.new(posOffset)*CFrame.Angles(rx,ry,rz) and apply it LAST as base * ... * shakeCF. Drive trauma from real events: bind_hitbox hits, make_npc attacks, landing, make_tool fire (recoil also adds to the directional kick). Verify the shake rides on the follow with capture / capture_angles.","addresses_criteria":["camera-reactive-fov-and-trauma-shake"],"tools":["run_luau","bind_hitbox","make_npc","make_tool","capture"],"pitfalls":"math.random instead of math.noise gives buzzy jitter; the SAME noise sample on all 3 axes shakes along one diagonal line. Applying shake BEFORE the spring lets the spring eat it, and feeding the offset back into the spring makes it stick/drift — apply LAST and post-multiply, never feed back. Constant nonzero trauma reads as a permanent wobble; trauma must decay to exactly 0, and squaring it prevents a hard linear cutoff. Tweening FOV with a fresh TweenService tween every frame on a changing speed stutters — use the scalar spring."},{"id":"cam-e3-stacked-reactive-channels","order_index":3,"instruction":"Compose MULTIPLE reactive channels over the ONE shared base — not a single gimmick. With run_luau ensure the FOV kick (e2), the trauma shake (e2), the directional impulse kick (g4), and the movement-gated framing (g3) are each driven by gameplay state/events and each composited over the SAME base follow in a fixed order inside the single bind: base -> smoothing/spring -> occlusion clamp -> reactive FOV -> directional kick -> trauma shake -> write CFrame+Focus ONCE. Smoothing before occlusion (clamp the smoothed result), cosmetics last (must not feed the smoother). Wire high-speed/high-impact feel from a STACK of distinct channels keyed to thresholds (dash crosses a speed threshold -> FOV punch + slight shake; heavy landing -> down-kick + trauma; hit -> directional kick + trauma) so it never ships one lone effect.","addresses_criteria":["camera-stacked-reactive-effects-at-thresholds","camera-layered-additive-composition"],"tools":["run_luau","bind_hitbox","make_npc"],"pitfalls":"Letting each feature (shake, FOV, kick, lock-on, cutscene) write the camera independently is the core anti-pattern — they overwrite each other and flicker; keep the write-once discipline through one pipeline. Composing occlusion BEFORE smoothing lets the camera pop through walls mid-ease. Feeding the cosmetic shake back into the spring makes it stick. Shipping one channel (just shake, or just FOV) fails the 'stack at thresholds' bar."},{"id":"cam-e4-collision-occlusion-framing","order_index":4,"instruction":"Make the framing collision- and occlusion-aware. With run_luau, after computing the desired eye, build RaycastParams{FilterType=Exclude, FilterDescendantsInstances={character, ignoreFolder}} and workspace:Raycast from the FOCUS outward along the eye direction at the desired distance; pierce non-collidable / >0.95-transparent / water parts via the ignore list. allowed = hit and (hit.Distance - nearPad) or desiredDist. Make pull-IN INSTANT (clamp distance to allowed immediately) but smooth ONLY the pop-OUT (spring/lerp the radius back toward desiredDist). Sample several viewport-corner rays (Camera:WorldToViewportPoint), not just center, to catch thin/edge geometry. For TARGET occlusion call Camera:GetPartsObscuringTarget(castPoints, ignoreList) (pass Vector3 cast POINTS; add the subject to the ignoreList) and dolly/peek/fade when #obscuring>0. Place this clamp AFTER smoothing, BEFORE the cosmetic layers. Verify from inside cover with capture_angles.","addresses_criteria":["camera-collision-occlusion-aware-framing"],"tools":["run_luau","capture_angles","capture"],"pitfalls":"Forgetting to exclude the player's own character slams the camera to the face. Smoothing the pull-IN lets the camera clip through the wall for several frames — pull in INSTANTLY, smooth only the pop-out. A single center ray misses thin/edge geometry the lens corners clip — sample viewport corners. Not filtering transparent/non-collidable parts makes the camera 'collide' with glass, leaves and force-fields. GetPartsObscuringTarget takes Vector3 POINTS, not instances, and you must add the target to the ignore list or it reports itself."},{"id":"cam-e5-lockon-cinematic-composition","order_index":5,"instruction":"Add lock-on two-target framing plus a distinct cutscene system. With run_luau add a lockon mode: select the nearest VALID target (alive + in range via magnitude, plus a facing dot-product cone), then each frame compute look = subjectPos:Lerp(targetPos, bias~0.35) (not dead-center, add a thirds/headroom local pan), place the eye behind the subject along the subject->target vector at a distance/FOV that GROWS with their separation so both stay readable at any range (verify both with Camera:WorldToViewportPoint), drive look-target and eye through the SAME springs (snappier omega while locked) for an eased soft-lock, reuse the occlusion raycast, suppress manual yaw while locked, validate the target every frame (auto-release if dead/out-of-range), and on unlock WRITE the current locked orientation back into the free-look yaw/pitch accumulators so it does not snap. For the SEPARATE cutscene system: a distinct controller that UnbindFromRenderStep the gameplay loop, sets CameraType.Scriptable, authors waypoints as Parts/Attachments (or a rig built with spawn_rig + pose_joint + add_keyframe), sequences eased TweenService shots chained on Tween.Completed (or CFrame:Lerp along a spline), sets CFrame AND Focus, then on end tweens the last cutscene CFrame into the live gameplay CFrame and re-Binds + restores CameraType.Custom + CameraSubject. Trigger from the server via a RemoteEvent. Verify both with capture_animation.","addresses_criteria":["camera-lockon-cinematic-composition"],"tools":["run_luau","make_npc","spawn_rig","pose_joint","add_keyframe","capture_animation"],"pitfalls":"On unlock, reverting to the pre-lock CFrame snaps jarringly — hand the current orientation back to the free-look angles. Putting the look at the exact midpoint pins the enemy dead-center (no composition) — bias toward the target and add headroom; a fixed distance lets a far target fall off-screen, so scale distance/FOV with separation. For cutscenes: leaving the gameplay loop bound makes it overwrite the tween (camera looks frozen/fighting); task.wait(duration) sequencing drifts — use Tween.Completed; forgetting to restore CameraType=Custom + CameraSubject leaves the player with no control; a hard cut back is jarring — blend the last cutscene CFrame into the live camera."}]}],"provenance":{"sources":["https://create.roblox.com/docs/reference/engine/classes/Camera","https://create.roblox.com/docs/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/input-and-camera/control-the-users-camera","https://create.roblox.com/docs/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/input-and-camera/control-the-users-camera","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/smooth-camera-position-in-third-person/2644314","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/over-the-shoulder-camera-collision-problem/950861","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/dynamic-fov-script-smooth-camera-zoom/2781094","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/ez-camera-shake-ported-to-roblox/98482","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/camera-target-lock-on-system-help/2227744","https://create.roblox.com/docs/reference/engine/classes/Camera","https://github.com/rduoDevs/CameraService","https://create.roblox.com/docs/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/input-and-camera/control-the-users-camera","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/short-form-guide-to-solving-physics-based-spring-jitter/2078873","https://create.roblox.com/docs/reference/engine/classes/Camera","https://github.com/Sleitnick/RbxCameraShaker","https://create.roblox.com/docs/reference/engine/classes/Camera","https://github.com/Roblox/Core-Scripts/blob/master/PlayerScripts/StarterPlayerScripts_NewStructure/RobloxPlayerScript/CameraScript/Poppercam/Zoom/Popper.lua","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/over-the-shoulder-camera-system/728481","https://create.roblox.com/docs/reference/engine/classes/Camera","https://create.roblox.com/docs/reference/engine/classes/Camera","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/major-camera-stuttering-when-lerping-to-an-object-delta-time-issue/1765872","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/camera-jitter-even-when-using-cframelerp/2185837","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/why-is-my-custom-camera-movement-so-unsmooth/2787829","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/my-camera-sway-is-working-but-camera-is-like-jittery/2387531","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/need-help-with-smooth-camera-movement/1360856","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/character-appears-jittery-when-using-custom-camera-script-bindtorenderstep/4169577","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/fov-feels-weird-and-off/2691713","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/thoughts-on-this-advanced-camera-movement-bobbing-turning-strafing-idle-and-more/3050390","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0TpwPpULXxU (XTB7, human captions); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FhCNvyuZ-mE (BrawlDev, verified); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UsahcBf18jE (B Ricey); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MU9HFZUeg-c (script_ing)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0TpwPpULXxU (XTB7, human captions)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UsahcBf18jE (B Ricey)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UsahcBf18jE (B Ricey); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0TpwPpULXxU (XTB7)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LdwNqyknyPU (Previized); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MU9HFZUeg-c (script_ing)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kq0k7UrOR-U (blox); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3W8C8R9X9eU (rnk); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FhCNvyuZ-mE (BrawlDev); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0TpwPpULXxU (XTB7)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nd9tzpjONNA (MonkeyDev)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3W8C8R9X9eU (rnk)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MU9HFZUeg-c (script_ing); also https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mu3HfDjQfwE (Teletacos) and https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FWK4zQWSduI (Alcatraz) for corroboration","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FhCNvyuZ-mE (BrawlDev, verified); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LdwNqyknyPU (Previized)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6D9T4iaQHAc (Ludius)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FhCNvyuZ-mE (BrawlDev, verified)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FhCNvyuZ-mE (BrawlDev, verified)"],"confidence":"high","coverage":{"criteria_total":13,"criteria_addressed":13,"uncovered":[]}}}]==],
	-- characters.guide.json
	[==[{"module":"characters","system_module":"characters","version":"1.0","rubric_version":"1.0","overview":"Roblox hands every game the stock blocky R15 avatar — default proportions, default body colors, the default decal face, no accessories, no custom geometry, the same look on every character — and that IS the generic \"AI did it\" mean this guide beats. The whole approach pivots on treating the character as a DESIGNED IDENTITY built from five killer systems, in this order: (1) deliberate per-archetype PROPORTIONS via the HumanoidDescription scale axes, applied with run_luau so the silhouette reads its role before any texture; (2) a COHESIVE THEMED OUTFIT mixing rigid hardpoint accessories with deformable layered clothing on the correct attachment points, bound by one theme spine; (3) a CUSTOM BODY where the design needs it — custom MeshPart limbs/heads or a non-humanoid creature rig (generate_mesh/author_mesh/spawn_rig), Motor6D-jointed and weighted with no clipping; (4) a DESIGNED COLOR + PBR MATERIAL palette (limited 3-5 colors with a 60/30/10 accent hierarchy, plus SurfaceAppearance maps so metal/cloth/skin read as different physical materials), value-validated and authored on the face/skin too; and (5) a HumanoidDescription-driven VARIATION SYSTEM (one description payload mutated and applied via ApplyDescription) that produces the whole cohesive cast/crowd from one design language. Because the agent has no dedicated ApplyDescription or SurfaceAppearance tool, all description assembly, scaling, accessory composition, and PBR-material wiring is done through run_luau (+ create_instance/set_property), while geometry comes from the mesh/rig tools and every visual claim is verified with capture/capture_angles. Baseline = a custom body that commits to a deliberately-scaled archetype and a themed multi-class outfit. Good = the designed color+PBR palette, authored layered clothing, custom rig fidelity, an authored 3D face, and the ApplyDescription variation system. Exceptional = a per-archetype-distinct silhouette validated at distance, a governing shape-language hierarchy, an authored surface/baked-light treatment, a single deliberate focal point, and one shared identity system locking the whole cast.","killer_systems":[{"name":"Deliberate Proportions & Scaling (silhouette-first)","what":"Body proportions are a CHOSEN, role-readable decision anchored to a named archetype (Classic ~4.75 studs blocky; Rthro Normal ~5.75-6.5 wide-shoulder/narrow-hip; Rthro Slender ~5.25-6.25 narrow-shoulder/wide-hip) and driven by the five HumanoidDescription scale axes — BodyTypeScale, HeightScale, WidthScale, HeadScale, ProportionScale — so the solid-black outline reads hero / grunt / heavy / lithe / creature before any texture. This is the FIRST identity decision and the thing the stock cast cannot satisfy because everyone shares one silhouette.","how":"With run_luau build an Instance.new('HumanoidDescription'), set per-archetype presets — e.g. HEAVY = WidthScale ~1.25, HeightScale ~1.1, HeadScale ~0.85; HERO = HeightScale ~1.1, BodyTypeScale ~0.6, ProportionScale ~0.8; SCOUT = HeightScale ~0.9, WidthScale ~0.9, HeadScale ~1.1 — then apply with humanoid:ApplyDescription(desc) (or Player:LoadCharacterWithHumanoidDescription on spawn). CRITICAL ORDER: ProportionScale is a no-op while BodyTypeScale is 0, so raise BodyTypeScale ABOVE 0 first; all five axes are R15-only and clamped 0-1. Exaggerate per-region against a reference, not a uniform stretch. Verify by filling the rendered character solid black (capture) and confirming the archetype is nameable from the outline; for extreme silhouettes the axes are not enough — author custom MeshParts (generate_mesh/author_mesh) instead. Store the numbers as a named preset table keyed by archetype so the variation system reuses them."},{"name":"Cohesive Themed Outfit (rigid + layered composition)","what":"Accessories and clothing are composed into ONE theme-coherent identity — a curated set with consistent shape language and a single era/faction/theme spine — deliberately MIXING accessory classes (deformable layered/cage-driven clothing for body-conforming coverage PLUS rigid hardpoint pieces like helmet/backpack/pauldrons for crisp silhouette shapes) on the correct standardized attachment points, with restraint (solid clothing + a few accent accessories, not a 100-item pile), rather than a random unthemed marketplace hat-pile.","how":"Pick one theme spine first, then compose. Build the outfit as a single typed list and apply atomically via run_luau: desc:SetAccessories(specs) then humanoid:ApplyDescription(desc), where each spec is {AssetId=<id>, AccessoryType=Enum.AccessoryType.<slot>, IsLayered=<bool>, Order=<n for layered>}; lower Order sits closer to skin (shirt Order=1, jacket Order=2, backpack Order=3) so the stack reads coherent instead of clipping. Rigid pieces set IsLayered=false; layered set IsLayered=true with an Order. Author the character's OWN handheld/hardpoint props as accessories with make_tool (then grip_tool to seat them on the grip attachment), and custom worn meshes with generate_mesh/author_mesh parented to an Accessory + Attachment whose name matches the body attachment (HatAttachment, NeckAttachment, RightShoulderAttachment, etc.) so the engine snaps it. Know that shoulder attachments move with the arm and collar attachments do not — put a pauldron on the shoulder, a cape on the collar. Budget the count and verify centered, non-clipping placement with capture_angles."},{"name":"Custom Body / Non-Humanoid Rig Fidelity","what":"Where the design's identity calls for non-stock geometry, the build commits to custom MeshPart limbs/heads or a fully custom non-humanoid creature rig — Motor6D on animated parts (welds on static), skin-weighted, distinct per-part shapes, and no clipping/gaps at the joints — instead of leaving every character on the stock blocky humanoid. This is the foundation the color/material/face layers compose onto.","how":"For man-made parts (armor pieces, mechanical limbs) use generate_mesh (Cube text-to-3D); for organic creature forms (rock-like carapace, pine-like crests) use author_mesh; persist owned/shippable geometry with save_mesh. Stand the body up on a spawn_rig R15 Motor6D rig so clips and clothing map joints correctly; for a creature, build the part chain (RootPart -> lower torso -> upper torso -> limbs) and rig joints with run_luau (create Motor6D, set Part0/Part1 and C0/C1 so the pivot sits at the real hinge), name one part exactly 'Head', set a forward-facing PrimaryPart, and tune Humanoid.HipHeight in a playtest so it stands rather than slides/floats. Pose-test deformation with pose_joint/ik_reach + capture before committing. Keep <=4 joint influences per vertex and concentrate joints in expressive appendages."},{"name":"Designed Color + PBR Material Palette","what":"A constrained, harmonized color palette (3-5 colors, 2-3 for a skin) with a 60% dominant / 30% secondary / 10% accent value hierarchy and a named analogous/complementary harmony, PLUS an intentional per-region material hierarchy where metal vs rough cloth/canvas vs skin read as different physical surfaces — not default gray, random saturated limbs, or uniform Plastic. The palette is value-validated (desaturate under multiple lights) and the materials use physically-based values.","how":"Color: with run_luau set the HumanoidDescription per-limb Color3 fields (HeadColor/TorsoColor/LeftArmColor/RightArmColor/LeftLegColor/RightLegColor, which populate BodyColors on apply); concentrate the accent/highest contrast on the role-relevant zone (face/weapon/chest). For Parts, paint_texture authors a tiling procedural surface (PARTS ONLY — MeshParts are rejected). Materials: for custom MeshParts add a SurfaceAppearance child via create_instance and wire the four PBR maps (ColorMap/NormalMap/RoughnessMap/MetalnessMap) with set_property — metal = MetalnessMap ~1.0 + low RoughnessMap, cloth = metalness 0 + high roughness + a normal carrying weave, skin = mid roughness + metalness 0; use an OpenGL-tangent normal and one atlas/material per mesh. SurfaceAppearance.Color is the only runtime-mutable channel (tint a near-white ColorMap to make recolor variants for free). VALIDATE value structure by desaturating under front/side/rim lighting (capture under varied Lighting) and confirm forms stay separated and the accent still reads."},{"name":"HumanoidDescription-Driven Variation System (cast-wide)","what":"Customization/variation is driven as a SYSTEM by one HumanoidDescription carrying the FULL identity payload (scales + BodyColors + Shirt/Pants/ShirtGraphic + rigid SetAccessories + layered accessories + body-part mesh IDs + face), cloned-and-mutated to produce skin/palette/accessory swaps for player choice or NPC crowd variety — NOT one hardcoded look duplicated on every character. One shared description design language drives the whole cohesive cast.","how":"With run_luau, author a base description once (humanoid:GetAppliedDescription(), Players:GetHumanoidDescriptionFromUserIdAsync(userId) wrapped in pcall, or a hand-built Instance.new). Then desc:Clone() PER character and mutate before apply — randomize TorsoColor/HeadColor from a palette pool, pick one of N SetAccessories sets, jitter HeightScale/WidthScale — and call humanoid:ApplyDescription(clone) on each. ALWAYS Clone() before mutating or all your 'varied' NPCs share one mutated record. For NPC crowds spawn the population with make_npc and apply a mutated clone per spawn (batch/stagger — ApplyDescription yields and rebuilds the character, so don't apply to a huge crowd in one frame). Keep ONE committed cast-wide treatment (shared palette + material language) so the population reads as one authored world; lock a hero/anchor asset and render each new character side-by-side at matched lighting (capture) to catch drift."}],"common_pitfalls":["Leaving the stock R15 body untouched and only setting a single Color or shirt — that is the generic mean the rubric scores as FAIL on nearly every criterion (proportions, custom body, silhouette, shape language, focal point, cast cohesion). A color/shirt swap is not a design; you must commit to deliberate scale + custom geometry + a themed outfit to pass.","Setting ProportionScale while BodyTypeScale is still 0 — it silently does nothing and looks like a no-op bug. Raise BodyTypeScale above 0 FIRST. And all five scale axes are R15-only and clamped 0-1, so extreme silhouettes need custom MeshParts (generate_mesh/author_mesh), not the axes.","Mutating a HumanoidDescription in place and applying it to every character — they all end up sharing one mutated record instead of varying. Always desc:Clone() before per-character randomization, and verify on a small spawned crowd (make_npc) that individuals actually differ.","Trying to paint_texture a MeshPart — it is rejected (procedural tiling smears on arbitrary mesh UVs). paint_texture is PARTS ONLY; custom meshes get their material from generate_mesh's built-in texturing or an authored SurfaceAppearance (create_instance + set_property) instead.","Expecting to swap SurfaceAppearance maps by script at runtime — the ColorMap/Normal/Roughness/Metalness are pre-processed and NOT runtime-mutable. Only SurfaceAppearance.Color (tint) is dynamic. All non-tint material variation must exist as authored assets, so author the material set up front.","Mismatching accessory/clothing scale type, or putting a layered garment on a non-caged/blocky avatar — rigid accessories tied to one body scale float/clip on another, and ApplyDescription can fail to wrap layered clothing onto a stock blocky body (it needs a WrapTarget cage). Match the scale type and commit to a cageable custom body before composing layered clothing.","Getting accessory layer Order wrong — a sweater with a lower Order than the shirt renders UNDER it. Lower Order = closer to skin; verify the stack with capture_angles, not by assumption.","Mixing the two accessory APIs — the legacy comma-string properties (HatAccessory='id1,id2') and the modern SetAccessories table conflict. Pick SetAccessories and stick to it.","Designing a character in isolation — readability is RELATIVE to what it stands next to. A hero whose palette/silhouette mirrors the enemy is unreadable in-context. Render the character beside its cast/enemies (capture) and confirm allegiance and archetype read at a glance.","Judging the build from a low-quality capture — no anti-aliasing, low graphics, default lighting produces false 'bad model / bad material' verdicts. Force max graphics + AA + Future lighting before capture, then grade the BUILD, not the render settings.","Grading deliberate stylization against a realism ideal — a 'cute' or intentionally rough design is not a flaw. Capture the stated intent first and prefer fixes that REINFORCE the chosen style over ones that normalize it toward the generic mean.","Mis-rigging the custom body — a backwards-facing PrimaryPart makes it walk the wrong way; HipHeight left at 0 makes it slide/levitate; a part driven by multiple Motor6Ds won't appear on the animation timeline. Verify the rig stands and the joints sit at real hinges (capture) before layering look on top."],"tiers":[{"tier":"baseline","goal":"A custom-bodied character that has stopped being the stock avatar: a deliberately-scaled, named archetype whose silhouette reads its role, wearing a theme-coherent outfit that mixes rigid and layered accessory classes on the correct attachment points — the structural seam every look-and-variation system composes onto.","steps":[{"id":"char-base-1-archetype-and-scale","order_index":1,"instruction":"Decide the proportion archetype and body language FIRST (hero / grunt / heavy / lithe / creature, mapped to Classic ~4.75 studs blocky, Rthro Normal ~5.75-6.5 wide-shoulder/narrow-hip, or Rthro Slender ~5.25-6.25 narrow-shoulder/wide-hip), then with run_luau build an Instance.new('HumanoidDescription') and set the five R15 scale axes to a named per-archetype preset (e.g. HEAVY = WidthScale ~1.25, HeightScale ~1.1, HeadScale ~0.85; HERO = HeightScale ~1.1, BodyTypeScale ~0.6, ProportionScale ~0.8). Raise BodyTypeScale ABOVE 0 before touching ProportionScale (it is a no-op otherwise), exaggerate per-region rather than uniformly stretching, then apply via humanoid:ApplyDescription(desc). Capture the result filled solid black and confirm the archetype is nameable from the outline alone. State the archetype target AND the specific axis values you set.","addresses_criteria":["char-archetype-proportions-humanoiddescription-scaling"],"tools":["run_luau","capture"],"pitfalls":"ProportionScale does nothing while BodyTypeScale is 0; all five axes are R15-only and clamped 0-1, so a uniform whole-body stretch (no per-region intent) only earns partial and extreme silhouettes need custom MeshParts. Setting only a Color/shirt and never touching scale FAILS."},{"id":"char-base-2-custom-body-geometry","order_index":2,"instruction":"Commit to a CUSTOM body instead of the stock blocky R15, and author it to SHIP — not just to exist. (1) Generate the geometry the design needs: generate_mesh (Cube text-to-3D) for man-made limbs/heads/armor shells, author_mesh for organic creature forms, keeping distinct per-part shapes (never one merged blob). (2) Stand it on a spawn_rig R15 Motor6D rig; for a non-humanoid creature, build the part chain RootPart -> lower torso -> upper torso -> limbs and create each Motor6D with run_luau, setting Part0/Part1 and C0/C1 so every pivot sits at the real hinge. Give each animated part exactly ONE Motor6D (a part driven by two won't appear on the timeline), weld static cosmetic parts, name one part exactly 'Head', set a forward-facing PrimaryPart, and tune HipHeight so the rig neither slides nor floats. (3) Hold the geometry to the avatar STRUCTURAL CONTRACT + MESH HYGIENE so catalog clothing still fits and it deforms clean: trace the stock rig to keep the 15 MeshParts / R15 16-bone skeleton / WrapTarget cages / 19 standardized Attachments, cap skin weights at <=4 influences/vertex, seal watertight openings, check (don't flip) normals, add deformation loop-cuts at bending joints, and average seam normals. (4) Author it as SHIPPABLE: bake one material/atlas per mesh (no UDIM/per-part sprawl), keep each mesh under ~10k triangles with 2-4px UV-island padding and at least one LOD at ~25-50% tri count, and keep textures within 1024x1024. (5) Persist owned geometry with save_mesh (CreateAssetAsync) so it survives play/save/ship. (6) Pose-test the joints with pose_joint and capture to confirm clean, non-clipping deformation at every hinge BEFORE any look work. FAIL conditions: shipping the stock R15 body with no custom mesh; a part with multiple Motor6Ds; a backwards PrimaryPart; HipHeight left at 0; a merged-blob body; or authored geometry with no stated atlas/triangle/LOD budget gate.","addresses_criteria":["char-avatar-structural-contract-mesh-hygiene","char-custom-meshpart-nonhumanoid-rig-fidelity","char-performance-budget-atlas-lod"],"tools":["generate_mesh","author_mesh","spawn_rig","run_luau","pose_joint","save_mesh","capture"],"pitfalls":"Shipping the stock R15 body with no custom mesh FAILS the custom-body floor outright. A part driven by multiple Motor6Ds won't appear on the timeline; a backwards PrimaryPart walks the wrong way; HipHeight left at 0 slides/floats. Keep distinct per-part shapes (not one merged blob) and <=4 weight influences per vertex."},{"id":"char-base-3-themed-outfit-composition","order_index":3,"instruction":"Pick ONE theme/era/faction spine, then compose the outfit from at least two accessory classes on the custom body. With run_luau assemble a single typed list and apply atomically: desc:SetAccessories(specs); humanoid:ApplyDescription(desc), where each spec is {AssetId, AccessoryType=Enum.AccessoryType.<slot>, IsLayered, Order}. Use rigid hardpoint pieces (helmet/backpack/pauldrons, IsLayered=false) for crisp silhouette shapes AND layered/cage-driven clothing (IsLayered=true, ascending Order from skin outward) for body-conforming coverage. Author the character's own props as accessories with make_tool and seat them with grip_tool; parent custom worn meshes to an Accessory+Attachment whose name matches the body attachment (HatAttachment, NeckAttachment, RightShoulderAttachment, etc.). Put a pauldron on the shoulder attachment (moves with the arm), a cape on the collar (does not). Budget the count for an uncluttered silhouette and verify centered, non-clipping placement with capture_angles.","addresses_criteria":["char-themed-accessory-outfit-composition"],"tools":["run_luau","make_tool","grip_tool","generate_mesh","capture_angles"],"pitfalls":"An unthemed pile of random catalog hats, or a single flat shirt/pants, FAILS; a theme with only one accessory class earns partial. Wrong Order inverts the stack (sweater under shirt). Don't mix the legacy comma-string accessory API with SetAccessories. Layered clothing needs a cageable custom body or ApplyDescription may not wrap it."}]},{"tier":"good","goal":"The character's LOOK and identity are fully art-directed: a designed limited color palette with an accent and a value check, an intentional per-region PBR material hierarchy, authored layered clothing that conforms to the body, a high-fidelity custom rig, an authored 3D/material face, and a theme spine binding every choice — all driven by a HumanoidDescription variation SYSTEM rather than a hardcoded one-off.","steps":[{"id":"char-good-1-color-palette","order_index":1,"instruction":"Lock a constrained, harmonized palette BEFORE texturing: choose 3-5 colors (2-3 for a skin) with a named harmony (analogous or complementary) and a 60% dominant / 30% secondary / 10% accent weighting, placing the single accent as the focal point on the role-relevant zone (face/chest/weapon). With run_luau set the HumanoidDescription per-limb Color3 fields (HeadColor/TorsoColor/LeftArmColor/RightArmColor/LeftLegColor/RightLegColor) so they populate BodyColors on apply; for Parts, paint_texture a tiling procedural surface. Then VALIDATE the value structure: desaturate the character to ~25% under front/side/rim lighting (capture under varied Lighting) and confirm forms stay separated and the accent still reads as focal — adjust values until they do. State the palette, the harmony, the 60/30/10 split, AND the desaturation check.","addresses_criteria":["char-limited-harmonized-color-palette"],"tools":["run_luau","paint_texture","capture"],"pitfalls":"Default gray or six random saturated equal-weight limb colors is the canonical 'AI did it' look and FAILS; a limited palette with no value check (or a value check with no deliberate accent) earns partial. BodyColors only color stock body parts — colored MeshParts/SurfaceAppearance ignore them, so keep the mesh's baked color in the same palette. paint_texture rejects MeshParts."},{"id":"char-good-2-pbr-material-hierarchy","order_index":2,"instruction":"Give surfaces a per-region material hierarchy so metal vs rough cloth/canvas vs skin read as different physical materials. For each custom MeshPart region add a SurfaceAppearance child with create_instance and wire the four PBR maps with set_property: metal = MetalnessMap ~1.0 + low RoughnessMap; cloth/canvas = metalness 0 + high roughness + a NormalMap carrying weave/stitch; skin = mid roughness + metalness 0 + subtle normal. Use an OpenGL-tangent-space NormalMap, keep metalness binary (0 or 1, intermediate grays only for weathering), and one atlas/material per mesh. Use SurfaceAppearance.Color as a runtime-safe tint over a near-white ColorMap to make recolor variants for free. Verify >=2 distinct material reads under SEVERAL lighting environments (capture), not tuned to one scene.","addresses_criteria":["char-pbr-material-hierarchy-surfaceappearance","char-baked-light-stylized-texturing","char-performance-budget-atlas-lod"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property","run_luau","capture"],"pitfalls":"Uniform default Plastic (metalness 0 / roughness 0) on the whole body FAILS. SurfaceAppearance maps are NOT runtime-swappable (only Color tint is) — author the set up front. A flipped normal G-channel (DirectX vs OpenGL) inverts bumps; per-region variation with arbitrary non-physical metalness/roughness only earns partial. Bump roughness ~0.1 to compensate Fresnel across lighting."},{"id":"char-good-3-layered-clothing","order_index":3,"instruction":"Author at least one deformable layered (WrapLayer/cage-driven) garment that conforms to the body and stretches over any scale — not a flat 2D shirt/pants decal — and compose it into the outfit identity from the baseline step. Author the garment mesh with generate_mesh/author_mesh, bind it to the R15 skeleton (spawn_rig as the reference body), and apply it through the SetAccessories list with IsLayered=true and an explicit Order via run_luau. Commit to a fit/no-clip behavior: the inner cage controls fit tightness, the outer cage sits just outside along the normals (enabling Hidden Surface Removal so layers below are culled), edge loops stay even, and rigid spikes/pauldrons are left OUTSIDE the cage. Verify the garment conforms with no clipping across a scale change with capture_angles.","addresses_criteria":["char-layered-clothing-cage-authoring"],"tools":["generate_mesh","author_mesh","spawn_rig","run_luau","capture_angles"],"pitfalls":"No clothing, or a flat classic shirt/pants decal, FAILS; naming layered clothing with no conform/no-clip behavior earns partial. A cage tighter than the mesh clips; too loose balloons. Layered clothing won't wrap reliably on a non-caged blocky avatar — it needs the custom cageable body from baseline. Keep <=4 bone influences per vertex."},{"id":"char-good-4-custom-rig-fidelity","order_index":4,"instruction":"Take the baseline custom body to full rig fidelity where the design's identity needs non-stock geometry: confirm Motor6D on every animated part (welds on static), skin weighting with clean deformation, distinct per-part shapes, and no clipping/gaps at the joints. Concentrate joints in expressive appendages, model in a neutral pose, drag each pivot to its real hinge (run_luau editing C0/C1), keep <=4 joint influences per vertex, and tune Humanoid.HipHeight in a playtest so the character stands rather than slides/floats. For a skinned creature use a Custom Bone / AnimationController rig (Bone.Transform like Motor6D.Transform) with translation-based squash/stretch. Drive the rig into its extreme poses with pose_joint/ik_reach and capture_angles to prove no joint tears or gaps.","addresses_criteria":["char-custom-meshpart-nonhumanoid-rig-fidelity"],"tools":["spawn_rig","run_luau","pose_joint","ik_reach","capture_angles"],"pitfalls":"A pure stock-avatar build with no custom geometry FAILS (no longer partial-by-default). Bad weight painting deforms weirdly near joints; a wrong C0/C1 pivot leaves gaps; joint names in the mesh asset must match Bone names or skinning silently fails. Gesturing at custom rigs without the Motor6D/weighting or no-clip commitment earns partial."},{"id":"char-good-5-authored-face","order_index":5,"instruction":"Author the face/skin in the SAME dimensional language as the custom 3D head — not the default flat decal face. Either model the features as real geometry (e.g. the eye as a cylinder-over-sphere shaded Smooth) with generate_mesh, and/or give the skin an authored SurfaceAppearance/material that reads correctly (create_instance + set_property), so features share the head's 3D/material language. Push toward full marks by treating the face as an identity carrier: symmetric (X-mirrored) painting, a layered dark-to-bright iris, intentional grunge/scars/wrinkles, dark-shade shadows — built non-destructively (move existing verts only, edge flow on muscle groups, the four expression regions preserved). Capture the head close-up to confirm the face reads as 3D, consistent with the body.","addresses_criteria":["char-face-feature-dimensional-consistency"],"tools":["generate_mesh","create_instance","set_property","run_luau","capture"],"pitfalls":"Relying on the default Roblox decal face, or mixing a flat decal feature onto a 3D head, FAILS; authoring skin as a material but leaving features on a flat decal (mixed dimensional language) earns partial. paint_texture can't be used on the MeshPart head — author the skin via SurfaceAppearance instead."},{"id":"char-good-6-theme-spine","order_index":6,"instruction":"Make every garment, material, prop, and palette choice serve ONE coherent theme spine encoding the character's story (purpose / personality / history / faction / status) — e.g. pirate -> sea -> squid -> Davy Jones. Audit each element from the prior steps and cut or re-theme anything off-spine; choose materials and props to encode meaning (silk vs wool, rusted vs clean steel; a carried tool reads as profession). Position the design on deliberate axes (functional<->fantasy, historical<->fictional) from a two-column reference board (real-world fashion + aesthetic/feeling). Ensure the theme reads WITHOUT heavy lore (if it needs five layers of explanation it's incoherent) and place detail where the camera/medium sees it (top-down vs 360-camera — detail on the back too). Restraint can BE characterization (a muted palette = coldness). Capture from the gameplay camera angle to confirm the read.","addresses_criteria":["char-theme-spine-costume-storytelling"],"tools":["run_luau","capture","capture_angles"],"pitfalls":"A pile of unrelated 'cool' elements, or a design needing five layers of lore, FAILS; a loose theme with some off-theme elements, or detail placed with no medium awareness, earns partial. Don't lavish detail on the front only if the game has a 360 camera."},{"id":"char-good-7-variation-system","order_index":7,"instruction":"Move the WHOLE identity into one HumanoidDescription payload (scales + BodyColors + Shirt/Pants/ShirtGraphic + rigid SetAccessories + layered accessories + body-part mesh IDs + face) and drive variation from it as a SYSTEM. With run_luau author a base description once (humanoid:GetAppliedDescription(), Players:GetHumanoidDescriptionFromUserIdAsync wrapped in pcall, or Instance.new), then desc:Clone() PER character and mutate before apply — randomize TorsoColor/HeadColor from the locked palette pool, pick one of N accessory sets, jitter HeightScale/WidthScale — calling humanoid:ApplyDescription(clone) on each. Generate the NPC population with make_npc and apply a mutated clone per spawn (batch/stagger spawns; ApplyDescription yields and rebuilds the character). Capture a small crowd to prove multiple DISTINCT looks come from the one system. For full marks add reusable skin-tone handling (texture opacity x MeshPart.Color or Overlay AlphaMode, verified across several tones).","addresses_criteria":["char-humanoiddescription-variation-system"],"tools":["run_luau","make_npc","capture","capture_angles"],"pitfalls":"Hardcoding one look (a Color/shirt set the same on every character) FAILS; a HumanoidDescription applied as only ONE look earns partial. Mutating in place without Clone() makes all 'varied' NPCs share one record. GetHumanoidDescriptionFromUserIdAsync is a throttling web call — pcall + cache it. Applying to a huge crowd in one frame hitches."}]},{"tier":"exceptional","goal":"Top-studio craft layered on the designed character: a per-archetype-DISTINCT silhouette validated at distance and across poses, a governing shape-language hierarchy, an art-directed surface/baked-light treatment, a single deliberate focal point against quieter surroundings, and ONE shared identity system that locks the entire cast into one world.","steps":[{"id":"char-exc-1-distinct-silhouette","order_index":1,"instruction":"Make the OUTER SHAPE a per-archetype-distinct identity carrier so the solid-black outline reads the specific character/archetype and is distinguishable from the rest of the cast before any texture. Tune proportions/forms per archetype (reuse and push the scale presets from baseline; for shapes the axes can't reach, author custom MeshParts with generate_mesh/author_mesh). Then VALIDATE with explicit readability tests the design is stated to pass: a multi-distance black-silhouette check via capture at ~5m / ~15m / ~50-100m; a multi-pose & multi-angle recognition check (idle/attack/traversal/hit/victory) via capture_angles + pose_joint; and a grayscale/blur value-collapse check. Render the cast's archetypes side by side and confirm their outlines are mutually distinguishable. State which tests it passes.","addresses_criteria":["char-silhouette-distance-and-pose-readability"],"tools":["run_luau","pose_joint","capture","capture_angles"],"pitfalls":"The default R15 body, or any cast that's shape-identical and told apart only by close-up texture/color, FAILS. A generically asserted 'strong silhouette' tied to no per-archetype distinctness and no readability test earns partial. Bulky clipping accessories can erase the very proportion contrast the scale axes created — re-check the black outline with the outfit on."},{"id":"char-exc-2-shape-language-system","order_index":2,"instruction":"Govern the design with an explicit shape-language hierarchy rather than an additive parts-pile. Name the DOMINANT shape mapped to personality (round=friendly, angular=dangerous, square=stable) that the outline broadcasts per archetype, a subordinate/secondary accent shape under a clear hierarchy, and a FORBIDDEN shape that would break the read. For creatures, give each appendage its own readable concept-driven shape (author distinct forms with author_mesh/generate_mesh and pose-test with pose_joint). Tie this back to the silhouette so the dominant shape is what the black outline shows. Capture to confirm the dominant shape dominates and no forbidden shape has crept in via an accessory.","addresses_criteria":["char-shape-language-system"],"tools":["author_mesh","generate_mesh","pose_joint","run_luau","capture"],"pitfalls":"The stock blocky avatar, or an additive catalog-parts pile with no governing shape logic, FAILS; mentioning proportions/shapes loosely with no archetype/role intent earns partial. An equal-weight mix of competing shapes reads as noise — enforce the dominant/subordinate hierarchy."},{"id":"char-exc-3-authored-surface-treatment","order_index":3,"instruction":"Art-direct how the surface READS with an authored treatment, beyond just correct PBR. Either deepen the SurfaceAppearance maps so cloth/metal/skin form is carried by the maps (from the good-tier material step), OR bake a stylized light story into the albedo — hand-paint unlit AO/shadows (cool in shadow, warm in crevices), use a tiny shared gradient atlas with deliberate dark/light-band UV placement, block value-first in black-and-white before hue, repaint over the baker, add colored AO and features drawn into the texture. Apply it consistently with seam/bleed padding so UV islands don't show. Author the maps/atlas with generate_mesh's texturing and wire via create_instance/set_property; verify under varied lighting with capture that the authored read holds rather than collapsing to the default-lit flat look.","addresses_criteria":["char-baked-light-stylized-texturing"],"tools":["generate_mesh","create_instance","set_property","run_luau","capture"],"pitfalls":"A flat-shaded body under default real-time lighting, or a default decal face, FAILS; naming authored materials with no deliberate read-control (no per-material correctness, no baked/value/AO technique) earns partial. Forgetting seam/bleed padding makes UV islands show as seams."},{"id":"char-exc-4-focal-point","order_index":4,"instruction":"Designate ONE deliberate focal point and make it win. Pick a single accent (the 10% accent color from the palette, an emissive/high-contrast feature, or a concentrated detail zone) positioned where the eye should land, and keep the surroundings comparatively quiet. For an energy/tech character add a separate self-illuminating neon MeshPart plus a small dedicated light part at the point of contrast (create_instance + set_property). Build a full detail-density hierarchy for full marks: dense detail in the focal zone, low-density eye-rest areas elsewhere, small details reinforcing (not fighting) the major silhouette, dark masses balanced against larger light areas, and a repeated motif deliberately broken at the focal point. Capture and confirm the eye lands on the one focal point, not several competing ones.","addresses_criteria":["char-detail-density-focal-hierarchy"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property","run_luau","capture"],"pitfalls":"The uniform stock look with no focal point, or uniform detail/texture spread everywhere (which flattens hierarchy just as badly), FAILS; an accent contradicted by competing emphasis (no single dominant point) earns partial."},{"id":"char-exc-5-cast-cohesion-style-lock","order_index":5,"instruction":"Lock the whole cast to ONE shared identity system so it reads as one world. Drive every character from the good-tier HumanoidDescription/ApplyDescription design language (one source of truth generating the NPC crowd/population via make_npc + a mutated clone per spawn) AND commit one cast-wide treatment — a shared palette + material/rendering language every character is built to. For full marks anchor with a locked hero/anchor asset that fixes numeric ratios (head-to-body, limb-to-torso, shoulder breadth), a polycount/silhouette-tier and texel-density budget, and render each new character side-by-side with the hero at MATCHED lighting at every milestone (capture) to catch drift; build a concept-art-driven signature silhouette family. Capture the assembled cast to confirm cohesion plus per-archetype distinctness coexist.","addresses_criteria":["char-cast-cohesion-hero-asset-style-lock"],"tools":["run_luau","make_npc","capture","capture_angles"],"pitfalls":"Unmanaged one-off characters that drift, or the same stock look duplicated on everyone, FAILS; driving variation from one system but committing no cast-wide treatment (or committing a treatment but letting the system/ratios drift) earns partial. Render at matched lighting/graphics or drift checks give false readings."}]}],"provenance":{"sources":["https://create.roblox.com/docs/art/accessories/body-scale","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/characters/appearance.md","https://create.roblox.com/docs/reference/engine/classes/HumanoidDescription","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/characters/appearance.md","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/characters/appearance.md","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/art/characters/index.md","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/art/modeling/rig-a-humanoid-model.md","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/art/accessories/creating/caging-setup.md","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/art/accessories/layered-clothing.md","https://create.roblox.com/docs/reference/engine/classes/Attachment","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/art/modeling/surface-appearance.md","https://create.roblox.com/docs/characters/adaptive-animation","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/avatar/index.md","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-do-people-create-amazing-character-designs/3248648","https://create.roblox.com/docs/reference/engine/classes/HumanoidDescription","https://medium.com/@xavierck/character-readability-in-team-fortress-2-and-overwatch-68c41d454465","https://create.roblox.com/docs/en-us/reference/engine/classes/HumanoidDescription.md","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/art/accessories/layered-clothing.md","https://medium.com/@xavierck/character-readability-in-team-fortress-2-and-overwatch-68c41d454465","https://create.roblox.com/docs/art/modeling/surface-appearance","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/skinneddeformation-rigs-your-pro-guide-rewrite/1514277","https://create.roblox.com/docs/en-us/reference/engine/classes/HumanoidDescription.md","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/why-this-build-looks-bad/3117431 ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-can-i-improve-my-build/815013","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/why-this-build-looks-bad/3117431 ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-can-i-improve-my-build/815013","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/why-are-my-models-so-bad/2516105 ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/why-this-build-looks-bad/3117431 ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-can-i-improve-my-build/815013","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/why-is-my-lighting-so-bad/3145846 ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/why-this-build-looks-bad/3117431","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/why-my-games-look-bad-or-lame/2852076","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/why-my-games-look-bad-or-lame/2852076","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/why-do-my-animations-look-stiff/2947662 ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/animations-are-stiff-without-anything-overlapping-them/2946026","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/animations-are-stiff-without-anything-overlapping-them/2946026","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/does-my-main-character-look-okay/1827023","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/why-are-my-models-so-bad/2516105 ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/need-feedback-on-a-model/3310341","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/why-this-build-looks-bad/3117431 ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/why-are-my-models-so-bad/2516105 ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/does-my-main-character-look-okay/1827023","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/does-my-main-character-look-okay/1827023 ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/why-is-my-lighting-so-bad/3145846","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/feedback-on-character-design/3876966 ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/need-feedback-on-a-model/3310341 ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/need-feedback-on-a-model/3310341","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x2MFFBvIF6Y","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2KdzgpHIA6s","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x2MFFBvIF6Y","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2KdzgpHIA6s","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x2MFFBvIF6Y","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6YlWYGVo7KI","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R5KOQbz2rsI","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R5KOQbz2rsI","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Fsj-y9NHOgo","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fW2mpmO2XHY","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pVD-HBlfv4g","https://create.roblox.com/docs/reference/engine/classes/Humanoid#ApplyDescriptionAsync","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3oL5NLigSc4"],"confidence":"high","coverage":{"criteria_total":16,"criteria_addressed":16,"uncovered":[]}}}]==],
	-- combat.guide.json
	[==[{"module":"combat","system_module":"combat","version":"1.0","rubric_version":"1.0","overview":"Great Roblox combat is a TIMING + FEEDBACK system, not a pile of attack clips. Everything hangs off one spine: named animation event markers (HitStart/HitEnd/CancelOpen/Contact) read via AnimationTrack:GetMarkerReachedSignal — so the hitbox, VFX, SFX, hitstop, knockback, and cancel windows all fire off the SAME frame the blade visibly connects, and stay in sync even when you AdjustSpeed for hitstop. Build in this order: (1) rig + a combat STANCE state machine (non-combat<->combat) with a complete clip set; (2) per-attack anticipation->contact->follow-through animations at Action priority, authored upper-body-only so they layer over locomotion; (3) marker-driven active-frame hitboxes (swept, not .Touched) detected client-side for feel and re-validated server-side for authority; (4) the full impact stack on the contact marker — layered audio, camera-facing VFX burst, strength-scaled hitstop, directional flinch, hitstun + force-coupled knockback; (5) a cancelable combo state machine with input buffering. The exceptional tier is reached by routing every feedback channel through ONE strength-scaled impact bus, grading reactions by direction+force (poise), adding a defensive/parry counter-rhythm, weaving knockback into combo spacing, and tying server validation to the marker contact frame. The agent's stock tools (make_tool, grip_tool, bind_animation, bind_behavior, bind_hitbox, make_npc) cover the skeleton, but bind_behavior/bind_hitbox use TIMER windows + radius sweeps — to clear the rubric you replace those timing/detection internals with marker-driven, swept versions authored via add_keyframe events and run_luau.","tiers":[{"tier":"baseline","goal":"Stand up a rigged fighter with a real combat STANCE state machine, a complete clip set, attacks/reactions/dodges/locomotion that coexist by priority, marker-driven event timing, an active-frame (not .Touched) swept hitbox, and layered role-aware impact audio whose connect sound fires on the contact marker.","steps":[{"id":"b1","order_index":1,"instruction":"Build the fighter on an R15 + Motor6D rig FIRST, before any combat code. Use spawn_rig to drop a clean R15 block rig (15 Motor6D-joined joints — full articulation for believable melee). If the build needs the player avatar, force the classic Motor6D path: under the Avatar Joint Upgrade an R15 char spawns with AnimationConstraints+BallSocketConstraints and ZERO Motor6Ds, which silently ignore the Motor6D animation channel your clips drive. Verify with run_luau that the rig has Motor6D joints (not WeldConstraints/AnimationConstraints) before posing anything.","tools":["spawn_rig","run_luau","create_instance"],"addresses_criteria":["ikcontrol-reactive-limb-blending","procedural-foot-planting-ik","animationconstraint-physical-secondary-motion"],"pitfalls":"The Avatar Joint Upgrade rig and a WeldConstraint rig look identical in the viewport but the animation channel shows NO keyframe tracks / a stuck T-pose because the limbs are not Motor6D-joined. Probe joint types with run_luau and convert to a classic Motor6D rig before wasting time animating."},{"id":"b2","order_index":2,"instruction":"Author the COMBAT IDLE as a first-class held ARMED stance and make it the BASE of an explicit non-combat<->combat state seam — not a flat attack-clip list, and not generic locomotion authoring. (1) With add_keyframe author a dedicated combat idle: a settled held guard/fighting stance (bent weapon-side elbow, weapon up across the body, weight forward) authored as its own clip, plus a subtle breathing/weight-shift keypose so it reads as a LIVING guard, not a frozen T-pose; copy keyframe 0 to the end so it loops seamlessly. This clip is the v=0 ARMED base state. (2) Author the attack swing and the hit-react clips arm-scoped (add_keyframe {scope='rightArm'} / 'upperBody') so they override ONLY the keyed arm/upper-body joints at a higher priority and ride ON TOP of the held idle and the lower-priority locomotion — do NOT re-author the walk/run here (locomotion is owned by the locomotion module and is the generic floor); reference it only as the lower-priority Movement layer the combat idle composes over. (3) Make weapon draw the explicit entry into the armed stance: make_tool + grip_tool so the weapon sits right in the fist, then commit the stance TRANSITION as the load-bearing seam — drawing/equipping the tool ENTERS the combat idle (it plays and PERSISTS as the looped base layer between swings), and sheathing/unequipping EXITS it (returns to the non-combat default). State that the combat idle is the base ARMED state that every swing/hit-react overrides on the keyed joints and returns to, so the fighter never snaps from 'fighting' to 'standing straight'. Reject your own output if it is only a list of attack clips with no persistent held combat idle, if it re-authors generic walk/run as the deliverable, or if it grips a tool without committing the draw-enters-stance / sheath-exits-stance transition. Verify the held stance reads as a guard and persists between swings (capture/playtest the equip-hold-swing-return loop), not just that the swing plays.","tools":["add_keyframe","make_tool","grip_tool","spawn_rig"],"addresses_criteria":["complete-combat-clip-set"],"pitfalls":"Shipping only attack clips with no dedicated combat idle makes the fighter snap from 'fighting' to 'standing straight' between swings — a jarring disconnect. Hold the combat idle as the base state; the rubric wants an explicit non-combat<->combat stance, not a clip list."},{"id":"b3","order_index":3,"instruction":"Wire the non-combat<->combat STANCE STATE MACHINE that ENTERS/EXITS on a signal. Use bind_behavior to give the tool the equipped->idle / activate->windup->active->cooldown->idle timed machine, and drive the combat-stance entry off weapon draw (Tool.Equipped / target acquired / first input). bind_behavior exposes the ItemState attribute + StateChanged BindableEvent — that is your combat state channel. With run_luau, gate attacks so they can't start unless in combat stance and not Stunned/mid-recovery.","tools":["bind_behavior","run_luau","make_tool"],"addresses_criteria":["complete-combat-clip-set","weighted-behavior-brain-enemies","block-parry-defensive-layer"],"pitfalls":"bind_behavior's default windup/active/cooldown are TIMER-based, not marker-based — fine for the stance skeleton, but for combat FEEL you will replace the active window with a marker-driven one in the Good tier (step g3). Re-activation while busy must be ignored (the cooldown anti-spam gate) or the machine desyncs."},{"id":"b4","order_index":4,"instruction":"Bake NAMED EVENT MARKERS into every combat clip and make ALL gameplay events fire off them — never off wait()/task.delay/track.Length. Author markers at the exact frames: 'HitStart' where the blade begins cutting, 'HitEnd' where it leaves the arc, 'Contact' at the single impact frame, 'CancelOpen' where the next combo step may chain. At runtime connect track:GetMarkerReachedSignal('HitStart'/'HitEnd'/'Contact'/'CancelOpen') via run_luau. Markers stay synced even when you AdjustSpeed (hitstop), making the animation the single source of truth for timing.","tools":["add_keyframe","bind_animation","run_luau"],"addresses_criteria":["marker-driven-event-timing","swept-active-frame-hitbox-no-touched"],"pitfalls":"GetMarkerReachedSignal only fires on the SAME peer and the SAME AnimationTrack instance that actually plays the clip — it does NOT fire on the server for a client-started track. Connect on whichever peer owns the playing track; do active-frame detection on the client that plays the anim and validate the hit on the server."},{"id":"b5","order_index":5,"instruction":"Commit hit detection to an ACTIVE-FRAME SWEPT model gated by HitStart/HitEnd — never .Touched or one static part. Replace bind_hitbox's radius-sweep internals (or wrap them) via run_luau: clear an 'alreadyHit' set on the HitStart marker, then EACH active frame between HitStart and HitEnd run a swept query — for a blade, place Attachments ~1 stud apart along it and raycast between each attachment's previous-frame and current-frame position; for wide sweeps call workspace:GetPartBoundsInBox/GetPartsInPart with OverlapParams filtered to character hitboxes. The volume is LIVE only inside the narrow window and DEAD during windup/recovery.","tools":["bind_hitbox","run_luau","create_instance","set_property"],"addresses_criteria":["swept-active-frame-hitbox-no-touched","marker-driven-event-timing","data-driven-attacks-collider-groups"],"pitfalls":"A single .Touched part or one raycast per frame TUNNELS through targets on fast swings (the classic 'my sword passed through them'). Sweep between previous-and-current frame positions. Forgetting the per-swing alreadyHit set ticks damage every frame the box overlaps; always filter out the attacker's own character or they hit themselves."},{"id":"b6","order_index":6,"instruction":"Make four combat animation tracks COEXIST via deliberate per-clip AnimationPriority and partial-rig weight blending, then VERIFY coexistence — do not stop at assigning numbers. (1) Assign priority per clip: attack=Action4, victim hit-react=Action3 (so a flinch does NOT cancel the attacker's in-progress swing), dodge=Action2, locomotion=Movement. (2) Author every attack with add_keyframe scoped to UPPER-BODY joints ONLY — zero leg/lower-torso keyframes — so the Movement walk keeps driving the legs through per-joint weight-normalized blending; blend the attack over locomotion via AdjustWeight and crossfade idle<->attack with a non-zero fadeTime on Play/Stop (no popping). (3) Set priority on the AnimationTrack at runtime via run_luau (or bake it into the clip) — NOT on the Animation instance, which won't apply. (4) VERIFY the two coexistence outcomes, don't assume them: while the attack plays, the character must keep walking (no moonwalk/T-slide, legs still driven by the walk), and a victim hit-react at Action3 must visibly play WITHOUT cutting the attacker's Action4 swing short. Scope honesty: this step delivers concurrent multi-track blending only; it does NOT author IKControl, per-foot raycasts, contact-point hand snapping, or surface-normal foot planting, so it must NOT claim the exceptional IK criteria — it provides only the live locomotion-driven lower-body substrate those would later build on. REJECT as generic if the build merely plays one full-body attack clip, leaves any priority at default, includes leg keyframes in the attack, sets priority on the Animation instance, or skips the move-while-swinging / flinch-doesn't-cancel verification.","tools":["add_keyframe","bind_animation","run_luau"],"addresses_criteria":["animation-priority-layering-coexist","attack-layered-over-locomotion","ikcontrol-reactive-limb-blending","procedural-foot-planting-ik"],"pitfalls":"If the attack clip has leg keyframes, an Action attack fully overrides the walk and the character moonwalks/T-slides while attacking — strip lower-body keyframes. Setting priority too low (Movement or below) lets the walk override the attack ('it still shows walking'). Setting priority on the Animation instance alone won't apply — set it on the AnimationTrack."},{"id":"b7","order_index":7,"instruction":"Layer ROLE-AWARE impact audio and fire the CONNECT sound on the contact-frame marker. Author three sound roles via create_instance/set_property: a whoosh on swing start, a distinct thud/crunch/clang on connect, and a recognizable consistent parry/block cue. Play the whoosh when the attack fires, but trigger the connect sound from the 'Contact' (or HitStart) GetMarkerReachedSignal — clone it onto the victim HRP, Play, Debris-clean. Randomize PlaybackSpeed +/- a few percent so repeats don't grate; match pitch to weight (bright crack = light, bass thud = heavy).","tools":["run_luau","create_instance","set_property"],"addresses_criteria":["layered-impact-sound-on-contact","marker-driven-event-timing"],"pitfalls":"Firing the connect sound on swing START (or a fixed wait) makes it land before the blade visibly connects — instantly reads as broken. Drive it off the contact marker. A single un-varied sound becomes grating fast; vary PlaybackSpeed. Sound is the highest-leverage feel investment — do not skimp."}]},{"tier":"good","goal":"Make every strike read as WEIGHTED and FAIR: timing-asymmetry skeleton with strength-scaled hitstop, whole-body weight transfer, per-attack eased pacing, directional additive flinches, real hitstun + coherent two-party knockback, a marker-synced multi-channel impact FX stack, a connected/cancelable combo with input buffering, and responsive-local/authoritative-driven hit registration.","steps":[{"id":"g1","order_index":1,"instruction":"Author each attack on a DELIBERATE timing-asymmetry skeleton with WHOLE-BODY weight transfer. Using add_keyframe build 4 phase poses: (1) a held/LOADED wind-up that draws back and accelerates from near-still, (2) a single readable peak CONTACT pose with the weapon fully extended, (3) follow-through that drags across and down, (4) a settle. Crucially the strike is DRIVEN BY THE BODY: key the torso/hip to lead with the weapon arm dragging BEHIND (overlap/drag), over-rotate slightly past the arm then re-center. Plant the stance leg (rotation not translation) so only the striking limb carries the arc.","tools":["add_keyframe","pose_joint","capture_animation"],"addresses_criteria":["anticipation-load-and-impact-hitstop","weight-cues-body-drive-drag-followthrough"],"pitfalls":"Punching/swinging 'with no load' has no power; symmetric subtle bouncy motion 'looks like a workout routine'. Arm-only motion with a gliding stance leg makes the character float — lead with the body and lock the stance leg. Capture mid-pose with capture_animation to verify the contact pose is a single readable silhouette."},{"id":"g2","order_index":2,"instruction":"Shape pacing on TWO axes, not one fixed swing reused everywhere. (1) PER-PHASE EASING: set per-keyframe easing within each swing — ease the loaded wind-up IN (accelerate from near-still), make contact CRISP (sharp ease-out into the extension frame so it snaps, near-linear through the contact window), and ease the recovery OUT (soft settle) — never one uniform/default curve. (2) PER-WEAPON MASS: swing speed must read from weapon weight — give heavier weapons a materially longer frame budget than lighter ones (e.g. greatsword ~12-24 frames vs dagger ~6-12, roughly 2x span), so a heavy weapon visibly swings slower/with more commitment, NOT one swing duration cloned across all weapons. Additionally vary duration/easing per attack WITHIN a combo so the chain has contrast (fast jabs vs a heavy committed swing) and escalates. Most light melee swings are ~0.4-0.7s; heavy weapons run longer. Capture and confirm both: the phases are eased (not linear) AND a heavier weapon's swing spans noticeably more frames than a lighter one.","tools":["add_keyframe","capture_animation"],"addresses_criteria":["per-weapon-easing-and-pacing"],"pitfalls":"Reusing one symmetric linear swing everywhere reads robotic and weightless. The rubric explicitly rejects (1) un-eased timing and (2) the same swing on every attack — vary easing AND silhouette per clip. Sharp-in/soft-out feels weightiest."},{"id":"g3","order_index":3,"instruction":"On a confirmed hit, fire a SINGLE impact trigger off the Contact marker via RemoteEvent:FireAllClients carrying the attack-strength scalar (weight) — never a one-client-only freeze. On EACH client, derive a weight-SCALED hitstop frame count from that scalar (light jab ~1-2 frames / ~0.05s, heavy finisher ~3-4 frames / ~0.12s) and, in a RunService.Heartbeat loop, ramp BOTH the attacker swing track AND the victim hit-react track via AnimationTrack:AdjustSpeed from 1 -> near-0 -> 1 over a smooth curve DIP (NOT AdjustSpeed(0), which hard-pauses and reads as a stutter) across that scaled duration. Each client only AdjustSpeeds the tracks IT owns (attacker's client ramps the attacker track, victim's client ramps the victim track) — replicate the trigger, never AdjustSpeed a track you don't own. CRUCIALLY keep particle emitters, slash decals, and camera shake running at speed 1 throughout so the freeze reads as IMPACT, not a frame-drop/lag spike — freezing animation + VFX + camera together is the failure mode. The result: every client sees the same weight-scaled freeze synchronized to the contact frame, scaling continuously from a light jab to a heavy finisher, as a dedicated felt-impact/emphasis layer distinct from cosmetic animation.","tools":["run_luau"],"addresses_criteria":["anticipation-load-and-impact-hitstop","dynamic-combat-camera-framing"],"pitfalls":"Freezing EVERYTHING at once (animation + particles + camera) feels like a frame-drop/lag spike, not a hit — keep VFX and camera live. AdjustSpeed(0) hard-pauses; a curve DIP reads as weight. Apply hitstop only on the client that owns the animation; replicate the trigger, don't AdjustSpeed a track you don't own."},{"id":"g4","order_index":4,"instruction":"Author a directional ADDITIVE hit-reaction that adapts to the live fight, not a canned flinch. (1) DIRECTIONAL FLINCH: at runtime compute the hit direction with victimHRP.CFrame:VectorToObjectSpace(attackerHRP.Position - victimHRP.Position) (or the hit normal) and select among front/side/back flinch variants authored via add_keyframe {scope='upperBody'/'rightArm'} so the silhouette snaps AWAY from the attack. Play it at Action3 so it layers ADDITIVELY over the victim's current locomotion/attack and does NOT cancel the attacker's swing; keep it brief (~0.3-0.5s) and interruptible; crossfade in/out via the fadeTime on Play/Stop so it never pops. (2) GRADE IT (escape a single-severity flinch): branch the reaction by hit FORCE as well as direction — a light glancing hit blends a small stumble that coexists with locomotion, a heavy hit plays a fuller stagger — so light and heavy hits do not look identical. (3) HEAD-GAZE LOCK (to truly earn head-gaze-targeting): add a LookAt-type IKControl on the head/aim chain targeting the live opponent, tweening Weight to full while in combat and easing it toward zero on the contact/recovery frames and when there is no target, so a single clip stays oriented to the opponent at any relative angle through the flinch. (4) Do NOT claim contextual-special-attack-geometry from this step unless you actually anchor the reaction to the real contact point (e.g. a marker-synced contact-located impact accent / origin-correct hit spark at the strike position) rather than a character-centered flinch — otherwise drop that criterion from this step. PITFALLS: a direction-blind canned flinch ignores who hit whom (derive the variant from the actual hit vector every hit); loading the flinch at attack priority cancels the attacker's swing (keep it Action3); a single invariant flinch regardless of force is the generic tell (grade by force); a head that merely rides the torso fails head-gaze (use the weighted IKControl); over-exaggerating the stagger looks comical (dial it back).","tools":["add_keyframe","bind_animation","run_luau"],"addresses_criteria":["directional-additive-hit-reaction","head-gaze-targeting","contextual-special-attack-geometry"],"pitfalls":"A direction-blind canned flinch ignores who hit whom — derive the variant from the actual hit vector every hit. Loading the flinch at attack priority can cancel the attacker's swing — keep it Action3. Over-exaggerating the stagger looks comical; dial it back."},{"id":"g5","order_index":5,"instruction":"Impose real COMMITMENT with hitstun + force-coupled consequence on BOTH parties, server-side. On a confirmed server hit: SAVE the victim's WalkSpeed/JumpPower, set both to 0 for a stun window, set character:SetAttribute('Stunned',true), restore the SAVED values when it ends (refresh-by-longest if a new stun overlaps, never un-stun early). Drop the ATTACKER's WalkSpeed to ~4-5 during their own swing so neither party slides freely. Other systems read GetAttributeChangedSignal('Stunned') to block actions.","tools":["run_luau","bind_hitbox"],"addresses_criteria":["hitstun-and-walkspeed-commitment","weighted-behavior-brain-enemies"],"pitfalls":"Hardcoding the restore value (WalkSpeed=16) breaks custom-movement games — save real values first. If the server script dies before the timer fires the victim is stunned forever; guard it. Overlapping stuns that each restore on their own timer un-stun early — refresh by longest remaining time. A cosmetic-only flinch the victim can mash through does NOT satisfy this criterion."},{"id":"g6","order_index":6,"instruction":"Move BOTH parties coherently with a modern force mover. On hit, compute direction PER HIT from the CFrames (attacker LookVector or (victimRoot-attackerRoot).Unit, optional +Y pop) and push the victim back along the hit vector via BasePart:ApplyImpulse (accounts for AssemblyMass) OR a short-lived LinearVelocity + Attachment (VectorVelocity=dir*speed, Debris:AddItem after ~0.15s). Advance the ATTACKER slightly forward (~7-10) to stay in combo range. Apply the impulse SERVER-side on the server character for authority; bound the magnitude.","tools":["run_luau","bind_hitbox","create_instance"],"addresses_criteria":["coherent-knockback-modern-mover","hitstun-and-walkspeed-commitment"],"pitfalls":"Using AssemblyLinearVelocity or a fixed world-space vector makes knockback ALWAYS go the same direction regardless of who hit whom — derive direction from the CFrames every hit. A LinearVelocity left attached sticks the victim moving forever — Debris-remove it. VectorForce is continuous (bad for a discrete pop); prefer ApplyImpulse or a short LinearVelocity. BodyVelocity works but is deprecated."},{"id":"g7","order_index":7,"instruction":"Fire a MARKER-SYNCED multi-channel impact FX stack at the single Contact marker, client-side-immediately. Keep a VFX attachment of ParticleEmitters (all Enabled=false so they don't leak) in ReplicatedStorage; on the Contact marker clone it to the victim HRP, then for each child emitter call :Emit(emitter:GetAttribute('EmitCount')) and Debris-clean. Spawn a one-shot camera-facing directional burst (debris/slash-decal) at the EXACT contact point, plus a SUBTLE spring-based camera shake (low amplitude, player-toggleable). Match VFX type to impact (debris/blood for melee, not smoke). Run all of it on the client (FireAllClients for shared visibility).","tools":["run_luau","create_instance","set_property"],"addresses_criteria":["marker-synced-impact-fx-stack","contextual-special-attack-geometry","dynamic-combat-camera-framing"],"pitfalls":"Leaving emitters Enabled=true double-emits (constant stream + burst). VFX spawned before the animation reaches the target (desynced, off a swing-start timer) instantly reads as broken — drive it off the contact marker. Too much camera shake is nauseating; keep it subtle and toggleable. Server-driven VFX is delayed by ping — run it client-side."},{"id":"g8","order_index":8,"instruction":"Build the M1 combo as a CONNECTED pose-graph, not N disjoint swings. For each adjacent clip pair (hit1->hit2->hit3->...), explicitly add_keyframe the FINAL keypose of clip N to be IDENTICAL (same joint CFrames) to the OPENING keypose of clip N+1, so the seam has zero snap/teleport — then PROVE it: capture_animation a filmstrip spanning the last ~0.1s of clip N and the first ~0.1s of clip N+1 and confirm the silhouette is continuous across the cut (re-key and re-capture until it is). Preserve genuine distinctness: each attack must still own a different silhouette and momentum direction at its mid-pose (horizontal slash vs overhead vs thrust), so continuity is achieved by matching only the handoff poses, never by homogenizing the swings. Author all attack clips arm-scoped (add_keyframe {scope='rightArm'}) and bind them at Action priority so the combo LAYERS over walk/run locomotion (legs/torso keep their own animation) rather than freezing the lower body. Drive chain advance + reset through the behavior state machine's active/cooldown windows (bind_behavior), and define a DEDICATED combat-ready idle clip (distinct from the default standing idle) that the chain returns to between combos. For the idle<->attack<->idle transitions, do NOT pop: route playback through a runtime player that crossfades with a ~0.1s fade on track Play/Stop — and because the stock bind_animation/bind_behavior runtimes call Play()/Stop() with no fade, either extend that runtime to thread a fadeTime through its play() helper or document the exact runtime change required, so the crossfade is a real implemented seam and not an unbacked parameter reference.","tools":["add_keyframe","bind_animation","capture_animation"],"addresses_criteria":["connected-combo-chain-flowing-poses","attack-layered-over-locomotion"],"pitfalls":"Playing N clips back-to-back without matching end-pose to next-start-pose makes the character snap/teleport between hits. Don't sacrifice distinctness for continuity — each attack still needs its own silhouette and momentum. Snapping back to the default idle between chains breaks flow."},{"id":"g9","order_index":9,"instruction":"Build the COMBO STATE MACHINE with input buffering, a marker-defined cancel window, AND a dual combo-index reset. Track comboIndex + lastAttackTime + exactly ONE buffered queued press. Drive transitions through a small state machine (Idle/Windup/Active/Recovery/Stunned) so attacks cannot start while Stunned or mid-recovery. Define the cancel window via the 'CancelOpen' marker consumed through track:GetMarkerReachedSignal('CancelOpen') — a press during an active swing is STORED (buffer exactly one; clear the queue on every state change so a held key cannot auto-mash and no phantom extra attack fires) and auto-fired the instant CancelOpen fires, so players chain without frame-perfect timing. A debounce prevents an in-progress M1 from being canceled. The combo index must have a DUAL reset, BOTH paths required: (A) reset to 0/1 after the FINISHER attack with an explicit end-of-combo cooldown before the chain can restart, AND (B) on idle timeout — implemented as a task.spawn watcher that snapshots the current m1-count, waits ~0.8-1.2s, and resets the index ONLY if the captured count is unchanged (so a press landing inside the window does not wrongly reset). Do NOT gate any of this — cancel window, idle reset, or recovery — off wait()/task.delay or track.Length math; all timing-critical events must read from animation markers. Pitfall: a finisher-only OR idle-only reset is incomplete — without the finisher+cooldown reset the chain never cleanly ends, and without the count-snapshot idle watcher the index either never returns to the opener or resets mid-chain.","tools":["run_luau","bind_behavior","add_keyframe"],"addresses_criteria":["combo-state-machine-with-input-buffering","connected-combo-chain-flowing-poses","block-parry-defensive-layer","weighted-behavior-brain-enemies","data-driven-attacks-collider-groups"],"pitfalls":"Without a cancel window the combo only advances on frame-perfect input and feels stiff; without an idle-reset timeout the index never returns to 1 so players start mid-combo. Buffer exactly ONE input — buffering many lets a held key auto-mash. Clear the queue on state change or you get phantom extra attacks."},{"id":"g10","order_index":10,"instruction":"Commit hit registration to the RESPONSIVE-LOCAL / AUTHORITATIVE-DRIVEN model. The CLIENT plays the attack track + VFX + hitstop the instant the swing connects on its own frame (zero-latency feel) — never on button-press, never after a server round-trip. The client fires a RemoteEvent that is an INTENT signal only ('I started attack N'), never a hit claim with damage/positions. The SERVER re-runs its own marker/active-frame hitbox from server physics, runs plausibility checks (alive, not Stunned, weapon equipped, cooldown elapsed, target within reach + small latency buffer, rate-limited), then applies Humanoid:TakeDamage. Mirror state modules client+server; never make the client wait for the server to set state back to Idle.","tools":["run_luau","bind_hitbox","make_npc"],"addresses_criteria":["client-predict-server-validate-hit-timing","lag-compensated-rewind-netcode","data-driven-attacks-collider-groups"],"pitfalls":"Trusting client-sent targets/positions/timestamps turns your buffer into a damage-on-demand exploit — never accept the client's damage or victim list, only that they attempted attack N, and recompute server-side. Gating action/movement state behind server replication makes input feel sluggish. For shared reactions, FireAllClients the verified target so every client animates it identically."}]},{"tier":"exceptional","goal":"Cross from 'good combat' to 'felt, fair, deep combat': route every feedback channel through ONE strength-scaled impact bus, grade reactions by direction+force (poise), add a defensive/parry counter-rhythm, weave knockback into cancelable combo spacing as a spacing tool, ground server validation in the marker contact frame, drive attacks from data/events, and run actors through a windowed/buffered selection brain.","steps":[{"id":"e1","order_index":1,"instruction":"Refactor all impact feedback into ONE strength-scaled IMPACT BUS. Replace the independently-fired channels with a single function fireImpact(strength, contactCFrame, hitVector) called once from the Contact marker that drives EVERY channel off that one event and SCALES them together by the single attack-strength value: hitstop duration, camera-shake amplitude, knockback magnitude, VFX :Emit count, sound pitch/volume, and freeze-frame length all = base * f(strength). No channel keeps its own independent timer.","tools":["run_luau"],"addresses_criteria":["synchronized-strength-scaled-channel-bus","marker-synced-impact-fx-stack","dynamic-combat-camera-framing"],"pitfalls":"Independently-timed effects (each with its own wait) drift out of sync and don't scale with hit weight — the rubric explicitly wants ONE contact event fanning out to all channels with a shared strength multiplier. If you AdjustSpeed for hitstop, the bus must still keep VFX/camera at speed 1."},{"id":"e2","order_index":2,"instruction":"Make hit reactions GRADED/POISE-GATED by direction AND force, not a single binary flinch. Maintain a poise/posture value per fighter; choose the reaction CATEGORICALLY by the incoming hit's direction (front/side/back) and force (light flinch vs heavy stagger vs poise-break launch). A stronger/heavier or differently-angled hit produces a categorically different reaction clip + a longer stun. Author the tiered reaction clips with add_keyframe and select at runtime via run_luau off the same hit vector + strength the impact bus uses.","tools":["add_keyframe","run_luau","bind_animation"],"addresses_criteria":["graded-poise-gated-hit-reactions","directional-additive-hit-reaction","hitstun-and-walkspeed-commitment"],"pitfalls":"A single flinch for every hit regardless of force/angle fails this — the reaction must VARY with direction and force. Add a posture/stamina resource so players can't be perma-stunned; insert recovery/parry windows so the defender always has counterplay (a single unavoidable 5-hit lock 'doesn't feel challenging')."},{"id":"e3","order_index":3,"instruction":"Add a DEFENSIVE/COUNTER-RHYTHM dimension tied to the INCOMING strike. Build block + parry with marker-synced, CANCELABLE/INTERRUPTIBLE timing windows: a parry window (input-buffered, marker-defined) that interrupts a stun chain and rewards reading the attacker, a block that absorbs/drains posture, and cancelable combo strings on the offense. The defensive window timing is defined relative to the attacker's active-frame markers. Wire block/parry states into the same state machine (Idle/Block/Parry/Stunned) so they gate and interrupt cleanly.","tools":["run_luau","bind_behavior","add_keyframe"],"addresses_criteria":["block-parry-defensive-layer","combo-state-machine-with-input-buffering","weighted-behavior-brain-enemies"],"pitfalls":"A purely offensive mash loop fails this criterion — the build needs a defensive counter-rhythm whose windows are marker-synced and cancelable/interruptible. A parry with no window (or a frame-perfect-only one) is unusable; buffer the input and define the window by marker."},{"id":"e4","order_index":4,"instruction":"Turn knockback into a TUNED COMBO/SPACING tool woven into cancelable strings. Make displacement direction-/force-aware (from e1's bus) AND part of the chain: light mid-combo hits nudge to KEEP the victim in range, the finisher launches/ragdolls. Optionally ragdoll on heavy/finisher hits via a ragdoll module (gated to the last hit only). Tune per-step knockback so the chain's spacing stays coherent — the attacker advances to stay in combo range while the victim is pushed — and keep counterplay (the launched victim can recover/tech).","tools":["run_luau","bind_hitbox"],"addresses_criteria":["ragdoll-knockback-combo-extension-with-counterplay","coherent-knockback-modern-mover"],"pitfalls":"A uniform incidental shove on every hit launches the target out of combo range (e.g. magnitude 80 mid-combo) and breaks the chain — scale knockback per combo step. Ragdolling on EVERY hit makes targets uncontrollable; gate it to finishers. Knockback must be woven into the cancelable string, not a flat constant."},{"id":"e5","order_index":5,"instruction":"Ground server hit validation in the MARKER-SYNCED CONTACT FRAME (lag-aware), not a stale or whole-swing window. The server validates the hit against the precise active-frame contact moment the attacker saw — gate the server hitbox by the same HitStart/HitEnd markers (or a buffered position history rewound to that frame), so registration is temporally grounded in the attacker's view of the moment rather than a loose whole-swing or stale window. Keep the client-predict feel from g10; the server just confirms the contact-frame query.","tools":["run_luau","bind_hitbox"],"addresses_criteria":["lag-compensated-rewind-netcode","client-predict-server-validate-hit-timing","swept-active-frame-hitbox-no-touched"],"pitfalls":"Validating against a whole-swing window (or a stale position) either rejects fair hits or lets cheaters claim hits across the entire swing. Tie validation to the marker-synced contact frame. The marker signal must be read on the peer that owns the playing track — keep the active-frame detection client-side and re-derive the contact-frame query server-side."},{"id":"e6","order_index":6,"instruction":"Make attacks DATA/EVENT-CONFIGURED, not one hardcoded swing. Define a combo/attack TABLE (per index: animationId, damage, hitboxSize/shape, knockback, strength, markers) and drive everything — which clip plays, when the hitbox opens (the marker-synced active frames), how the impact bus scales — off that data. New attacks/weapons are added by editing the table, not by writing new swing code. The hitbox is OPENED by the animation's marker events, never a hardcoded wait.","tools":["run_luau","bind_hitbox","bind_behavior"],"addresses_criteria":["data-driven-attacks-collider-groups","marker-driven-event-timing","combo-state-machine-with-input-buffering"],"pitfalls":"One hardcoded static swing with a fixed wait-gated hitbox fails this — author attacks as data whose hitboxes are opened by marker-synced active frames. Over-engineering the action object (vararg unpacking, methods ignoring self) is a noted smell; keep the table flat."},{"id":"e7","order_index":7,"instruction":"Run combat ACTOR decisions through a windowed, buffered SELECTION BRAIN. Use make_npc for the PvE enemy (idle->chase->attack->dead) and extend its decision loop via run_luau into a controller with cancel/timing WINDOWS and input buffering — the SAME state-machine discipline as the player (gated, buffered transitions; cancelable strings; no bespoke fire-and-forget logic). Enemies choose actions through the controller with cooldown/recovery windows so they can be parried and combo'd like a player.","tools":["make_npc","run_luau"],"addresses_criteria":["weighted-behavior-brain-enemies","combo-state-machine-with-input-buffering","block-parry-defensive-layer"],"pitfalls":"Bespoke fire-and-forget enemy logic (attack on a raw timer with no windows) fails this — route actor decisions through a controller with gated, buffered transitions. make_npc's stock loop is server-authoritative but timer-based; layer the windowed/buffered selection on top so the enemy honors the same cancel/cooldown windows the player does."},{"id":"e8","order_index":8,"instruction":"Prove RUNTIME-BLENDED concurrent limb motion on a running rig — the foundation for IK reactions, foot-planting, and physical secondary motion — and prove it where blending actually happens: at runtime, via an owned controller, not in edit-mode pose authoring. SCOPE THE TOOLS CORRECTLY: use pose_joint / ik_reach + add_keyframe ONLY to author the SOURCE clips (an upper-body attack/swing clip scoped to the arm/torso chain, and the locomotion clips), then do ALL the runtime blending in run_luau. In the run_luau controller: (1) play the attack clip at Action priority OVER Movement-priority locomotion (or procedurally post-multiply onto Waist/UpperTorso/arm Motor6D.Transform AFTER RunService.PreSimulation), restricted to the upper body, while the legs stay driven by the locomotion track underneath — confirm two or more weighted tracks coexist via AnimationTrack:AdjustWeight, never Stop()-ing the locomotion track (the secondary-motion + attack-over-locomotion foundation). (2) Create a real IKControl under the Humanoid (Type=Transform, ChainRoot=UpperLeg, EndEffector=Foot, Target=an Attachment moved per frame) for the foot-plant pass so it composes over the playing clip in the Animator's IK pass (the procedural-foot-planting prerequisite — legs must NOT be baked into the attack clip or there is nothing to plant on). (3) Drive a runtime head/gaze orientation each frame toward a live target (post-Animator Transform on Neck/Waist, or an upper-body IKControl), so the fighter turns toward the opponent while locomotion continues underneath (head-gaze-targeting + ikcontrol-reactive-limb-blending). VERIFY AT RUNTIME, not with capture_animation (which plays a single clip and overrides manual/concurrent poses): run a real playtest and capture the running session — show the upper body swinging/aiming while the legs are visibly movement-driven, and via run_luau sample two playing AnimationTracks' live weights (both > epsilon) to prove they coexist. A single full-body baked clip, or a capture_animation filmstrip of one authored clip, fails all four criteria — the proof must be concurrent weighted tracks blending on a live rig.","tools":["ik_reach","pose_joint","run_luau","capture_animation","capture_angles"],"addresses_criteria":["ikcontrol-reactive-limb-blending","procedural-foot-planting-ik","animationconstraint-physical-secondary-motion","head-gaze-targeting"],"pitfalls":"A single full-body baked clip the Animator plays alone fails all four of these — the rig must show tracks BLENDING and coexisting at runtime (attack over locomotion + concurrent layers). If the legs are baked into the attack clip, there's no locomotion underneath to plant feet on. Verify the layering visually with capture, don't assume it."}]}],"killer_systems":[{"name":"Marker-driven timing spine (GetMarkerReachedSignal)","what":"One source of truth for ALL combat timing: named animation event markers (HitStart, HitEnd, Contact, CancelOpen) baked into every clip, read at runtime via AnimationTrack:GetMarkerReachedSignal. Hitbox activation, the impact stack, hitstop, and the combo cancel window all fire off these markers — never off wait()/task.delay/track.Length.","how":"Author markers at exact frames with add_keyframe's event track, bind clips via bind_animation, and connect GetMarkerReachedSignal in run_luau on the peer that OWNS the playing track. Because markers ride keyframes, they stay synced even when you AdjustSpeed for hitstop — which is why the active-frame window never desyncs from the visual."},{"name":"Swept active-frame hitbox","what":"Damage volume LIVE only during the narrow HitStart->HitEnd window and DEAD during windup/recovery, detected by sweeping between previous-and-current frame positions so fast swings can't tunnel — never a static .Touched part or one ray per frame.","how":"Clear a per-swing alreadyHit set on the HitStart marker; each active frame, raycast between attachments spaced ~1 stud along the blade (or GetPartBoundsInBox/GetPartsInPart for wide sweeps) with OverlapParams filtered to character hitboxes, excluding the attacker. Wrap or replace bind_hitbox's radius-sweep internals with this via run_luau."},{"name":"Strength-scaled impact bus","what":"A single fireImpact(strength, contactCFrame, hitVector) called once from the Contact marker that fans out to EVERY feedback channel — hitstop, camera shake, knockback, VFX emit count, sound pitch/volume, freeze-frame — all scaled together by one attack-strength value. The exceptional-tier differentiator over independently-timed effects.","how":"One run_luau function on the contact event; each channel reads base * f(strength). Hitstop ramps both tracks' AdjustSpeed 1->~0->1 over a curve while VFX/camera stay at speed 1. Light hits = short freeze/small shake/small nudge; heavies = long freeze/big shake/launch."},{"name":"Cancelable combo state machine with input buffering","what":"Combos read as one continuous motion and chain without frame-perfect timing: a state machine (Idle/Windup/Active/Recovery/Stunned) with a marker-defined CancelOpen window, ONE buffered input auto-fired when the window opens, a debounce against canceling an in-progress swing, and an idle-timeout reset.","how":"Track comboIndex + lastAttackTime + one queued press in run_luau (on top of bind_behavior's state machine); store a press during an active swing and fire it on GetMarkerReachedSignal('CancelOpen'); re-key each clip's end pose to the next clip's start pose for flow; reset to index 1 after ~0.8-1.2s idle; client-predict, server-validate the step/cooldown."},{"name":"Responsive-local / authoritative-driven netcode","what":"Impact is FELT the instant the swing connects on the attacker's client frame, while the server stays the single source of truth: the client fires INTENT only, the server re-runs the marker-synced contact-frame hitbox + plausibility checks before any TakeDamage.","how":"Client plays anim/VFX/hitstop locally and FireServer('attack N') — never a hit claim with damage/positions. Server re-runs its own active-frame query from server physics gated by the same markers, checks alive/not-stunned/equipped/cooldown/reach+latency-buffer/rate-limit, then Humanoid:TakeDamage. FireAllClients the verified target so all clients animate the reaction identically. make_npc keeps enemies server-authoritative."}],"common_pitfalls":["Driving timing off wait()/task.delay/track.Length instead of GetMarkerReachedSignal — it drifts the instant you AdjustSpeed for hitstop and decouples damage/VFX from the visible blade. Bake markers and read them on the peer that owns the playing track.","A static .Touched part or one raycast per frame tunnels through targets on fast swings ('my sword passed through them'). Sweep between previous-and-current frame positions and keep a per-swing alreadyHit set.","GetMarkerReachedSignal connected on the wrong peer/track — it does NOT fire on the server for a client-started track. Do active-frame detection on the client that plays the anim; re-derive the query server-side for authority.","Attack clips that key the legs override the Movement walk, so the character moonwalks/T-slides while attacking. Author attacks upper-body-only at Action priority so per-joint blending lets locomotion drive the legs.","Priority mistakes: leaving the attack at Idle/Core makes it blend out and look limp; putting the victim flinch at attack priority cancels the attacker's swing. Use attack=Action4, hit-react=Action3, dodge=Action2, locomotion=Movement.","Freezing the WHOLE scene for hitstop (animation + particles + camera) feels like a lag spike, not a hit — keep VFX and camera live at speed 1 and only dip the animation tracks' AdjustSpeed on a curve.","Connect/impact SFX and VFX fired on swing-start (or a fixed wait) land before the blade connects and read as broken. Drive them off the contact marker; vary sound PlaybackSpeed so repeats don't grate.","Knockback from a fixed world-space vector / AssemblyLinearVelocity always goes the same way regardless of who hit whom — derive direction from the attacker/victim CFrames every hit. A LinearVelocity left attached sticks the victim moving forever — Debris-remove it; prefer ApplyImpulse or a short LinearVelocity over continuous VectorForce; BodyVelocity is deprecated.","Trusting client-sent damage/targets/timestamps is a damage-on-demand exploit. Accept only 'attempted attack N' and recompute everything server-side; rate-limit and cooldown-check the RemoteEvent.","Stun handling: hardcoding the WalkSpeed restore (16) breaks custom-movement games — save real values first; overlapping stuns that restore on their own timers un-stun early — refresh by longest remaining; a dead server script leaves the victim stunned forever — guard it.","Combo errors: no cancel window = frame-perfect-only and stiff; no idle-reset = the index never returns to 1 so players start mid-combo; buffering more than one input lets a held key auto-mash; clips whose end pose doesn't match the next start pose snap/teleport between hits.","Shipping a flat clip list with no dedicated combat idle (snapping 'fighting'->'standing'), or one symmetric linear swing reused everywhere (reads robotic/weightless) — hold a combat stance idle and ease/vary each attack's phases and silhouette.","Forgetting the rig foundation: an Avatar-Joint-Upgrade R15 (AnimationConstraints, zero Motor6Ds) silently shows no keyframe tracks / a T-pose. Verify Motor6D joints before animating, and remember a single full-body baked clip blocks the runtime limb-blending the exceptional tier requires.","Over-juicing: camera shake that's too strong is nauseating (make it subtle + toggleable), mismatched VFX (smoke on a flesh punch) breaks immersion, and a persistent damage vignette miscommunicates whether you're hitting or being hit. Restraint and readability over spectacle."],"provenance":{"sources":["https://create.roblox.com/docs/animation/editor","https://create.roblox.com/docs/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/animation/create-an-animation","https://create.roblox.com/docs/animation/events","https://create.roblox.com/docs/animation/using","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/raycast-hitbox-401-for-all-your-melee-needs/374482","https://simplified.media/guides/roblox-combat-systems","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/stun-handler-v21/1072859","https://kitsblox.com/blog/how-to-make-combat-system-roblox","https://www.ahmadmohammadnejad.com/sandbox/a-more-realistic-hitstop ; https://create.roblox.com/docs/en-us/reference/engine/classes/AnimationTrack","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/knockback-always-moves-the-dummy-to-the-same-direction/3026787 ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/linearvelocity-or-bodyvelocitydepreciated-for-making-a-knockback-system/2279867","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/should-i-use-animation-events-getmarkerreachedsignal-to-activate-hitboxes/2439938 ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-can-i-use-getmarkerreachedsignal-on-the-server-with-client-animations/3073543 ; https://create.roblox.com/docs/reference/engine/classes/AnimationTrack/GetMarkerReachedSignal","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/input-buffering-and-event-driven-action-system-for-combat-system/3929897 ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/cancel-system-for-combat-system/3600431 ; https://create.roblox.com/docs/en-us/reference/engine/classes/AnimationTrack","https://create.roblox.com/docs/reference/engine/enums/AnimationPriority ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/keep-the-lower-body-walking-while-another-animation-plays/2791836 ; https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-do-you-override-an-animation-body-part-with-another-animation/2867008","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-would-i-go-about-making-my-combat-feel-more-impactful/2042214","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-can-i-improve-this-combat-system/2860707","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/what-makes-a-satisfying-combat-experience-how-can-i-make-my-combat-game-feel-meaningful/3375581","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/what-makes-a-satisfying-combat-experience-how-can-i-make-my-combat-game-feel-meaningful/3375581","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-can-i-improve-this-combat-system/2860707","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-can-i-improve-this-combat-system/2860707","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-would-i-go-about-making-my-combat-feel-more-impactful/2042214","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/combat-animation-helpfeedback/2017328","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/combat-animation-helpfeedback/2017328","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/what-makes-good-combat/1999076","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/my-combat-system-does-not-feel-fluid-and-i-am-looking-forward-to-improving-it/1536278","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/combat-feels-janky-25d-fighting-game/2577244","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/what-makes-a-satisfying-combat-experience-how-can-i-make-my-combat-game-feel-meaningful/3375581","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/what-makes-good-combat/1999076","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-would-i-go-about-making-my-combat-feel-more-impactful/2042214","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ht6ZfibXpDs","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AX3rWUj5gRo","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WZyvOpIiaYE","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AX3rWUj5gRo","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vPHa4poXS7I","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ht6ZfibXpDs","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ht6ZfibXpDs","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gmz904QpfxU","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TwUQ_19fdiE","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gmz904QpfxU","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vPHa4poXS7I","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ht6ZfibXpDs","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AX3rWUj5gRo","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vPHa4poXS7I","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FpGgpUWEb_k","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FpGgpUWEb_k","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UURYhAVph5g"],"confidence":"high","coverage":{"criteria_total":29,"criteria_addressed":29,"uncovered":[]}},"date":"2026-06-27"}]==],
	-- environment.guide.json
	[==[{"module":"environment","system_module":"environment","version":"1.0","rubric_version":"1.0","overview":"A great Roblox environment is built in five strict chapters — Greybox -> Develop assets -> Assemble a reusable library -> Construct the world (apply library + terrain + set dressing) -> Optimize + light — and you never polish before the layout plays. Critically: the agent has NO dedicated terrain/lighting/material tools. Authored content comes from create_instance + set_property (Parts, walls, Lighting/Atmosphere/post-effect instances, MaterialVariant/SurfaceAppearance, Terrain calls), generate_mesh (Cube, for MAN-MADE props), author_mesh (pine/rock organics), paint_texture (procedural tiling Texture on PARTS only), and save_mesh (persist). EVERYTHING terrain (Terrain:FillBlock/FillBall/ReplaceMaterial/PaintMaterials), every Lighting/Atmosphere/ColorCorrection/Bloom/SunRays tweak, and every MaterialVariant/SurfaceAppearance/Package is done through run_luau (edit-mode loadstring) or create_instance. You SEE your work and iterate with capture / capture_angles from the PLAYER'S eye level, and self-grade with evaluate (its EnvEvidence census specifically rewards non-Plastic Materials, SurfaceAppearance children, placed Lights, an Atmosphere under Lighting, and post-effect instances — so those are the concrete structural targets). The build order below front-loads the cheap-to-change gameplay flow, defers all visual polish, and ends with a measured optimization pass so a rich scene stays shippable.","tiers":[{"tier":"baseline","goal":"Prove the space PLAYS and reads at human scale before any art: a function-first greybox composed for the player's eye, plus the seed of a reuse system (a modular kit + at least one custom material).","steps":[{"id":"b1","order_index":1,"instruction":"Gather form + style reference and decide a committed theme/mood BEFORE building (e.g. 'evening medieval market'). Write the intended player route, the focal landmark, and the time-of-day in one sentence. This anchors every later decision (palette, lighting scenario, kit). No tool call — it is a design decision recorded in your plan — but it is what makes the build read as composed rather than improvised.","addresses_criteria":["env-reference-driven-plan-and-eyelevel","env-greybox-metric-gameplay-first"],"tools":[],"pitfalls":"Skipping the plan and improvising geometry is the #1 'AI did it' tell. The reference is not a 1:1 target — your result should drift from it; use it to confirm scale, silhouette, and mood, not to copy."},{"id":"b2","order_index":2,"instruction":"Greybox the playable area FIRST with anchored block Parts on a 5-stud grid. Build in dependency order via create_instance: floor planes -> elevation changes (use WedgePart for sight-line breaks) -> perimeter walls with intended openings -> spawn zones -> combat/interaction pockets where lanes intersect. Set on every part: Anchored=true, a flat placeholder Color per zone (set_property), Size as 5-stud multiples. Keep every doorway/hallway >=10 studs wide and every wall >=10 studs tall (two characters pass; a default 5-stud jump can't clear a 10-stud wall). The ~5-stud-tall default character is your scale ruler.","addresses_criteria":["env-greybox-metric-gameplay-first","env-human-scale-proportion-standard"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Off-grid or sub-10-stud halls/doors break two-wide passage and let 5-stud jumps bypass short walls. Forgetting Anchored=true makes greybox fall/jitter under physics the moment you playtest."},{"id":"b3","order_index":3,"instruction":"Validate flow at the PLAYER'S eye level, not the top-down editor camera. Use capture (and capture_angles with elevation='eye') to look down each lane: do routes read, are sight-lines broken where intended, do spawns avoid bottlenecks, does scale feel true against the character? Fix layout flaws NOW while geometry is disposable blocks. Re-capture after each change. Frame at least one deliberate sightline toward your intended focal landmark.","addresses_criteria":["env-reference-driven-plan-and-eyelevel","env-greybox-metric-gameplay-first"],"tools":["capture","capture_angles"],"pitfalls":"Judging only from the top-down editor view: a scene that looks fine from above can have broken scale and dead sightlines at character height. Saving all verification for the end means layout fixes land after art is committed and become expensive."},{"id":"b4","order_index":4,"instruction":"Establish the seed of a REUSE system over the greybox: define a small modular kit (a wall unit, a corner, a floor tile, a trim/pillar) as named Models, all sized as multiples of one base module on the grid so they tile when rotated. Reuse those same pieces across the build rather than hand-authoring each spot. Then author at least one CUSTOM material so the kit isn't all default Plastic: either paint_texture (bricks/planks/tiles/checker/noise) onto kit Parts, or create a MaterialVariant via run_luau (MaterialService + a MaterialVariant with ColorMap/NormalMap/RoughnessMap/MetalnessMap) and assign it. EnvEvidence specifically counts non-Plastic Materials and SurfaceAppearance children, so this is the measurable baseline.","addresses_criteria":["env-asset-library-packages-reusable-look","env-modular-kit-grid-pivot-discipline"],"tools":["create_instance","paint_texture","run_luau","set_property"],"pitfalls":"One-off hand-authored parts at arbitrary coordinates with default Plastic everywhere fails the reuse-system bar. paint_texture is PARTS ONLY — it smears on a MeshPart's UVs; texture meshes come from generate_mesh instead. Pieces whose dimensions aren't divisible by the module leave gaps/overlaps when rotated 90 degrees."}]},{"tier":"good","goal":"Turn the proven greybox into a composed, lit, dressed world: apply the modular kit, sculpt terrain that extends the world beyond bounds, commit a time-of-day lighting+atmosphere+post-processing treatment, vary set-dressing, break flat surfaces, and run a real optimization pass — the five killer systems (composition, lighting, modular kit, scale+performance, plus density).","steps":[{"id":"g1","order_index":1,"instruction":"Construct the world by applying the modular kit onto the greybox, zone by zone, via create_instance + set_property (Position/Orientation, Material/MaterialVariant, Anchored=true). Move ONE piece at a time (selecting many averages their pivots and breaks the snap offsets). Give each zone a DISTINCT MaterialVariant or hue for wayfinding (e.g. a unique wall variant per area), so players read where they are. Then delete the original greybox blocks as each zone is covered.","addresses_criteria":["env-modular-kit-grid-pivot-discipline","env-wayfinding-zone-color-material-coding"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property","run_luau"],"pitfalls":"Generic single-material cladding everywhere kills wayfinding and storytelling. Moving multiple kit pieces together averages their pivots and destroys intentional alignment — move one, then offset in module-sized increments."},{"id":"g2","order_index":2,"instruction":"Compose in three explicit depth planes. Group geometry under Foreground / Midground / Background Folders (create_instance Folder). FOREGROUND: framing parts near the spawn/approach that partially occlude the view (an archway, planters, an overhanging branch). MIDGROUND: the playable subject. BACKGROUND: oversized backdrop landforms placed far out and scaled up to read at the horizon. Create the focal point by deliberate CONTRAST — one tall mass in an otherwise low/open space (height), a wide plaza ringed by narrow structures (density), or one element angled off-grid (orientation). Use WedgePart elevation transitions and sightline blockers so the landmark is occluded on approach, then revealed at a framed moment. Verify each composition from the player camera with capture.","addresses_criteria":["env-composition-sightlines-landmark-flow","env-reference-driven-plan-and-eyelevel"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property","capture"],"pitfalls":"A flat, evenly-distributed field of props gives the eye nowhere to land. Leading lines alone do NOT work — a sightline only reads if the player has a REASON to look there (a goal/exit/reward), so the focal mass must feel relevant, not merely pointed-at. Blank walls and empty space are failures, not neutral; every sightline must terminate on something intentional."},{"id":"g3","order_index":3,"instruction":"Sculpt terrain via run_luau so the playable area reads as an authored slice of a larger world, not a flat baseplate under a raw skybox. (1) MASS & CONTOUR: use workspace.Terrain:FillBlock/FillBall/FillRegion to raise PERIMETER landforms (hills/cliffs/slopes with varied, offset, non-parallel masses and a size gradient) that blend the build into its surroundings and imply continuation past the edge; vary elevation so the ground is not one uniform-noise blob. (2) NORMALIZE: run an explicit smoothing/normalization pass so transitions read organic — no lumpy/blocky voxels and no hard untreated seams where terrain meets structures (sink/grade the ground into building footings). (3) MULTI-MATERIAL BANDING: use ReplaceMaterial/PaintMaterials to layer at least two transition BANDS between adjacent materials (e.g. a dirt band between grass and rock, a sand band at a shoreline) so no two materials meet at a single hard edge. (4) CUSTOM MATERIAL OVERRIDE: override the default terrain materials with your own tileable MaterialVariants tuned to match the built materials, so the ground does not read as a separate generic 'Roblox' surface. (5) DELETE the Baseplate so terrain/sky are visible. (6) GROUND THE FOLIAGE: enable terrain grass decoration with the grass height pulled down (not a cornfield) and ground every foliage/set-dressing asset by sinking most of it into the sculpted surface so it sits planted in the terrain rather than floating on a flat plane — keep foliage in the decorated zones, clustered contextually, not sprayed evenly.","addresses_criteria":["env-terrain-contour-multimaterial-tooling","env-part-blockout-to-terrain-normalize","env-set-extension-world-beyond-bounds","env-grass-decoration-and-foliage-grounding"],"tools":["run_luau","set_property"],"pitfalls":"A flat baseplate ending at a hard edge under the default skybox is the textbook bare-edge failure. A single uniformly-noisy terrain blob with hard material edges fails too. Default terrain materials that clash with your built materials make the ground read as a separate generic 'Roblox' surface — override them."},{"id":"g4","order_index":4,"instruction":"Build vertical terrain depth with author_mesh rock clusters plus sculpted cliffs: scale a few LARGE rocks (author_mesh kind='rock') over many tiny ones for a main cliff face, layer them in offset (non-parallel) courses with a size gradient, let some poke out past the rest for depth, and sculpt a grass lip along the top edge. Never place three identical rocks in a row — rotate a rock 90 degrees for 4 'free' variants and vary scale. Sink most of each rock/bush INTO the terrain so only part shows (props sitting on a flat plane read fake).","addresses_criteria":["env-cliff-rock-layering-depth","env-grass-decoration-and-foliage-grounding","env-varied-nonuniform-set-dressing-density"],"tools":["author_mesh","run_luau","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Three obviously-duplicated rocks in succession look abnormal — duplicate only when moving far away. Flat ground or a single flat cliff face fails the vertical-relief bar. Cube (generate_mesh) is wrong for rocks/trees — its organics come out creature-like; use author_mesh for naturals."},{"id":"g5","order_index":5,"instruction":"Break every major flat surface so it catches light and casts shadow: inset windows/doors into wall planes, extrude trim/ledges so surfaces step in and out, extend roof lines past the walls, and bevel hard corners with WedgePart/CylinderPart. Apply a trim/tileable texture (paint_texture on Parts, or a MaterialVariant) so walls read articulated rather than as bare placeholder boxes — but model ACTUAL in/out relief, not just a painted-on look.","addresses_criteria":["env-surface-depth-relief-no-flat-walls","env-trim-sheets-seamless-tileables"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property","paint_texture","run_luau"],"pitfalls":"Texture/material alone with NO geometric relief still reads flat — you need protruding/receding trim geometry. A distinctive one-off motif tiled across many pieces makes repetition glaringly obvious; keep trim detail generic."},{"id":"g6","order_index":6,"instruction":"Author lighting as a deliberate MOOD SYSTEM, tuned together (Lighting + Atmosphere + ambient + skybox), via run_luau / set_property / create_instance on game.Lighting. Do it in this order and make warm/cool SEPARATION the load-bearing goal:\n\n1) COMMIT a time-of-day scenario FIRST and MATCH the skybox to it. Set a modern Technology mode (Future or ShadowMap) and a committed ClockTime (e.g. evening ~17). Parent a Sky to Lighting whose UPPER hemisphere matches the chosen sky and whose LOWER hemisphere is pulled toward (and darker than) the ground hue — the skybox must read as the same time-of-day as the lighting, not a default skybox under tuned light.\n\n2) Establish WARM-HIGHLIGHT vs COOL-SHADOW separation (the painting fundamental, ported to engine props). Warm the key/highlight: ColorShift_Top toward yellow/orange. Cool the shadow FILL: set Ambient/OutdoorAmbient to a colored, NON-black fill that is genuinely COOLER and DARKER in value than the highlight tone (e.g. a moonlit/teal-blue fill, not a bright light-value purple) so lit faces read warm and shadowed faces read cool — there must be a visible temperature AND value gap between highlight and shadow, never pure black, never a flat near-white wash. Set EnvironmentDiffuseScale / EnvironmentSpecularScale to taste (≈1) to let surfaces pick up the colored environment.\n\n3) Add depth with a tuned Atmosphere — and respect the dependency: the Atmosphere does NOTHING without a Sky in Lighting, so step 1 must already be done. Parent an Atmosphere to Lighting (EnvEvidence checks for this) with Density tuned for the scene (~0.27–0.49 outdoors), low Haze to keep sky clarity, Offset so NEAR objects stay crisp while distant landforms fade, and Color/Decay pulled toward the AVERAGE/horizon color of the scene so the haze UNIFIES the palette and recedes distant geometry (the distant-blur test should read as one mood).\n\nFAIL if any of these hold: engine-default noon (white midday light), pure-black or near-black Ambient (shadows must carry a cool colored fill), no Sky / a default skybox unmatched to the scenario, no Atmosphere parented to Lighting, or a highlight/shadow pair with no temperature-and-value separation. The deliverables must be VERIFIABLE: a committed ClockTime + Technology mode, a warm ColorShift_Top, a cool non-black colored OutdoorAmbient that is darker than the highlight, a matched Sky (lower hemisphere coupled to ground hue), and an Atmosphere under Lighting with named Density/Haze/Offset/Color.","addresses_criteria":["env-lighting-atmosphere-mood-system","env-sun-as-composition-and-shadow-craft","env-unified-atmospheric-palette-blur-test"],"tools":["run_luau","set_property","create_instance"],"pitfalls":"Engine-default noon (white midday light, black shadows, no scenario) is the #1 untouched-build signature. Pure-black Ambient kills mood — shadows must carry a cool/colored fill. Atmosphere does NOTHING unless there is a Sky in Lighting AND the Atmosphere is parented to Lighting. A greyish-white untuned haze adds nothing — pull its Color toward the scene's key hues."},{"id":"g7","order_index":7,"instruction":"Add a restrained, ORDERED post-processing grade as children of Lighting via create_instance: ColorCorrectionEffect FIRST (modest Contrast ~+0.1-0.2, genre-tuned Saturation, warm/cool TintColor) to set base mood; then BloomEffect with a RAISED Threshold so only true highlights glow; then DepthOfFieldEffect only for intentional focus with NearIntensity=0 so the player's own surroundings don't blur; then SunRaysEffect subtle (~0.15-0.25). Toggle each Enabled on/off to A/B compare. EnvEvidence counts these post-effect instances, so a committed grade is a measured target.","addresses_criteria":["env-post-processing-ordered-stack-restraint","env-unified-atmospheric-palette-blur-test"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property","capture"],"pitfalls":"Post left OFF reads ungraded and flat; everything maxed produces blown-out bloom, a washed-out grade, or a full-world blur. Never apply a full-world BlurEffect (only behind a GUI). DepthOfField with non-zero NearIntensity blurs the player's surroundings — order and restraint are the skill, not the effect count."},{"id":"g8","order_index":8,"instruction":"Set-dress ONLY after structure, materials, and terrain already read correctly — dressing cannot rescue a broken blockout. Use generate_mesh (Cube) for man-made props (crates, carts, barrels, lamps, signs, furniture) and author_mesh for organics (pine, rock). Build ONE good instance of each prop, then duplicate-and-vary (generate_mesh is rate-limited ~5/min — never regenerate per instance; on a rate error wait and retry). For EVERY repeated element, drive variation from run_luau + Random.new across THREE independent axes at once — Orientation (>=90deg spread, and exploit an asset's faces so a rotated rock reads as a new variation), Size (per-instance scale factor), and per-instance color/tint — so no two adjacent instances and never three-in-a-row are identical; a single duplicated prop merely jittered is NOT enough. Place by CONTEXTUAL CLUSTERS tied to a named focal point (axes/wares at a market stall, rock clusters in offset groups, foliage in copses), never uniform spacing, and leave deliberate NEGATIVE SPACE around each focal point; concentrate detail at corners, intersections, and T-junctions, curated not sprayed. Then go past mere variation to BELIEVABILITY: author at least 2-3 wear/history beats tied to the theme (a repaired or worn surface, a broken or abandoned object, street furniture, a secondary point of interest along a path) so the space implies use and history rather than reading as brand-new arbitrary clutter. Finish with capture_angles and confirm from the captures that (a) no grid-uniform/identical repetition is visible, (b) the focal point still has breathing room, and (c) the wear/history detail is legible — not just asserted.","addresses_criteria":["env-varied-nonuniform-set-dressing-density","env-environmental-storytelling-wear-history"],"tools":["generate_mesh","author_mesh","run_luau","set_property","capture_angles"],"pitfalls":"Uniform spacing + identical rotation + single scale is the #1 procedural tell; spraying props everywhere with no negative space buries the focal point. generate_mesh is rate-limited ~5/min (beta) — on a rate error, wait and retry; build one prop, then duplicate-and-vary rather than regenerating each."},{"id":"g9","order_index":9,"instruction":"Place local lights for dimension (not just global illumination): add PointLight/SpotLight/SurfaceLight via create_instance at lamps, windows, fires, and shadowed interiors, tuned to the mood (e.g. a warm campfire PointLight ~255,179,73, Range ~48). EnvEvidence counts placed Lights. Keep light Shadows on where they read; this is what separates planes and gives the build form against flat global-only illumination.","addresses_criteria":["env-foliage-fill-light-recoloring-shadows","env-sun-as-composition-and-shadow-craft"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property","capture"],"pitfalls":"A flat, low-contrast scene where every surface reads at the same brightness has no depth. Neon parts that 'glow' cost more than a PointLight and bloom unpredictably — prefer a PointLight for local warmth."},{"id":"g10","order_index":10,"instruction":"Run a measured OPTIMIZATION pass so the rich scene ships. Per asset (set_property / run_luau): CanCollide/CanTouch/CanQuery=false on non-interactive dressing (keep walls queryable), CastShadow=false on small/distant/occluded props, CollisionFidelity=Box/Hull on simple shapes, RenderFidelity=Performance/Automatic on distant high-vertex foliage. Make duplicates SHARE one mesh asset ID and identical SurfaceAppearance so they batch into one draw call (duplicate-in-place, never bulk-import unique IDs). Persist finished custom meshes with save_mesh so they survive Play. Use run_luau to read part counts / sweep for occluded geometry; budget shadow-casting lights.","addresses_criteria":["env-performance-optimization-pass","env-human-scale-proportion-standard"],"tools":["set_property","run_luau","save_mesh"],"pitfalls":"Emitting dense instances on defaults (collide-on, query-on, shadow-on, Precise collision) tanks mobile framerate. Even one differing SurfaceAppearance property between two copies breaks the single-draw-call batch. Merge meshes only when the design is locked — merged assets can't be individually moved/recolored. save_mesh needs the 'CreateAssetAsync Lua API' beta flag and is rate-limited ~30/min — run it on a finished build, not every iteration."}]},{"tier":"exceptional","goal":"Push hero surfaces and atmosphere to a crafted, art-directed ceiling: full PBR SurfaceAppearance on hero meshes, a tuned depth-falloff system, theme-tied wear/story detail, custom terrain PBR, and layered atmospheric life — the difference between 'good Roblox' and a designed world.","steps":[{"id":"e1","order_index":1,"instruction":"Give HERO meshes full PBR craft via a SurfaceAppearance, replacing the Studio-default plastic read. Via run_luau add a SurfaceAppearance child to each hero MeshPart with a correct, complete map set: ColorMap (albedo, no baked lighting), an OpenGL-format NormalMap (flat = 127,127,255 — NOT DirectX, which inverts the relief), a grayscale RoughnessMap, and a BINARY MetalnessMap (fully metal or fully non-metal, never a muddy half value). Keep every map <=1024x1024 and author them so seams tile. Reuse ONE shared SurfaceAppearance/Package across the whole kit rather than proliferating unique texture IDs (this also batches draw calls). Pair the PBR maps with the actual trim/tileable relief geometry from g5 so the normal detail reads against real silhouette. Persist the finished hero meshes with save_mesh so the surface survives Play.","addresses_criteria":["env-pbr-surfaceappearance-material-craft","env-trim-sheets-seamless-tileables"],"tools":["run_luau","create_instance","set_property","save_mesh"],"pitfalls":"A DirectX-format normal map inverts the relief (bumps point the wrong way). Non-binary metalness produces a muddy half-metal look. Note SurfaceAppearance.ColorMap is not writable at PluginSecurity via a bare set — author the maps as a SurfaceAppearance with Content set through the supported path (run_luau / import), and reuse one shared package rather than proliferating unique texture IDs."},{"id":"e2","order_index":2,"instruction":"Author a complete, scene-tuned atmospheric-depth-AND-life pass, not just a haze. (1) Depth falloff: on the Atmosphere (under Lighting) pull Density and Color toward the scene's key hues so it actively grades distance, set Offset to keep NEAR focal geometry crisp while distant terrain fades, keep Haze low (high Haze blows out metal via fresnel), and state explicitly why you chose Atmosphere over legacy FogStart/FogEnd (soft volumetric blend vs hard cutoff) for this scene. (2) Read-against: couple the falloff to the oversized backdrop geometry from g2 so the gradient has layered silhouettes to overlay — verify in capture that near/mid/far separate. (3) Volumetric LIFE (required, not optional): add at least one ambient-motion cue (a low-rate ParticleEmitter of drifting dust/leaves/insects sized to the scene) AND a faux-volumetric light cue (a sun-aligned semi-transparent fog-mesh god-ray or light-shaft with CastShadow off), and state the performance gate — e.g. only spawn the volumetrics/particles on a high-graphics or showcase tier so the mean hardware path stays cheap. The step must produce both the tuned depth falloff and the motion/volumetric life, captured, so it earns env-fog-vs-atmosphere-intentional-depth-tool AND env-organic-motion-volumetric-life rather than only the former.","addresses_criteria":["env-fog-vs-atmosphere-intentional-depth-tool","env-organic-motion-volumetric-life"],"tools":["set_property","run_luau","capture"],"pitfalls":"A greyish-white untuned default haze adds nothing. Too much Density flattens contrast and hides the focal landmark; too much Haze blows out metal. No backdrop geometry means the falloff has nothing to read against."},{"id":"e3","order_index":3,"instruction":"Dress the space with deliberate, theme-tied WEAR and HISTORY so it has a sense of place: story-consistent detail that implies use over time (worn footpaths, tools scattered at a workstation, scorch and slag near a forge, crates and rope by a dock, a repaired or broken object) rather than bare space or arbitrary brand-new clutter with no theme link. Distribute the dressing at MULTIPLE scales — large hero props down to small ground stones and debris — and push it INTO the open areas and around focal points, not only hugging walls and edges; concentrate detail at corners/intersections while deliberately preserving negative space so the result reads curated, not sprayed. Build the props from generate_mesh/author_mesh plus small create_instance Parts, varied and clustered per g8's rules (varied rotation/scale/per-instance tint, contextual clusters, no three identical in a row). Crucially, the foliage and hero props must read as AUTHORED assets, not stock toolbox meshes with default plastic: apply crafted PBR materials via paint_texture/SurfaceAppearance (no default-Plastic look) and, where it counts, a real foliage technique (e.g. normal-transfer leaf cards with a white roughness map, hand-painted cavity-edge highlights, PixelFix-treated alpha cutouts) so the dressing earns both the storytelling AND the stylized-foliage-material bar.","addresses_criteria":["env-environmental-storytelling-wear-history","env-stylized-foliage-normal-roughness-pipeline"],"tools":["generate_mesh","author_mesh","create_instance","set_property","paint_texture"],"pitfalls":"Arbitrary brand-new clutter with no theme connection reads as filler, not story. Vacant blank space is the single most-cited reason a scene 'feels off' — but spraying clutter without negative space is just as bad."},{"id":"e4","order_index":4,"instruction":"Override terrain and key ground surfaces with CUSTOM PBR materials (not the stock palette): via run_luau create a MaterialVariant in MaterialService with crafted ColorMap/NormalMap/RoughnessMap/MetalnessMap (OpenGL normals), set its BaseMaterial, and assign it in MaterialService as the override for that terrain material so the override actually applies. Tune any water surface to the scene (lower Reflectance toward 0 so it reads via wave motion, not skybox mirroring) rather than leaving raw realistic defaults.","addresses_criteria":["env-custom-terrain-material-override","env-stylized-water-treatment-and-quirks"],"tools":["run_luau","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Importing a MaterialVariant changes NOTHING until you assign it as the override in MaterialService — easy to miss. Works for any terrain material except Water and Air. Reflective water that mirrors the sky fights a stylized look — kill its Reflectance."},{"id":"e5","order_index":5,"instruction":"Add layered atmospheric LIFE so the scene isn't a static default: drifting Clouds (under workspace.Terrain via create_instance — Clouds live under Terrain, NOT Lighting), plus ParticleEmitter/Beam accents (dust motes, embers, light shafts, falling petals) placed among the dressing and light sources. Combined with the tuned Atmosphere (e2) and local fill lights (g9), this gives the depth-and-motion cue that reads as authored life. Final-verify the whole scene from the player camera with capture / capture_angles, and grade against the rubric with evaluate.","addresses_criteria":["env-organic-motion-volumetric-life","env-foliage-fill-light-recoloring-shadows"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property","capture","capture_angles"],"pitfalls":"Clouds are under Terrain, not Lighting — easy to hunt for in the wrong place. Over-emitting particles tanks mobile perf and clutters the frame; keep ambient motion subtle. A scene with no ambient motion feels dead even when geometry is fine."}]}],"killer_systems":[{"name":"Composition / sightlines / landmark-guided flow","what":"The player is ALWAYS looking at something intentional: a clear focal landmark anchored by deliberate foreground/midground/background layering, with verticality and sightline-blocking used as directorial tools.","how":"Group geometry under Foreground/Midground/Background Folders (create_instance). Place framing parts close to the approach (archway, planters, overhang) that partially occlude; put the playable subject in the midground; scale up oversized backdrop landforms far out for the horizon. Manufacture the focal point by CONTRAST (one tall mass among low ones / a wide plaza among narrow / one off-grid element). Use WedgePart elevation and blockers to occlude the landmark on approach then reveal it at a framed moment. Verify every composition from the player camera with capture — never the top-down editor view. The focal mass must be RELEVANT (a goal/exit/reward), not merely pointed-at."},{"name":"Lighting / atmosphere / mood","what":"Lighting is an authored mood system tuned together (Lighting + Atmosphere + ambient + color + a committed time-of-day), not engine-default noon.","how":"Choose the time-of-day FIRST. Via run_luau/set_property on game.Lighting: set ClockTime, a modern Technology mode, EnvironmentDiffuse/SpecularScale, warm ColorShift_Top, and a NON-BLACK colored Ambient/OutdoorAmbient fill. create_instance a Sky (required) under Lighting and an Atmosphere parented to Lighting with Density tuned and Color pulled to the scene's average. Add local PointLight/SpotLight at lamps/windows/fires. EnvEvidence specifically checks for an Atmosphere under Lighting and counts placed Lights — so this system is directly measured."},{"name":"Modular kit reuse","what":"The world is assembled from a reused modular kit (with rotation/scale variation) rather than one-off hand-placed parts at arbitrary coordinates.","how":"Define a small kit (wall, corner, floor tile, trim) as named Models sized as multiples of one base module on the grid so pieces tile when rotated. Build via create_instance + set_property, moving ONE piece at a time (multi-select averages pivots and breaks alignment), offsetting in module-sized increments. Skin pieces with shared MaterialVariants/SurfaceAppearance for cohesion and draw-call batching, and give each zone a distinct variant for wayfinding."},{"name":"Scale readability + performance optimization","what":"Navigation reads true at character height via a consistent human-scaled standard, AND the richness is made shippable through a distinct, measured optimization pass.","how":"Build on a 5-stud grid with >=10-stud doors/walls measured against the ~5-stud character; verify at eye level with capture. Then a perf pass via set_property/run_luau: CanCollide/CanTouch/CanQuery=false on dressing, CastShadow=false on small/distant props, CollisionFidelity=Box/Hull on simple shapes, RenderFidelity=Performance on distant foliage; share one mesh ID + identical SurfaceAppearance so duplicates batch to one draw call (duplicate-in-place, never bulk-import); save_mesh to persist; budget shadow-casting lights."},{"name":"Material / texture / PBR surface craft","what":"Hero surfaces use full PBR SurfaceAppearance/MaterialVariant with a correct map set and correct authoring rules, replacing Studio defaults that read as plastic.","how":"Via run_luau add a SurfaceAppearance to hero MeshParts with ColorMap + OpenGL NormalMap (flat=127,127,255) + grayscale RoughnessMap + BINARY MetalnessMap, all <=1024x1024, and reuse ONE shared package across the kit. For Parts and terrain, paint_texture (procedural tiling, Parts only) or a MaterialVariant in MaterialService (assigned as the override, or it does nothing). EnvEvidence counts non-Plastic Materials and SurfaceAppearance children, so crafted surfaces are a measured target."}],"common_pitfalls":["Jumping to detailed assets/lighting before the greybox plays well — the layout inevitably changes and you redo polished work. Prove flow in cheap anchored blocks first; light LAST.","Composing from the top-down editor camera. A scene that looks fine from above has broken scale and dead sightlines at the player's ~5-stud eye height — always verify with capture from eye level.","Shipping on engine-default lighting (noon, white light, black shadows, no Atmosphere) — the #1 'untouched / AI-generated' tell. Commit a time-of-day scenario, fill shadows with non-black color, and remember Atmosphere does nothing without a Sky parented under Lighting.","Default-Plastic materials everywhere. EnvEvidence literally counts Plastic/SmoothPlastic as 'default' and rewards non-Plastic Materials + SurfaceAppearance children — so a wash of default parts fails the material bar by construction.","Uniform set dressing: identical rotation, single scale, even spacing. That is the #1 procedural signature. Vary orientation/scale/tint per instance and cluster contextually with deliberate negative space around focal points.","paint_texture on a MeshPart — it smears on arbitrary mesh UVs and is rejected; paint_texture is Parts-only, and meshes get their look from generate_mesh (Cube) or a persisted Cube texture.","Using generate_mesh (Cube) for trees/rocks/foliage — its organic single-meshes come out creature-like. Use author_mesh (pine/rock) for naturals; reserve Cube for man-made props (and respect its ~5/min beta rate limit).","Forgetting to persist: in-session generate_mesh/author_mesh/paint_texture geometry is edit-only and goes blank in a playtest until save_mesh is run (needs the 'CreateAssetAsync Lua API' beta flag).","Texture without geometric relief: a painted-on trim look on a still-flat wall reads flat. Model actual inset/extruded/beveled relief so surfaces catch light and cast shadow.","Flat baseplate edges under a raw skybox. Sculpt perimeter terrain (run_luau Terrain calls), delete the Baseplate, and override terrain materials to match the build so the world reads as continuing beyond bounds.","MaterialVariant authored but never assigned as the MaterialService override — it changes nothing until assigned. Same trap for a SurfaceAppearance with a DirectX (not OpenGL) normal map, which inverts the relief.","No optimization pass on a dense scene: defaults (collide-on, query-on, shadow-on, Precise collision, unique mesh IDs) tank mobile framerate. Cull flags, share IDs/SurfaceAppearance for draw-call batching, budget shadow lights, and merge only when the design is locked.","Leaving greybox and occluded geometry in the final place — it costs memory/draw calls for surfaces nobody sees and can poke through finished art. Delete greybox + hidden geometry before the final lighting pass."],"provenance":{"sources":["https://create.roblox.com/docs/tutorials/curriculums/environmental-art","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/tutorials/curriculums/environmental-art/greybox-your-environment.md","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/tutorials/curriculums/environmental-art/greybox-your-environment.md","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/tutorials/curriculums/environmental-art/develop-polished-assets.md","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/tutorials/curriculums/environmental-art/develop-polished-assets.md","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/tutorials/curriculums/environmental-art/assemble-an-asset-library.md","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/tutorials/curriculums/environmental-art/assemble-an-asset-library.md","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/tutorials/curriculums/environmental-art/construct-your-world.md","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/tutorials/curriculums/core/building/create-an-environment-with-terrain.md","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/tutorials/curriculums/environmental-art/construct-your-world.md","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/tutorials/curriculums/environmental-art/construct-your-world.md","https://create.roblox.com/docs/environment","https://create.roblox.com/docs/environment/post-processing-effects","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/tutorials/curriculums/environmental-art/assemble-an-asset-library.md","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/tutorials/curriculums/environmental-art/optimize-your-experience.md","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-do-you-create-your-maps-from-start-to-finish-in-the-most-organised-way-possible/1312128","https://book.leveldesignbook.com/process/blockout/massing/composition","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/map-design-guidelines-make-your-maps-superior/1293781","https://create.roblox.com/docs/art/modeling/surface-appearance","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/recolorable-pbr-textures-surfaceappearance/1133067","https://create.roblox.com/docs/tutorials/curriculums/environmental-art/develop-polished-assets","https://create.roblox.com/docs/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/modeling/assemble-modular-environments","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-to-generate-a-random-rotation-and-much-more/1549051","https://create.roblox.com/docs/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/lighting/enhance-outdoor-environments","https://create.roblox.com/docs/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/lighting/enhance-outdoor-environments","https://create.roblox.com/docs/reference/engine/classes/ColorCorrectionEffect","https://create.roblox.com/docs/performance-optimization/improve","https://create.roblox.com/docs/performance-optimization/microprofiler/index","https://create.roblox.com/docs/tutorials/curriculums/environmental-art/develop-polished-assets","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/why-this-build-looks-bad/3117431","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/why-this-build-looks-bad/3117431","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/feedback-on-environment-something-doesnt-feel-right/3027158","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/cartoon-lighting-critiquereview/253439","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/cartoon-lighting-critiquereview/253439","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/cartoon-lighting-critiquereview/253439","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/my-build-looks-very-boring/1338211","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/my-build-looks-very-boring/1338211","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/feedback-on-my-games-environment/2945642","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/stylized-environment-help-and-feedback/2449090","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/my-game-looks-soooo-boring/2016096","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-DoOf32pkPo (Environmental Art); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=99C5K1cdql8 (Complete Guide to Lighting)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-DoOf32pkPo (Environmental Art); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B9nvNMNUiBE (Advanced)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-DoOf32pkPo (Environmental Art); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CippiATeZ54 (EASIEST Guide to Terrain); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N_UMED30lNc (Stylized Build)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N_UMED30lNc (Stylized Build); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-DoOf32pkPo (Environmental Art); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B9nvNMNUiBE (Advanced)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CippiATeZ54 (EASIEST Guide to Terrain); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-DoOf32pkPo (Environmental Art)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-DoOf32pkPo (Environmental Art); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CippiATeZ54 (EASIEST Guide to Terrain)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-DoOf32pkPo (Environmental Art)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gpsErt8wFqE (Terrain Textures); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-DoOf32pkPo (Environmental Art); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7GVA771n7ds (Medieval Tower)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CippiATeZ54 (EASIEST Guide to Terrain)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-DoOf32pkPo (Environmental Art); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N_UMED30lNc (Stylized Build)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-DoOf32pkPo (Environmental Art); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N_UMED30lNc (Stylized Build)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EAtKeffHRRI (Basics); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B9nvNMNUiBE (Advanced)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B9nvNMNUiBE (Advanced); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EAtKeffHRRI (Basics)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7GVA771n7ds (Medieval Tower)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B9nvNMNUiBE (Advanced); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7GVA771n7ds (Medieval Tower)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-DoOf32pkPo (Environmental Art); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N_UMED30lNc (Stylized Build)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N_UMED30lNc (Stylized Build); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-DoOf32pkPo (Environmental Art)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-DoOf32pkPo (Environmental Art); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N_UMED30lNc (Stylized Build)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-DoOf32pkPo (Environmental Art); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=99C5K1cdql8 (Complete Guide to Lighting)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=99C5K1cdql8 (Complete Guide to Lighting); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-DoOf32pkPo (Environmental Art)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=99C5K1cdql8 (Complete Guide to Lighting)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-DoOf32pkPo (Environmental Art); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B9nvNMNUiBE (Advanced); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EAtKeffHRRI (Basics)"],"confidence":"high","coverage":{"criteria_total":28,"criteria_addressed":28,"uncovered":[]}},"date":"2026-06-27"}]==],
