	[==[{"module":"vehicles","system_module":"vehicles","version":"1.0","rubric_version":"1.0","overview":"A great Roblox vehicle is a FORCE-DRIVEN physics body, never a part teleported by CFrame or pinned at constant velocity. Everything hangs off three commitments made in BUILD ORDER before any handling tuning: (1) a genuine multi-body assembly — a heavy chassis PrimaryPart with separate UNANCHORED wheels, deliberate mass/CoM/collision-group treatment; (2) a NAMED suspension architecture per corner — either a constraint axle (CylindricalConstraint slide + SpringConstraint) OR a raycast spring-damper force model — so the body dips/leans on real travel; (3) DRIVER network ownership handed over on sit so physics simulate locally with zero input latency. Only after the assembly moves as one owned rigid body do you layer the tuned drive/steer model (acceleration ramp toward a top speed, speed-scaled steer lock), an explicit per-wheel lateral grip/traction model (decompose tire force into Y suspension / X lateral / Z longitudinal and apply each independently), the multi-channel speed-sensation layer (velocity-scaled FOV + camera + audio, all read from AssemblyLinearVelocity), and slip-GATED skid effects (trails/smoke/SFX fire only when measured slip exceeds threshold). The exceptional tier deepens the SAME systems: load-sensitive grip (scale each tire's coefficient by its suspension compression so weight transfers), a Pacejka slip-curve + friction circle, a build-speed powertrain elaborated into a gearbox, braking-as-character on top of a real powertrain, server-side ownership validation with a multi-seat tradeoff, and frame-rate-independent force integration across 30-240 FPS. The agent has no dedicated vehicle tool — you build the entire thing from create_instance/set_property (chassis, wheels, constraints, attachments, VehicleSeat, particles, sounds) and run_luau (network ownership, the per-frame raycast/force/grip/steer loop, the speed-feedback loop, the server validator). spawn_rig only seeds a test driver; capture/capture_angles verify the body dips and the wheels sit on the ground.","tiers":[{"tier":"baseline","goal":"Stand up a real physics vehicle: a multi-body assembly (heavy chassis PrimaryPart + separate unanchored wheels, deliberate mass/CoM/collision groups), a NAMED per-wheel suspension architecture (constraint axle OR raycast spring-damper) so the body rides on real travel, a VehicleSeat as the input source, and explicit DRIVER network ownership handed over on sit — force-driven, never CFrame-teleported or constant-velocity.","steps":[{"id":"b1","order_index":1,"instruction":"Build the vehicle as a Model with a HEAVY chassis PrimaryPart, NOT a single welded/anchored part. With create_instance make a Model and add the body Part/MeshPart as the chassis; set_property Model.PrimaryPart to it. Tune its mass DELIBERATELY via CustomPhysicalProperties density (or add an internal low ballast part) so the center of mass sits LOW — this alone removes most tipping. Keep the chassis UNANCHORED (anchored parts can never receive network ownership and stay server-simulated/laggy). The body is NEVER moved by CFrame each frame — every later step drives it with forces/constraints. This is the single line that separates a real vehicle from the generic anti-pattern.","tools":["create_instance","set_property","run_luau"],"addresses_criteria":["veh-mass-com-collision-tuning","veh-nonwheeled-aero"],"pitfalls":"A body welded to a seat and CFrame-teleported (or pinned at constant velocity) is the exact anti-pattern the whole rubric rejects — it has no suspension travel and fails the lean/dip requirement entirely. Default density gives a too-heavy body the suspension can't hold up; tune mass via density and seat the CoM low. Forgetting PrimaryPart breaks later seat.Parent.PrimaryPart lookups."},{"id":"b2","order_index":2,"instruction":"Make it a genuine MULTI-BODY assembly: separate wheel parts, not one rigid block. With create_instance add four UNANCHORED Cylinder PHYSICS wheels (never the visual mesh — weld a CanCollide-off mesh onto each cylinder later). Give wheels REAL mass via CustomPhysicalProperties (massless/too-light wheels oscillate, clip through ground, and make springs freak out). Then put the chassis and wheels in separate collision groups via run_luau (PhysicsService) so wheels never collide with the body — skipping this is a classic source of a car that vibrates/hops/launches on spawn.","tools":["create_instance","set_property","run_luau"],"addresses_criteria":["veh-mass-com-collision-tuning"],"pitfalls":"Massless or CustomPhysicalProperties-light wheels oscillate and clip through the ground. Skipping the car<->wheel collision-group exclusion makes the wheels jam against the chassis and the car twitches/launches on spawn. A single rigid/welded part with no separate wheels fails the multi-body criterion outright."},{"id":"b3","order_index":3,"instruction":"Commit to a NAMED per-wheel suspension architecture and CONSTRAIN its travel — this is the load-bearing baseline decision. Pick ONE and name it: (A) CONSTRAINT AXLE: per wheel, create_instance two aligned Attachments (one on the body hub, one at the wheel center directly under it) + a CylindricalConstraint (Attachment0=body, Attachment1=wheel); its linear/slide axis is the vertical suspension travel (rotate the body attachment 90° on Z so travel is vertical), its angular axis is the wheel spin. set_property LimitsEnabled with UpperLimit/LowerLimit to BOUND travel (leave a small gap at the top, e.g. UpperLimit -1 not 0, so the spring always has room). OR (B) RAYCAST FORCE MODEL: no physical wheels — each corner Attachment casts a ray down -chassis.UpVector for RAYCAST_LENGTH and applies a spring-damper force (see g-suspension). Either way the body must ride on real travel that dips/leans, not a teleport.","tools":["create_instance","set_property","run_luau"],"addresses_criteria":["veh-chassis-physics-suspension","veh-suspension-architecture-robustness"],"pitfalls":"Misaligned hub/center attachments make wheels oscillate and wander. Put the slide on Attachment0 of the BODY, not relative to the wheel, or the suspension acts in the wrong frame. A wheel that slides left/right instead of up/down means you didn't rotate the body attachment 90° on Z. A single welded/CFrame-driven rigid part has NO suspension architecture and fails both criteria. Bad chassis geometry (impossible attachment angles) makes any suspension jitter — get geometry right before tuning."},{"id":"b4","order_index":4,"instruction":"Weld in a VehicleSeat as the INPUT source and know its hard limit. With create_instance add a VehicleSeat (NOT a normal Seat) and WeldConstraint it rigidly into the chassis (seat=Part0, body=Part1); place it LOW and centered for a low CoM. The VehicleSeat auto-welds the player on touch and auto-exposes ThrottleFloat (-1..1) and SteerFloat (-1..1) you read in script — no input code needed. Its built-in MaxSpeed/Torque/TurnSpeed only auto-drive Motor6D joints RIGIDLY connected to the seat, so for a constraint/raycast car you IGNORE the auto-drive and read the Float inputs yourself.","tools":["create_instance","set_property"],"addresses_criteria":["veh-drive-steer-model"],"pitfalls":"VehicleSeat auto-control only detects motors connected directly to the seat or through a rigid connection — a wheel reached through a HingeConstraint/CylindricalConstraint is NOT detected, so its built-in driving silently does nothing. That's why constraint/raycast cars read ThrottleFloat/SteerFloat and apply forces by hand. The integer Throttle/Steer are deprecated; use the Float versions. A seat buried inside the body can't be touched to sit — handle entry later with a ProximityPrompt."},{"id":"b5","order_index":5,"instruction":"Hand the DRIVER explicit network ownership on sit so physics simulate locally with zero input latency — wire this BEFORE any handling tuning. With run_luau, a SERVER Script watches the seat: connect VehicleSeat:GetPropertyChangedSignal('Occupant') (or Humanoid.Seated). On sit, resolve the player and call chassisPrimaryPart:SetNetworkOwner(player); because ownership applies to the whole UNANCHORED welded assembly, the entire car transfers and the driver simulates it locally. On exit (Occupant==nil) call SetNetworkOwnershipAuto() (or SetNetworkOwner(nil)). Must be called server-side; parts must be unanchored. Name-guard the seat so you don't hand ownership for every seat in the game.","tools":["run_luau"],"addresses_criteria":["veh-network-ownership-responsive"],"pitfalls":"Leaving DEFAULT auto-ownership means the server owns the car and the driver fights round-trip lag on every input (the laggy/rubber-bandy failure) — and the engine's auto-owner is often the closest/first-to-sit character, NOT the driver. Anchored parts can never change owner. SetNetworkOwner errors if called from the client or on an anchored part. Forgetting to reset on exit leaves the car owned by a player who left (jitter/stall limbo)."}]},{"tier":"good","goal":"Make the same physics FEEL like a tuned vehicle: per-corner suspension contact from real raycasts/constraints with a critical-damping tune, a built acceleration ramp toward top speed with bounded speed-scaled steering, an explicit per-wheel lateral grip/traction model (Y/X/Z decomposition) with controllable slip, velocity-scaled multi-channel speed sensation (FOV+camera+audio), skid effects GATED on measured slip, and a feel-and-framing layer a tank-style fixed-camera car cannot have.","steps":[{"id":"g1","order_index":1,"instruction":"Compute per-corner suspension contact from REAL raycasts/constraints and tune it from physics, not by guessing. RAYCAST route: each frame in a RunService.Heartbeat/PreSimulation loop (run_luau), per corner Attachment raycast straight down (workspace:Raycast, RaycastParams filtering OUT the car's own body); compression = SUSPENSION_HEIGHT - hitDistance; derive Stiffness from equilibrium so the car rides at target height (k = mass*gravity/(targetHeight*numWheels)); Damping = dampingRatio * 2*sqrt(massPerWheel*k) with ratio ~0.8 (HIGH damping kills bounce; under-damping pogos); totalForce = k*compression - damping*springVelocity, applied UP the ray via ApplyImpulseAtPosition (force*dt) or a per-wheel VectorForce. CONSTRAINT route: add a SpringConstraint across the same two attachments — FreeLength=ride height, Stiffness=(mass*g)/height/wheelCount, Damping=2*sqrt(mass*Stiffness)*0.3..0.9/wheelCount (critical damping). When zero rays hit, the car is airborne — skip suspension.","tools":["run_luau","create_instance","set_property"],"addresses_criteria":["veh-suspension-contact-quality","veh-chassis-physics-suspension","veh-suspension-architecture-robustness"],"pitfalls":"RAYCAST_LENGTH shorter than rest height = the car snaps/clips through ground or never finds it on bumps. Pure spring with no damping oscillates forever; LOW damping (the common mistake — people assume low=less bounce) leaves a pogo-stick. Forgetting to store last-frame length per wheel means the damper has no velocity to act on. ApplyImpulse must be scaled by dt or it explodes on frame-rate changes. Re-derive force when assembly mass changes (a passenger enters) or ride height jumps."},{"id":"g2","order_index":2,"instruction":"Build a TUNED drive + steering model — a built acceleration toward top speed, never instant-max or tank-turn. DRIVE (read VehicleSeat.ThrottleFloat on the OWNING client): ramp the powered wheels' target rather than snapping it — lerp/tween the motor AngularVelocity (or VectorForce magnitude) toward ThrottleFloat*targetSpin each frame for an acceleration CURVE, capped at top speed. Cap correctly: targetAngularVelocity = MaxSpeed/(wheel.Size.Y/2) (linear speed ÷ tyre radius → rad/s) so the MaxSpeed dial is truthful, NOT a hardcoded spin rate. For Motor wheels set AngularActuatorType=Motor + a FINITE MotorMaxAccel (~5-50, never inf), and negate one side's velocity for mirrored geometry. STEER (read SteerFloat): rotate the front attachments / drive a Servo HingeConstraint, but INTERPOLATE TargetAngle over frames (a SteerSpeed/tween ~0.4s) instead of snapping, and scale max steer lock DOWN with speed (effectiveSteer = Steer*maxAngle*(1 - clamp(speed/MaxSpeed,0,1)*0.6)) so it turns sharp slow and gentle fast.","tools":["run_luau","set_property"],"addresses_criteria":["veh-drive-steer-model"],"pitfalls":"Snapping AngularVelocity/TargetAngle to its goal = instant max speed and tank-style instant turning, which FAILS the brief and can flip the car at speed. MotorMaxAngularAcceleration left at inf gives unlimited grab and instability; MotorMaxTorque can't be negative (wrap throttle in math.abs). Forgetting the left/right sideSign makes one axle fight the other ('spins in place'). Hardcoding the spin rate makes MaxSpeed lie (reads 25, does 200); radians/sec vs studs/sec confusion is the #1 'why is my car insanely fast' bug. Ramp on the network-owner client, not the server."},{"id":"g3","order_index":3,"instruction":"Add an EXPLICIT per-wheel lateral grip/traction model — kill the 'sliding on ice' default friction. In the per-frame run_luau loop, per wheel compute the contact-point velocity = chassis.AssemblyLinearVelocity + chassis.AssemblyAngularVelocity:Cross(point - chassis.Position). Transform into the wheel/chassis LOCAL frame (CFrame:VectorToObjectSpace) and DECOMPOSE into Y (suspension, from g1), X (lateral/steering grip) and Z (longitudinal accel/brake) and apply each INDEPENDENTLY. For X: apply an opposing impulse along the wheel's right vector = -lateralSlipVel * gripCoefficient * normalLoad via ApplyImpulseAtPosition (or a per-wheel VectorForce) AT THE CONTACT POINT (not body center — that yaw torque is what makes drift read real). DRIFT: make gripCoefficient a function of slip/handbrake — high grip at low slip, drop it (especially rear) as slip angle grows so the tail steps out controllably. For constraint cars, the cheap baseline is wheel Friction ~1.5 + lower Elasticity, but the real model is the lateral counter-force.","tools":["run_luau","create_instance","set_property"],"addresses_criteria":["veh-grip-traction-drift"],"pitfalls":"A FIXED grip coefficient regardless of slip is the classic bug — either glued (can't drift) or slides forever like ice; grip must scale DOWN past a slip threshold. Forgetting normalLoad means airborne/light wheels still grab. Applying the lateral force at the body center instead of each contact point removes the yaw torque that makes drift feel real. Raycast wheels with NO lateral force slide like ice — you must explicitly kill side velocity."},{"id":"g4","order_index":4,"instruction":"COMMUNICATE speed through multiple channels all scaled to MEASURED velocity, not throttle and not a fixed camera. On the OWNING client each RenderStepped (run_luau), read chassis.AssemblyLinearVelocity.Magnitude and drive: (1) Camera FOV — LERP workspace.CurrentCamera.FieldOfView from ~70 toward ~90+ as speed rises (the single biggest 'fast' cue), using dt in the lerp so it's frame-rate-independent; (2) a slight speed-scaled camera pull-back + low-frequency shake (small CFrame offset) that grows with speed and is capped; (3) engine audio — map a looping Sound.PlaybackSpeed to speed/RPM with an idle floor and a redline ceiling (optionally crossfade idle/redline loops by Volume). Because the owning client simulates the physics, all of this is local and frame-perfect.","tools":["run_luau","create_instance","set_property"],"addresses_criteria":["veh-speed-sensation-feedback"],"pitfalls":"Hard-cutting FOV/audio instead of lerping is nauseating/jittery — always smooth toward the target. Tying FOV/pitch to THROTTLE instead of real velocity widens it even against a wall and desyncs from motion (coasting should still read fast). Maxing shake/FOV makes high speed unreadable and sickening — keep the curve gentle and capped. No upper clamp = the engine pitch screeches at high speed. Doing camera/FOV off the owning client adds latency and stutter."},{"id":"g5","order_index":5,"instruction":"Gate skid effects on MEASURED slip so they are a truthful readout, not a keypress toggle. Reuse the SAME lateral-slip value the tire model computes in g3: when |lateralVelocity| (or slip angle / grip-breakaway) exceeds a threshold, Enable a ParticleEmitter (tire smoke) at the rear-wheel CONTACT POINT, stamp skid-mark Decals/Trails behind the wheels, and play a skid SFX; DISABLE all of them when grip is regained. Scale emitter Rate and particle Size with the magnitude of slip (gentle drift smokes lightly, full handbrake slide smokes hard). Place smoke/marks at the raycast hit position so they stay on the ground. Clean up skid decals over time so they don't leak.","tools":["run_luau","create_instance","set_property"],"addresses_criteria":["veh-slip-gated-effects"],"pitfalls":"Emitting smoke constantly (a cosmetic keypress toggle, not gated on real slip) looks fake and tanks performance — drive it off the same slip value the tire model uses. Particles spawned at the wheel CENTER instead of the ground contact float in the air. Skid decals never cleaned up leak instances over a session. Effects driven by a handbrake KEY rather than measured slip fail the 'truthful readout' bar even if the key usually correlates."},{"id":"g6","order_index":6,"instruction":"Add the FEEL-AND-FRAMING layer that a tank-on-rails fixed-camera car cannot have — on top of the real grip model from g3 so slip and counter-steer are meaningful. The FLOOR is that steering already comes from an actual grip/traction model (g3 makes slip and counter-steer real) AND the camera responds to motion (g4 ties FOV/shake/pull-back to measured velocity). Layer in: tighten the camera follow and add a small body/camera ROLL on hard cornering (read steer input + lateral slip), and a slight look-into-the-turn. Optionally add an automatic counter-steer assist (compare facing vs velocity vector and nudge steering toward velocity when the rear steps out) so a physically-correct car stays drivable for casual players.","tools":["run_luau","set_property"],"addresses_criteria":["veh-counter-steer-camera-feel","veh-speed-sensation-feedback"],"pitfalls":"A fixed default camera over a tank-style instant-turn car cannot satisfy this — the floor REQUIRES the real grip model (g3) plus a motion-responsive camera (g4) before the feel layer means anything. Over-rolling the camera on corners is disorienting; keep it subtle. A physically-correct car with no counter-steer assist frustrates casual players (they spin out) — add the assist but don't let it fully autopilot the steering."}]},{"tier":"exceptional","goal":"Deepen the SAME systems into emergent, fair, robust handling: load-sensitive grip with weight transfer, a Pacejka slip-curve + friction circle elaborating the existing tire model, a build-speed powertrain elaborated into a gearbox, braking-as-character on top of a real powertrain, server-validated driver ownership with a deliberate multi-seat tradeoff, frame-rate-independent integration across 30-240 FPS, and a force-native non-wheeled (boat/hover/aircraft) variant.","steps":[{"id":"e1","order_index":1,"instruction":"Make grip LOAD-SENSITIVE so weight transfer emerges. In the per-wheel loop (run_luau), scale each tire's grip coefficient (and its force budget) by that wheel's CURRENT suspension load — the spring compression from the constraint or the suspension force magnitude from the raycast (g1). Now braking dives weight onto the front tires (they bite more, rear bites less), cornering loads the outer wheels, and throttle squats the rear — redistributing which wheels grip and producing emergent throttle/inertia oversteer-understeer instead of flat on-rails grip on all four.","tools":["run_luau"],"addresses_criteria":["veh-load-sensitive-grip-weight-transfer"],"pitfalls":"Treating tire load as constant (flat identical grip on all four wheels) means no dive/squat/weight-transfer and the car feels on rails — the criterion explicitly wants grip scaled by live spring compression. Forgetting normalLoad on airborne/unloaded wheels lets light wheels grab. This PRESUPPOSES the explicit tire model from g3 already exists — without it there is no coefficient to scale."},{"id":"e2","order_index":2,"instruction":"Elaborate the EXISTING tire model into a slip-curve + friction circle (Pacejka). PRESUPPOSES g3's explicit grip model exists — this is its exceptional elaboration, not a from-scratch system. Per wheel compute slip angle (angle between wheel forward and its point-velocity) and feed Pacejka: D*sin(C*atan(B*x - E*(B*x - atan(B*x)))) (starter B=1, C=2.8, D=1.6, E=1.2) so grip RISES to peak then FALLS OFF past the slip limit (what makes the back step out under power). Couple it to e1's load. Then enforce a shared longitudinal+lateral budget — clamp the combined demand to a friction CIRCLE so throttle-while-cornering breaks traction — NOT a fixed coefficient or a hard F=uN clamp. Tune the coefficients visually (e.g. in Desmos).","tools":["run_luau"],"addresses_criteria":["veh-friction-circle-pacejka","veh-grip-traction-drift"],"pitfalls":"A fixed friction coefficient or a hard F=uN clamp fails this — it must be a slip-based curve with a shared friction-circle budget. Un-clamped friction can spike to absurd numbers and fling the car; always clamp to the circle. This is the elaboration of an EXISTING model — building Pacejka with no prior explicit tire model would skip the floor it presupposes."},{"id":"e3","order_index":3,"instruction":"Elaborate the build-speed powertrain into a real DRIVETRAIN/GEARBOX. PARTIAL FLOOR (must already be true from g2): throttle produces a BUILT acceleration — a torque/AngularVelocity curve feeding POWERED wheels toward a top speed, not an instant constant top speed. To clear the exceptional bar, elaborate that into a powertrain: an engine torque curve vs RPM, discrete gear ratios (each gear maps wheel-spin to an RPM band), shift points (up/down), and a final-drive ratio scaling MotorMaxTorque/target spin per gear. Choose a drive layout (RWD = power rear wheels only, AWD = all four) as a deliberate character decision. Wheel-spin reads back into RPM so acceleration tapers near each gear's top and resumes after a shift.","tools":["run_luau","set_property"],"addresses_criteria":["veh-drivetrain-gearbox","veh-drive-steer-model"],"pitfalls":"A build whose throttle sets an instant constant top speed (velocity/CFrame) has NOT even cleared the partial floor and cannot reach this tier. A single fixed acceleration with no gears/ratios is only the floor, not the exceptional elaboration. Keep the gearbox data-driven (a ratios table) rather than hardcoded per-vehicle."},{"id":"e4","order_index":4,"instruction":"Give BRAKING and drivetrain layout CHARACTER — presupposes BOTH a real powertrain AND a real braking model exist. FLOOR: a real powertrain (e3) plus a real braking model — brake = drive the powered wheels' target toward 0 with high MotorMaxTorque (or a strong reverse longitudinal force), handbrake = apply that to the REAR wheels only (which, with e1/e2, locks the rear and lets the tail slide). On that floor, add character: front/rear brake bias, an engine-brake/idle torque so the car holds on hills and decelerates naturally (default a small MotorMaxTorque when throttle is released), and per-layout behavior (RWD vs AWD vs FWD feel). Tune brake force and bias so the car dives and stays stable under heavy braking rather than spinning.","tools":["run_luau","set_property"],"addresses_criteria":["veh-braking-drivetrain-character","veh-drivetrain-gearbox"],"pitfalls":"This PRESUPPOSES both a real powertrain and a real braking model — bolting brake character onto a constant-velocity car fails. Zero idle/engine-brake torque makes the car roll away on slopes and coast forever ('floaty, no engine'); too high a baseline makes it impossible to coast. A handbrake applied to all four wheels won't step the tail out — rear-only."},{"id":"e5","order_index":5,"instruction":"GUARD the driver-owned physics server-side and handle the multi-occupant tradeoff deliberately. PRESUPPOSES explicit driver ownership (b5) with a server-validation intent. On the server (run_luau), sample the chassis position every ~1s, compute distance traveled vs the physically-possible max (topSpeed*dt + margin); if exceeded, CFrame-snap the car back to the last valid position (validate over a WINDOW, not a single frame, so lag spikes don't rubber-band legit drivers). Put the vehicle in its own CollisionGroup so an owned car can't be weaponized to fling players. Handle the MULTI-SEAT tradeoff explicitly: only the DRIVER seat grants network ownership (passengers never take it), and decide what happens to ownership on driver swap/exit — re-assign to the new driver or back to the server, never leave it in no-owner limbo.","tools":["run_luau"],"addresses_criteria":["veh-ownership-validation-multiseat","veh-network-ownership-responsive"],"pitfalls":"Trusting the client outright lets exploiters teleport/speed/fling the owned car. Over-aggressive single-frame snap-back rubber-bands legit drivers on lag — validate over a window with a generous margin. This PRESUPPOSES b5's explicit driver ownership; without it there's nothing to validate. Handing ownership to a passenger or leaving it in limbo on driver swap is the multi-seat failure."},{"id":"e6","order_index":6,"instruction":"Make the physics FRAME-RATE INDEPENDENT across 30-240 FPS — presupposes there IS a force/integration loop (g1-g3) to make independent. In the per-frame run_luau loop, drive every force/impulse by the real dt: impulse = force*dt for ApplyImpulseAtPosition, integrate spring/damper velocities with dt, and lerp camera/FOV/steer with dt-aware factors. For stability under variable frame time use a FIXED-STEP accumulator (accumulate dt, run the physics substep at a constant timestep, interpolate the remainder) so the suspension/grip/Pacejka forces behave identically at 30 and 240 FPS. Test at capped and uncapped frame rates and confirm ride height, top speed, and grip don't change.","tools":["run_luau"],"addresses_criteria":["veh-framerate-independence"],"pitfalls":"Applying forces/impulses without dt (or with raw per-frame deltas) makes the car accelerate/bounce differently at 30 vs 240 FPS and can explode on frame-rate changes. Pacejka/sway-bar forces are notoriously FPS-sensitive — the friction-circle build that jitters outside 40-60 FPS is the classic failure; use a fixed-step accumulator. This PRESUPPOSES a force/integration loop exists — a CFrame-teleported body has nothing to make frame-independent."},{"id":"e7","order_index":7,"instruction":"Provide a FORCE-NATIVE non-wheeled variant (boat/hover/aircraft) so the force-driven-mover commitment holds for any vehicle class. Keep the SAME spine — VehicleSeat input + driver SetNetworkOwner + owning-client loop — and swap the ground suspension for a buoyancy/lift/hover force. THRUST: a VectorForce (object-space, along the hull's CFrame.LookVector*throttle*speed) so it rotates WITH the craft. STEER/YAW: an AngularVelocity constraint about the up axis (watch RelativeTo). STABILIZE: AlignOrientation (the modern replacement for deprecated BodyGyro) to keep it upright. FLOAT: boats add an upward buoyancy VectorForce below the waterline (several sample points for tilt); hovercraft a constant hover force ≈ weight; aircraft a lift force scaled by forward speed (per-part GetVelocityAtPosition + dot products for independent wing forces / angular drag). Motion comes from physics-native force/velocity controllers, NEVER per-frame teleporting the body.","tools":["run_luau","create_instance","set_property"],"addresses_criteria":["veh-nonwheeled-aero","veh-drive-steer-model"],"pitfalls":"A WORLD-space VectorForce doesn't rotate with the craft — apply thrust in the hull's CFrame frame or it keeps pushing the original direction after you turn (object-space Z is reversed — negate thrust). BodyGyro/BodyVelocity are deprecated; use AlignOrientation/LinearVelocity. An unset AngularVelocity.RelativeTo spins it in the wrong frame. Using AssemblyLinearVelocity instead of per-part GetVelocityAtPosition gives both wings identical force (no roll/yaw authority). Any per-frame CFrame teleport of the body fails the force-native floor entirely."}]}],"killer_systems":[{"name":"Named per-wheel suspension architecture (constraint axle OR raycast spring-damper)","what":"The chassis rides on real per-corner suspension travel — either a CylindricalConstraint slide axis + SpringConstraint, or a raycast spring-damper force applied per corner — so the body visibly dips under braking, leans in corners, and absorbs bumps. This is the floor that separates a real vehicle from a CFrame-teleported/constant-velocity block.","how":"Constraint route: per wheel, two aligned Attachments (body hub + wheel center) → CylindricalConstraint (slide=suspension travel with LimitsEnabled/Upper/LowerLimit bounding it, angular=wheel spin) + a SpringConstraint across the same attachments with FreeLength=ride height, Stiffness=(mass*g)/height/wheelCount, Damping=2*sqrt(mass*Stiffness)*0.3..0.9/wheelCount (critical damping — HIGH damping, not low). Raycast route: each Heartbeat cast down -UpVector per corner, compression=SUSPENSION_HEIGHT-hitDistance, force=k*compression-damping*springVel applied via ApplyImpulseAtPosition(force*dt) or a per-wheel VectorForce; filter the car's own body out of the ray; skip suspension when no ray hits (airborne). Build via create_instance/set_property + the run_luau per-frame loop."},{"name":"Per-wheel tire force model (Y/X/Z decomposition with slip)","what":"Each tire's force is computed in its own local frame and decomposed into Y (suspension), X (lateral/steering grip) and Z (longitudinal accel/brake), applied INDEPENDENTLY at the contact point — replacing Roblox default part-vs-part friction (slides like ice or grabs unpredictably) with a real traction model that enables controllable drift.","how":"In the per-frame run_luau loop, contactVel = AssemblyLinearVelocity + AssemblyAngularVelocity:Cross(point - chassis.Position); CFrame:VectorToObjectSpace into the wheel frame; the X (right) component is lateral slip → apply -slip*gripCoef*normalLoad along the wheel right vector via ApplyImpulseAtPosition AT THE CONTACT POINT (the yaw torque sells the drift). gripCoef falls off past a slip threshold (drift). Exceptional: scale gripCoef by live suspension load (weight transfer) and replace the linear falloff with a Pacejka slip-curve + a friction-circle clamp on combined X/Z demand."},{"name":"Driver network ownership + server validation","what":"Network ownership of the whole unanchored assembly is handed to the DRIVER on sit so steering/physics simulate locally with zero input latency (vs server-owned round-trip lag or no-owner jitter), while the server stays the authority on legitimacy and the multi-seat tradeoff is handled deliberately.","how":"A server Script (run_luau) watches VehicleSeat:GetPropertyChangedSignal('Occupant'); on sit, chassisPrimaryPart:SetNetworkOwner(player); on exit, SetNetworkOwnershipAuto(). Only the DRIVER seat grants ownership; on driver swap re-assign or return to server (never limbo). Server-side validation: sample position every ~1s, compare distance vs topSpeed*dt+margin over a WINDOW, CFrame-snap back on violation; put the vehicle in its own CollisionGroup so it can't be weaponized. Parts must be unanchored; calls are server-side only."},{"name":"Tuned drive + steer model with truthful top speed","what":"Throttle produces a BUILT acceleration (a ramp toward top speed, not instant-max), steering is BOUNDED and speed-scaled (sharp slow, gentle fast, interpolated not snapped), and the MaxSpeed dial actually matches real speed — the deliberate model that replaces instant-max-speed and tank-style instant turning.","how":"On the owning client (run_luau), lerp/tween the motor AngularVelocity (or force magnitude) toward ThrottleFloat*target each frame, capped at targetAngularVelocity = MaxSpeed/(wheel.Size.Y/2) so MaxSpeed is truthful; Motor wheels use AngularActuatorType=Motor + finite MotorMaxAngularAcceleration + math.abs(throttle)*torque with one side's velocity negated. Steering interpolates TargetAngle (Servo HingeConstraint) or rotates the front attachments over ~0.4s and scales max lock down with speed. Exceptional: elaborate the drive ramp into a gearbox (torque curve + gear ratios + shift points) and add braking-as-character (rear-only handbrake, engine-brake idle torque, brake bias)."},{"name":"Velocity-scaled speed-sensation + slip-gated effects bus","what":"Speed is communicated through multiple channels all read from the vehicle's MEASURED AssemblyLinearVelocity (FOV widens on a curve, camera pulls back/shakes, engine pitch rises), and skid trails/smoke/SFX fire ONLY when measured slip exceeds threshold — a truthful readout of physics state, not a fixed camera or a cosmetic keypress toggle.","how":"On the owning client each RenderStepped (run_luau): lerp Camera.FieldOfView ~70→~90+ by speed (dt-aware, capped), grow a small camera pull-back + shake with speed, map Sound.PlaybackSpeed to speed/RPM between an idle floor and redline ceiling. Reuse the tire model's lateral-slip value to gate ParticleEmitter smoke + skid Decals/Trails + skid SFX at the rear-wheel CONTACT point, scaling emit Rate/Size with slip magnitude and disabling on regrip; clean up decals over time. Build emitters/sounds via create_instance/set_property."}],"common_pitfalls":["The cardinal sin: moving the body by CFrame teleport (or pinning it at constant velocity / a single linear Tween) instead of physics forces. It has no suspension travel, no lean/dip, and fails nearly every criterion. Drive the chassis with constraints/forces and let it ride on real per-wheel suspension.","Anchored or welded-into-one-rigid-part chassis: anchored parts can NEVER receive network ownership (stay server-laggy), and a single rigid block fails the multi-body/suspension criteria. Keep the chassis and separate wheels unanchored; give both real mass.","Leaving DEFAULT network ownership (server-owned) — every input round-trips and the car feels laggy/rubber-bandy — or no-owner limbo (jitter/stall). Explicitly SetNetworkOwner(driver) on sit and reset on exit; only the driver seat grants it.","Snapping motor speed/steer angle to the goal = instant max speed + tank-style instant turning (the brief's headline failure). Ramp the throttle toward top speed (finite MotorMaxAngularAcceleration), interpolate steering, and scale steer lock down with speed.","Hardcoding the wheel spin rate so the MaxSpeed property lies (reads 25, does 200); radians/sec vs studs/sec confusion is the #1 'why is my car insanely fast' bug. Cap at MaxSpeed/(wheel radius).","Under-damped suspension (assuming low damping = less bounce — it's the opposite): the chassis pogos forever and reads as fake. Derive Damping from critical-damping (HIGH relative to Stiffness) and confirm it settles in one bounce after a drop.","RAYCAST_LENGTH shorter than rest height (car clips through / never finds ground), not filtering the car's own body out of the suspension ray (it detects itself as the floor and climbs itself), or applying impulse without *dt (explodes on frame-rate change).","A FIXED lateral grip coefficient regardless of slip — glued (can't drift) or slides like ice forever. Grip must rise then fall past a slip threshold; raycast cars must explicitly cancel side velocity or they have zero traction.","Applying lateral grip force at the body center instead of each contact point — removes the yaw torque that makes drift feel real; and forgetting normalLoad lets airborne/light wheels grab.","Speed feedback tied to THROTTLE instead of measured velocity (FOV widens against a wall, coasting reads slow), or hard-cut instead of lerped (nauseating), or maxed/uncapped (unreadable, screeching audio). Read AssemblyLinearVelocity, lerp dt-aware, cap the curve.","Skid smoke/marks emitted constantly or off a handbrake KEY rather than gated on the same measured slip the tire model computes — looks fake, tanks performance, leaks un-cleaned decals. Gate on slip, place at the ground contact point, clean up.","Massless/too-light wheels and skipped car<->wheel collision groups (or missing raycast filter) — the car oscillates, hops, and launches on spawn; bad chassis geometry makes any suspension jitter no matter how you tune damping.","Exceptional criteria built without their floor: a friction circle / gearbox / braking-character / ownership validation / framerate-independence each PRESUPPOSES the underlying explicit tire model, build-speed powertrain, braking model, driver ownership, and force loop already exist — skipping the floor fails the elaboration.","Porting BeamNG/real-world numbers 1:1 (too floaty/drifty) and changing Workspace.Gravity without re-tuning suspension; loading a fresh AnimationTrack per steering frame (memory leak); building proportions by eye instead of real-world dimensions (doll-house scale)."],"provenance":{"sources":["https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/physics/network-ownership.md","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-to-implement-vehicle-mechanics-using-constraints/3575431","https://robloxapi.github.io/ref/class/CylindricalConstraint.html","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/reference/engine/classes/VehicleSeat.yaml","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Arctxrus/Roblox-Dynamic-Suspension/main/RaycastSuspension.lua","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-could-i-change-my-raycast-cars-grip-based-on-its-drift-angle/1457331","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/boat-and-velocity/2741308","https://create.roblox.com/docs/reference/engine/classes/VehicleSeat","https://robloxapi.github.io/ref/class/CylindricalConstraint","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/outdated-how-to-properly-tune-spring-constraints-for-cars-critical-damping-formula/2007050","https://github.com/Arctxrus/Roblox-Dynamic-Suspension","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/a-vehicle-chassis-with-the-pacejka-friction-circle-raycast-suspension-gearbox/2400821","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-could-i-change-my-raycast-cars-grip-based-on-its-drift-angle/1457331","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/realistic-vehicleseat-acceleration/2391414","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/camera-fov-handler-based-on-velocity/1910355","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-to-set-sound-pitch-based-on-car-speed/820152","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/struggling-to-implement-an-intense-vehicle-camera-fov-camera-shake-motion-blur/2508964","https://create.roblox.com/docs/physics/network-ownership","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/reducing-vehicle-lag-with-setnetworkownerplayer-but-worried-about-exploits/1388561","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/feedback-on-car-suspension/2540574","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/my-most-realistic-car-chassis-yet-a-mixture-of-constraints-and-scripted-suspension/1547681","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/a-vehicle-chassis-with-the-pacejka-friction-circle-raycast-suspension-gearbox/2400821","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/a-vehicle-chassis-with-the-pacejka-friction-circle-raycast-suspension-gearbox/2400821","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/a-vehicle-chassis-with-the-pacejka-friction-circle-raycast-suspension-gearbox/2400821","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/a-vehicle-chassis-with-the-pacejka-friction-circle-raycast-suspension-gearbox/2400821","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/feedback-on-my-vehicle-system/2510291","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/tweening-vehicle-looks-very-bad/3056458","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/vehicle-chassis-suspension-freaking-out/1672973","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/feedback-on-my-vehicle-system/2510291","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/are-proportions-off-or-is-it-simply-the-style-of-building-im-making/275601","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/why-this-build-looks-bad/3117431","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/feedback-on-car-models/2182000","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/feedback-on-my-realistic-car-suspension/1652137","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FVOowr2DXbM (Suphi Kaner, Cars With Suspension, 01:33-03:04); corroborated https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WSxGsKypFCw (Krokosha666, Raycast Suspension 2, 00:00-03:23 chassis+PrimaryPart)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FVOowr2DXbM (Suphi Kaner, 03:04-09:09)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FVOowr2DXbM (Suphi Kaner, 06:06-07:37); raycast-filter corroboration https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WSxGsKypFCw (Krokosha666, 04:59-05:44)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FVOowr2DXbM (Suphi Kaner, 09:09-13:43 limits+spring, 21:19 rear FreeLength); raycast spring+damper https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WSxGsKypFCw (Krokosha666, 00:47-02:01); hinge+spring+prismatic variant https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oxVaINPVXl4 (BlockyScript, 01:00-02:03)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FVOowr2DXbM (Suphi Kaner, 15:14-22:53); VehicleSeat foundation https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Zs-QXSEvQv4 (Roblox official, Making a Car: VehicleSeat)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FVOowr2DXbM (Suphi Kaner, 22:53-27:26); aircraft identical pattern https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V3nKBCnJOKQ (Suphi Kaner, Flight Physics, 13:09-16:11); manual per-car ownership https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WSxGsKypFCw (Krokosha666, 03:23-04:10)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FVOowr2DXbM (Suphi Kaner, 28:56-36:32, connection-leak fix at 33:28-34:58)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FVOowr2DXbM (Suphi Kaner, 27:26-28:56, 37:36-42:41); hinge-motor + sign-flip https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Zs-QXSEvQv4 (Roblox official, 03:12-04:13); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oxVaINPVXl4 (BlockyScript, 08:09-09:09 negate right wheels)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FVOowr2DXbM (Suphi Kaner, 42:41-45:43); radians/sec caveat https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Zs-QXSEvQv4 (Roblox official, 01:07-01:38)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FVOowr2DXbM (Suphi Kaner, 27:26-28:56 handbrake-torque, 40:23-41:08 idle-brake default)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FVOowr2DXbM (Suphi Kaner, 45:43-53:26, tween at 50:24); servo-steer https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Zs-QXSEvQv4 (Roblox official, 02:42-03:12) and https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=C9yr8CUeROk (Roblox official, Joints, 01:36-02:38); servo+drift https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oxVaINPVXl4 (BlockyScript, 02:03-03:05)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FVOowr2DXbM (Suphi Kaner, 13:43-15:14 friction/elasticity); lateral-velocity grip https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WSxGsKypFCw (Krokosha666, 02:01-02:37); drift-key https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oxVaINPVXl4 (BlockyScript, 10:10-16:23)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FVOowr2DXbM (Suphi Kaner, 53:26-1:06:59 center-of-mass, prompt, multi-attachment exit)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WSxGsKypFCw (Krokosha666, 04:59-06:30 visual wheels in separate loop over folder)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V3nKBCnJOKQ (Suphi Kaner, Flight Physics, 12:08-1:06:41; per-part velocity fix 47:14-48:28, occluded-face raycast 49:00-58:29, global wind 58:29, air-density falloff 1:00:31-1:04:31)"],"confidence":"high","coverage":{"criteria_total":17,"criteria_addressed":17,"uncovered":[]}}}]==],
	-- vfx.guide.json
	[==[{"module":"vfx","system_module":"vfx","version":"1.0","rubric_version":"1.0","overview":"A great Roblox VFX is never one ParticleEmitter doing everything — it is a coordinated STACK of role-separated layers (a bright core/flash, a soft glow/haze, fast sparks/debris, slow rising smoke, a directed/linear element, a synced light), each owning ONE visual job, each driven by authored NumberSequence/ColorSequence curves over particle life, fired as a triggered one-shot burst with a real lifecycle (Emit -> place -> replicate -> clean up), and finished with a lighting/post/game-feel juice layer. Because the agent has no native particle editor, you author every layer programmatically: create_instance the anchor Part + Attachments + each emitter/Beam/Trail/PointLight, then set_property (or one run_luau block) to assign NumberSequence/ColorSequence, LightEmission/LightInfluence, Drag/Acceleration, ZOffset, and Emit()/Debris lifecycle. Build the scaffold first, then layer roles, then curve and differentiate motion per layer, then choreograph timed phases and post. Verify visually with capture/capture_angles and grade with evaluate against the vfx rubric, then refine. The spine that separates an above-the-mean effect from a recolored default is: MULTIPLE role-separated layers + SHAPED lifetime curves + ADDITIVE self-lit authored textures + BOUNDED, actively-cleaned one-shot lifecycle.","killer_systems":[{"name":"Layered role-separated composition","what":"The effect is a coordinated SET of 3-5 emitters/elements, each owning ONE job (bright core flash + glow/haze + fast sparks/debris + slow rising smoke + a synced light + a directed/linear element), never a single ParticleEmitter trying to be blast, smoke and debris at once.","how":"create_instance an invisible anchor Part (Transparency=1, Anchored, CanCollide=false) and an Attachment in it; then create_instance one ParticleEmitter per role parented to the Attachment, plus a Beam/Trail for the directed element and a PointLight sibling for the synced light. Give each layer its OWN Size/Transparency/Color sequences, Rate, Lifetime, Speed, Drag, Acceleration via set_property. Depth-sort with ZOffset (core in front high ZOffset, smoke behind low/negative). A single-emitter effect FAILS this killer system by construction."},{"name":"Authored lifetime curves","what":"Size, Transparency and Color are driven by eased NumberSequence/ColorSequence curves OVER each particle's life (fade-in at birth, fade-out at death, grow-or-shrink Size, a physically-motivated Color ramp) with per-particle envelope randomness — never left at constant values.","how":"set_property Transparency to a NumberSequence that starts near 1, dips toward 0 mid-life, returns to 1 at death (hides spawn/despawn pops); Size to a grow-then-shrink or grow curve; Color to a ColorSequence ramp (e.g. bright yellow -> orange -> dark red so it encodes cooling). Use the optional 3rd 'envelope' arg on NumberSequenceKeypoint for per-particle variety. First/last keypoint times MUST be exactly 0 and 1. Build these as Luau sequence literals in a run_luau block since structured set_property can't express a multi-keypoint curve easily."},{"name":"Additive self-lit authored texture","what":"Energy/fire/magic layers are self-lit (additive blending + low light influence + brightness) and use a real authored sprite — not the default white square at constant lighting.","how":"set_property LightEmission toward 1 (additive: overlapping particles brighten/bloom), LightInfluence toward 0 (so dark scenes don't mute the color), and raise Brightness. Author a grayscale, soft-edged, transparent-background sprite via paint_texture (or use a crafted asset id) and drive ALL hue through the Color ColorSequence — never bake color into the texture. Design the texture on black because additive ignores black pixels."},{"name":"Per-layer differentiated motion","what":"Each role-separated layer moves on its OWN motion profile — fast outward sparks that shoot then decelerate, slow buoyant rising smoke, a stationary core flash — rather than every particle drifting on one shared profile.","how":"Per emitter, set_property a distinct Speed (NumberRange), Acceleration (Vector3: negative Y for gravity sparks, positive Y for rising smoke), and Drag (high Drag ~10 makes a fast burst flare then decelerate; ~0 lets sparks fly). Sparks: high Speed + wide SpreadAngle + high Drag. Smoke: low Speed + upward Acceleration + long Lifetime. Core: Speed ~0.001 (NOT 0, which hides velocity-oriented particles) so it shimmers in place."},{"name":"Triggered one-shot lifecycle with post/juice","what":"The effect is a discrete triggerable one-shot fired on a precise event as an Emit()/clone burst at a placed position, choreographed as timed phases (flash, shockwave, debris, smoke, embers) with a synced one-frame PointLight flash + camera shake + screen flash, then actively torn down (Debris/disable/Destroy) — not a constant-Rate emitter left running.","how":"Set each emitter Enabled=false (idle). In a run_luau script: clone or place the rig at the raycast hit / Attachment, task.delay the phases (Emit core+flash at t=0, shockwave Beam + sparks at t=0.02, enable smoke ~0.3s then disable, embers linger), tween a sibling PointLight Brightness from a spike to 0 over ~0.15s, fire a client camera shake + a brief color-matched ColorCorrection screen flash, then Debris:AddItem the whole rig with delay >= max Lifetime so in-flight particles finish before teardown. Run impact VFX on the SERVER (or a RemoteEvent fan-out) so all players see it."}],"common_pitfalls":["Single ParticleEmitter trying to be the whole effect — the #1 amateur tell. Blast, smoke, sparks and glow each need their own emitter with their own properties; one emitter can only hold one Size/Color/motion profile.","Flat constant Size/Transparency/Color (a single-keypoint sequence) — particles pop in and out abruptly and read as a recolored default. Always taper Transparency toward 1 at BOTH ends and ramp Color over life.","Baking color into the texture instead of using a grayscale sprite + Color ColorSequence — yields muddy, monochromatic, stock-looking particles and kills runtime color control.","Leaving LightInfluence high / LightEmission low on energy effects — dark scene lighting mutes the color and there's no glow. Drop LightInfluence to ~0 and push LightEmission toward 1 plus Brightness for energy.","Overdraw: stacking many large, high-Rate, semi-transparent layers (big glow + big smoke at high Rate) tanks GPU fill-rate and looks muddy. Keep big transparent layers low-Rate; reserve high counts for tiny sparks; cap total on-screen particles.","Unbounded emission / no cleanup — a constant-Rate one-shot left Enabled, or a rig never destroyed, leaks instances and particles forever. Idle bursts at Enabled=false, bound Rate/Lifetime/Emit-count, and Debris:AddItem the rig after a delay >= max Lifetime.","Destroying the rig immediately after Emit() — cuts in-flight particles mid-life. Disable the emitter and let Lifetime expire (or Debris with a delay >= max Lifetime) instead of an instant Destroy.","Parenting a ParticleEmitter to a big Part instead of an Attachment — particles scatter randomly inside the part's bounding box. Parent to an Attachment for a precise point origin (and avoid Sphere/Cylinder spawn shapes on an Attachment — only Box/Disc render correctly there).","Beam/Trail with one or missing attachment — renders nothing. Both Attachment0 and Attachment1 must exist and be separated; Trail width IS the inter-attachment distance, and a Trail only renders while its part MOVES.","Firing everything in one frame / no anticipation — a heavy impact reads flat. Choreograph discrete TIMED phases on their own task.delay offsets (flash -> shockwave -> debris -> smoke -> embers) with a synced one-frame light flash.","Running impact VFX in a LocalScript — only the local player sees it. Cosmetic muzzle/cast can be client-side for responsiveness, but shared impacts must run on the SERVER or via a RemoteEvent fan-out.","Tuning only in the edit viewport — runtime lighting, camera and timing differ. Verify with capture/capture_angles (and a playtest), then iterate Rate/Size/Speed/Transparency in small passes against the contrast/impact principle."],"tiers":[{"tier":"baseline","goal":"A composited, curve-driven, self-lit, bounded one-shot — clears the four baseline killer systems (layered composition, lifetime curves, additive+texture, performance budget).","steps":[{"id":"vfx-scaffold-anchor-attachment","order_index":1,"instruction":"Build the spatial scaffold as the explicit mount for a ROLE-SEPARATED multi-emitter stack, not a single generic emission point. First create_instance an anchor Part (className='Part') with Transparency=1, Anchored=true, CanCollide=false, parented under a named workspace effects folder. Then, BEFORE authoring any look, name the >=3 cooperating layers this rig will host (e.g. core flash + glow/haze + fast sparks/debris + slow rising smoke + synced light) and provision the spatial origins each needs: create_instance a primary Attachment as the shared point origin, AND a dedicated Attachment per layer that needs a distinct spatial role — at minimum a SECOND Attachment offset from the first to define the axis/direction for the directional/linear layer (sparks/debris/shockwave edge), so the directed role emits along a real vector rather than omnidirectionally from the same point as the diffuse body. State which emitter role will parent to which Attachment. This makes the scaffold encode per-layer spatial structure (point origins for diffuse layers, a directional axis for the linear layer, a near-static origin for the core) that a single bounding-box-parented emitter cannot, and makes the whole stack track/rotate as one unit with whatever it is later attached to. Pitfalls: parenting any emitter directly to the large Part scatters its particles randomly through the bounding box — every emitter must parent to an Attachment for a precise origin; a single shared Attachment for all layers collapses the directional role into the same omnidirectional point as the diffuse body (give the directed layer its own offset Attachment/axis); forgetting Transparency=1 leaves a grey box occluding the effect; forgetting Anchored=true + CanCollide=false makes the anchor fall/collide when later spawned on a character.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-layered-multi-emitter-composition"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Parenting a ParticleEmitter directly to a large Part scatters particles randomly inside its bounding box; parent emitters to the Attachment for a point origin. Forgetting Transparency=1 leaves a grey box occluding the effect; forgetting Anchored+CanCollide=false makes the anchor fall/collide when later spawned on a character."},{"id":"vfx-role-layers-core-glow-sparks-smoke","order_index":2,"instruction":"Author the effect as 3-5 SEPARATE ParticleEmitters parented to the Attachment, each owning exactly ONE role, and prove the separation with role-DISCRIMINATING per-layer property values (not just three copies of one emitter): (1) CORE — small Size, high LightEmission (~1, additive) with LightInfluence<1, short Lifetime, moderate Rate; (2) GLOW/HAZE — much larger Size, high Transparency, LightEmission~1, soft round texture, LOW Rate (it is a big transparent layer — keep Rate low to avoid overdraw); (3) SPARKS/DEBRIS — tiny Size, high Speed, wide SpreadAngle, short Lifetime; (4) SMOKE — large Size, slow Speed, long Lifetime, dark Color, LOW-but-nonzero LightEmission/LightInfluence so it still reads as grounded and never as bright as the core. create_instance each ParticleEmitter, then set_property the role-specific values above so the layers differ in brightness, size, speed AND lifetime — a build that names the roles but leaves their Rate/Lifetime/Size/LightEmission identical earns only partial. PITFALLS: making the glow/smoke layers as bright as the core erases the hot-core-vs-soft-shell contrast that is the whole point — the core must be the brightest, tightest, shortest-lived layer and the surround dimmer/larger/softer. Stacking many large high-Rate transparent layers tanks GPU via overdraw, so the big glow/smoke layers MUST stay low-Rate. A single emitter cannot encode multiple roles and FAILS the layered-composition criterion by construction.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-layered-multi-emitter-composition"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Making the glow/smoke layers as bright as the core erases the hot-core-vs-soft-shell contrast that is the whole point. Stacking many large high-Rate layers tanks GPU via overdraw — keep big transparent layers low-Rate."},{"id":"vfx-authored-texture-grayscale-soft","order_index":3,"instruction":"Give every layer a real authored sprite, never the default white square. Use paint_texture to author a procedural sprite (or set the emitter's Texture to a crafted grayscale, soft-edged, transparent-background asset id). Author the sprite in GRAYSCALE so all hue is driven by the Color property at runtime — one texture serves many tinted effects and avoids the muddy 'baked-in color' stock look.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-additive-emission-and-authored-texture"],"tools":["paint_texture","set_property","create_instance"],"pitfalls":"Baking color into the texture removes Color-property control and reads as a generic stock asset. Hard-edged or opaque-background sprites show visible rectangles and harsh seams — blur edges to transparent. (paint_texture tiles on PARTS; for the particle sprite itself use a grayscale soft blob image id.)"},{"id":"vfx-lifetime-curves-size-transparency-color","order_index":4,"instruction":"Drive each emitter's Size, Transparency and Color with eased sequences over particle life via a run_luau block (a single set_property can't express multi-keypoint curves cleanly). Transparency = NumberSequence that starts near 1, dips toward 0 mid-life, returns to 1 at death (hides spawn/despawn). Size = grow-then-shrink (impact/smoke) or large-to-small (sparks). Color = ColorSequence ramp encoding the phenomenon (e.g. bright yellow -> orange -> dark red). Add the 3rd 'envelope' arg on keypoints for per-particle variety. First/last keypoint times MUST be exactly 0 and 1.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-lifetime-curves-size-transparency-color"],"tools":["run_luau","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Flat single-keypoint (constant) values are the #1 amateur tell — particles pop in/out and look synthetic. Spend ~80% of tuning time on Size+Transparency curves. Keypoint times that aren't strictly increasing (or don't start at 0 / end at 1) error on construction."},{"id":"vfx-additive-selflit-energy","order_index":5,"instruction":"Make energy/fire/magic layers self-lit. set_property LightEmission toward 1 (additive blending so overlapping particles brighten and bloom), LightInfluence toward 0 (~0.1 so dark scene lighting doesn't mute your authored color), and raise Brightness. This 'fake glow' is what reads as energy; the core layer should carry the highest LightEmission, the smoke layer the lowest.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-additive-emission-and-authored-texture"],"tools":["set_property"],"pitfalls":"LightEmission=1 makes black/dark texture pixels vanish (additive ignores black) — design sprites on black so only bright parts show. Maxing LightEmission without Brightness (or leaving LightInfluence high) under-delivers the glow; they stack. Many overlapping additive particles blow out to pure white — keep Rate/overlap modest."},{"id":"vfx-performance-budget-bounded-cleanup","order_index":6,"instruction":"Bound the effect and make it clean up. set_property a finite Rate (<=400/s desktop, <=100/s mobile) and a bounded Lifetime NumberRange on every emitter; for one-shots set Enabled=false and trigger a bounded Emit(count) instead of a running stream. Then actively tear down: in a run_luau block disable the emitters and Debris:AddItem the whole rig with a delay >= the max Lifetime so no unbounded emission is left running and no instances leak.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-performance-budget-bounded-cleanup"],"tools":["set_property","run_luau","destroy_instance"],"pitfalls":"Destroying the rig immediately after Emit() cuts in-flight particles mid-life — disable first and Debris with delay >= max Lifetime. A constant-Rate emitter left Enabled is the classic unbounded leak. An infinite Lifetime makes particles never disappear."}]},{"tier":"good","goal":"Differentiated per-layer motion, spatial/directional structure, animated shapes, a directed/linear element, a core+glow depth stack, and event-bound bursts — clears the 'good' criteria.","steps":[{"id":"vfx-color-graded-ramp-randomized-lifetime","order_index":1,"instruction":"Refine the Color ramp to be physically motivated and de-synchronize particle death. set_property the Color ColorSequence to follow a temperature ramp (hot bright core -> warm mid -> cool/dark end). Make Lifetime a Min,Max NumberRange (e.g. 0.5,1) and set a Rotation range + RotSpeed so particles spin at varied rates — this avoids the synchronized mechanical death where every particle dies on the same frame.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-color-graded-ramp-and-randomized-lifetime"],"tools":["set_property","run_luau"],"pitfalls":"A single-value Lifetime makes the whole batch die in lockstep (gif-like restart). Identical Rotation on all particles looks mechanical — randomize via a range and the envelope band."},{"id":"vfx-per-layer-differentiated-motion","order_index":2,"instruction":"Give each role-separated layer its OWN motion profile so the composite reads as a real phenomenon. Per emitter set_property a distinct Speed (NumberRange), Acceleration (Vector3 — negative Y for gravity-pulled sparks/debris, positive Y for buoyant rising smoke), and Drag (high ~10 makes a fast burst flare out then decelerate hard like an impact puff; ~0 lets sparks fly). Sparks: high Speed + high Drag. Smoke: low Speed + upward Acceleration + long Lifetime. Core flash: Speed ~0.001 so it shimmers in place rather than flying off.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-motion-acceleration-drag-squash-orientation"],"tools":["set_property"],"pitfalls":"Speed exactly 0 makes velocity-oriented particles invisible — use 0.001 for a stationary core. Every layer sharing one Speed/Acceleration reads as fake. Short Lifetime with no Speed bump means particles barely travel."},{"id":"vfx-zoffset-depth-directional-emission","order_index":3,"instruction":"Author spatial structure: depth-sort the layers and make at least one layer emit with INTENT. set_property each emitter's ZOffset so layers occupy distinguishable render planes (core in front at a higher ZOffset, smoke behind at a lower/negative ZOffset like -5 to render behind props). Then make at least one layer DIRECTED — set a narrow SpreadAngle and an EmissionDirection (and verify the EmissionDirection NormalId after any reorientation) for a shaped cone, rather than every layer puffing 180,180 omnidirectionally.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-zoffset-depth-and-directional-emission-shape"],"tools":["set_property"],"pitfalls":"Without ZOffset ordering, co-located layers z-fight and flicker. EmissionDirection is relative to the parent part's face — rotating the part redirects the spray unexpectedly. Pushing ZOffset too high pulls particles so close they clip out when the camera nears (keep roughly -5..5)."},{"id":"vfx-flipbook-animated-shape","order_index":4,"instruction":"Give turbulent layers (fire licks, fireballs, rolling smoke, splashes) a shape that CHANGES over each particle's life so it isn't a frozen billboard. Set the layer's Texture to a sprite-sheet, then set_property FlipbookLayout to match the grid (Grid2x2/Grid4x4/Grid8x8), FlipbookFramerate, FlipbookMode (OneShot is cleanest for stylized effects), and FlipbookStartRandom=true so particles don't all play the same frame in lockstep. If no flipbook sheet is available, fake it with a strong evolving Size+Rotation curve so the form visibly morphs.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-flipbook-animated-particles"],"tools":["set_property","run_luau"],"pitfalls":"A FlipbookLayout that mismatches the sheet (4x4 mode on an 8x8 sheet) renders garbled frames. Without FlipbookStartRandom every particle animates in lockstep and looks mechanically repetitive. Flipbook textures cost more memory — reuse one sheet and drop resolution for mobile."},{"id":"vfx-directed-linear-element-beam-trail","order_index":5,"instruction":"Add at least one DIRECTED/LINEAR element authored as its OWN role, visually distinct from the diffuse body: a streak, radial spark spray, connecting tendril, or expanding shockwave edge. For a connecting/energy line use a Beam between the two Attachments (assign Attachment0/Attachment1, set Texture, TextureMode=Wrap + TextureSpeed to scroll, Width0/Width1 to taper, CurveSize0/CurveSize1 to bend, FaceCamera=true, LightEmission for glow, Segments up if faceted). For a motion streak on a moving part use a Trail (two Attachments — their distance IS the width — Lifetime, Texture, Color ColorSequence, Transparency fading the tail, LightEmission). create_instance the Beam/Trail and set_property its fields.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-beam-craft-taper-glow-scroll-curve"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"A Beam or Trail stops rendering entirely if either attachment is missing/removed. A curved Beam looks faceted if Segments is too low. A Trail only renders while its part MOVES, and a too-long Lifetime leaves a stale smear — shorten it for crisp slashes. Beams have no collision — a damaging laser needs a separate hitbox."},{"id":"vfx-core-glow-depth-stack","order_index":6,"instruction":"Build bright heat as a stacked PAIR so it reads as volume: an intense, tight, additive CORE layer sitting inside a softer, larger, dimmer GLOW/HALO surround. create_instance two emitters on the same Attachment; set_property the core to small Size + high LightEmission + high Brightness + short Lifetime, and the glow to larger Size + higher Transparency + lower LightEmission + longer Lifetime, with ZOffset placing the core in front of the glow. Each carries its OWN size/brightness/transparency.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-trail-body-core-stack-taper-shortlife"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Making the glow as bright/opaque as the core collapses the depth read — the contrast is the point. A soft, unfocused core kills impact; keep the inner core small, bright and crisp."},{"id":"vfx-attachment-bound-emit-on-event","order_index":7,"instruction":"Bind emitters to Attachments on the moving source with a deliberate LockedToPart choice and fire them as precise bursts on game events. set_property Enabled=false (idle until needed) and LockedToPart per layer (true so particles ride the source; false so a burst is left in world space). Then in a run_luau script trigger emitter:Emit(count) on the exact event moment (a Touched handler, a RemoteEvent, or an animation marker via GetMarkerReachedSignal) — never hardcoded to a fixed world position or left always-on.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-attachment-bound-emit-on-event"],"tools":["set_property","run_luau"],"pitfalls":"Leaving Enabled=true on a burst emitter makes it spew constantly instead of firing once. Emitting at a guessed point instead of the real event/attachment makes the effect float. Wrong LockedToPart makes a world-space burst drag along with the source (or vice versa)."}]},{"tier":"exceptional","goal":"Burst-then-decay timing with a synced light flash, choreographed impact phases, faked volume from a depth-ordered stack, full lighting/post + game-feel juice, and a triggered networked one-shot lifecycle — clears the exceptional criteria.","steps":[{"id":"vfx-motion-timing-burst-decay-anticipation","order_index":1,"instruction":"Shape the whole effect as a burst-then-decay with anticipation/impact/secondary beats, not a flat even fade. In a run_luau script: optionally play a tiny anticipation beat, then Emit the core/sparks with very short Lifetime (e.g. 0.2,0.6) + high Speed (20,40) + high Drag (~10) so the burst flares then decelerates, then let the Transparency curves decay the survivors. Sync a one-frame PointLight flash to the burst (tween Brightness from a spike to 0 over ~0.1s). Add a secondary beat (a smaller follow-up Emit) a few frames later.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-motion-timing-burst-decay-anticipation"],"tools":["run_luau","create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"A constant-Rate stream has no punch — use Emit() bursts. Snapping the light on/off instead of tweening the spike->0 reads as a blink, not a flash. No anticipation/secondary beat makes a heavy hit feel weak."},{"id":"vfx-impact-phase-choreography","order_index":2,"instruction":"Choreograph a heavy impact as discrete TIMED phases on their own delays rather than firing everything in one frame. In a run_luau script use task.delay offsets: t=0 flash (core Emit + PointLight spike); t~0.02 expanding shockwave (a ring Beam or a fast wide-SpreadAngle Emit); t~0.04 radial debris/sparks Emit; t~0.1 enable rolling smoke for ~0.3s then disable; t~0.4 lingering embers/dust at low Rate. Each phase is its own role-separated layer triggered on its own beat.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-impact-phase-choreography"],"tools":["run_luau","create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Firing all layers on the same frame collapses the choreography into a single puff. Forgetting to disable the smoke phase leaves it streaming. Phase delays that exceed a layer's Lifetime mean the layer is gone before its beat — match Lifetimes to the schedule."},{"id":"vfx-faked-volume-depth-stack","order_index":3,"instruction":"Fake 3D volume on the 2D-billboard engine by building the effect from MULTIPLE co-located layers occupying naturally different depth planes that read foreground-to-background: a tight bright core/flash nearest (highest ZOffset), a softer glow/haze around it, then diffuse smoke/dust furthest (lowest/negative ZOffset). set_property each layer's ZOffset to order the stack and give each its own Size/Transparency so the parallax between planes sells depth as the camera moves.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-beam-stacking-parallax-volume"],"tools":["set_property","create_instance"],"pitfalls":"Layers at the same ZOffset z-fight and flicker instead of reading as depth. Equal Size/brightness across planes flattens the parallax — the front must be tighter/brighter, the back larger/dimmer. Overdraw from many big stacked planes still applies — keep the back planes low-Rate."},{"id":"vfx-lighting-post-and-game-feel-juice","order_index":4,"instruction":"Finish with the lighting/post + game-feel layer the textures can't provide alone. Add a color-matched PointLight as a SIBLING of the emitter (create_instance, tween its Brightness on the burst). Add Bloom on the bright emissive pixels and a ColorCorrectionEffect grade under Lighting (create_instance + set_property). On impact, run client-side juice: a time/spring-based camera shake (bind to RenderStepped or use a CameraShaker module, client only), a brief color-matched ColorCorrection screen flash, and an optional short hitstop freeze. Drive these with TweenService so they grow/peak/dissipate on one coordinated timeline.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-lighting-post-and-game-feel-juice"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property","run_luau"],"pitfalls":"A fixed per-frame CFrame camera offset scales with framerate — use a time/spring-based shake bound to RenderStepped. Camera shake and screen flash are per-client — do them on the client, not the server. A ColorCorrection flash with wrong saturation/contrast washes the scene; tune those with the tint. Snapping (not tweening) the light/post makes the layers start/stop on different frames and look disjointed."},{"id":"vfx-event-synced-raycast-placed-networked","order_index":5,"instruction":"Author the whole effect as a discrete, triggerable one-shot with a real lifecycle — fired, placed, replicated, cleaned up. In a run_luau script: stage the rig in ReplicatedStorage; on a precise event, raycast to the real hit position, clone the rig and set its CFrame to that placed position (or to the source Attachment), Emit() all burst layers (staggered per the phase schedule), replicate to all clients by running on the SERVER or firing a RemoteEvent fan-out, then actively tear down via Debris:AddItem with a delay >= max Lifetime — never a constant-Rate emitter left running.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-event-synced-raycast-placed-networked","vfx-performance-budget-bounded-cleanup"],"tools":["run_luau","create_instance","destroy_instance"],"pitfalls":"Placing the burst at a guessed point instead of the raycast hit makes it float in the wrong spot. Running impact VFX in a LocalScript means other players never see it — replicate via server/RemoteEvent. Cloning per shot at scale without pooling/culling is expensive — reuse the rig and cull off-screen instances."},{"id":"vfx-verify-and-evaluate-loop","order_index":6,"instruction":"Verify visually and grade before calling it done. Use capture (single framing) or capture_angles (orbit the effect from several sides at the burst peak) to SEE the composite — particle editors lie in the edit viewport, so confirm timing/density/depth as it actually renders. Then call evaluate(category='vfx', subject=<rig path>, design=<your summary of the layers/roles/lifecycle you built>) to grade against the vfx rubric, and refine the fails-first criteria (curves, additive, motion differentiation, cleanup) in small passes.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-layered-multi-emitter-composition","vfx-lifetime-curves-size-transparency-color","vfx-performance-budget-bounded-cleanup","vfx-motion-timing-burst-decay-anticipation"],"tools":["capture","capture_angles","evaluate"],"pitfalls":"Tuning only in the edit viewport produces effects that look wrong at runtime (lighting/camera/timing differ) — capture at the burst peak, since a one-shot is invisible at rest. Chasing exact numbers from a tutorial fails because values are texture- and scale-dependent — iterate by eye against the contrast/impact principle and the evaluate report."}]}],"provenance":{"sources":["https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/tutorials/curriculums/core/building/create-basic-visual-effects.md","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/effects/particle-emitters.md","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/vfx/use-particles-for-explosions.md","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/tutorials/curriculums/core/building/create-basic-visual-effects.md","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/introduction-to-vfx-particles/2068650","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/effects/beams.md","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/effects/trails.md","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/vfx/use-particles-for-explosions.md","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/vfx/create-waterfalls.md","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/effects/particle-emitters.md","https://kitsblox.com/blog/roblox-particle-system-deep-dive","https://create.roblox.com/docs/effects/particle-emitters","https://create.roblox.com/docs/reference/engine/classes/ParticleEmitter","https://create.roblox.com/docs/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/vfx/use-particles-for-explosions","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/tutorials/curriculums/core/building/create-basic-visual-effects.md","https://github.com/Sleitnick/RbxCameraShaker","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/vfx/custom-part